window.__examData={"exam":"jee","name":"JEE Main","total":464,"sections":[{"name":"Mathematics","count":151,"questions":[{"id":1122,"text":"If A = {1, 2, 3} and B = {2, 3, 4}, find A n B.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"{1}"},{"label":"B","text":"{2, 3}"},{"label":"C","text":"{2, 3, 4}"},{"label":"D","text":"{1, 2, 3, 4}"}],"answer":1,"solution":"A n B = elements common to both sets = {2, 3}."},{"id":1123,"text":"Find the derivative of f(x) = sin(x²).","options":[{"label":"A","text":"cos(x²)"},{"label":"B","text":"2x cos(x)"},{"label":"C","text":"2x cos(x²)"},{"label":"D","text":"x² cos(x²)"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Using chain rule: d/dx[sin(x²)] = cos(x²) · d/dx(x²) = 2x cos(x²)."},{"id":1124,"text":"The value of ∫₀¹ x² dx is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1/2"},{"label":"C","text":"1/3"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":2,"solution":"∫₀¹ x² dx = [x³/3]0¹ = 1/3 - 0 = 1/3."},{"id":1125,"text":"If the probability of an event A is 0.4 and of B is 0.5, and A and Bare independent, then P(A n B) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.9"},{"label":"B","text":"0.1"},{"label":"C","text":"0.2"},{"label":"D","text":"0.02"}],"answer":2,"solution":"For independent events: P(A n B) = P(A)·P(B) = 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2."},{"id":1137,"text":"The sum of first 10 natural numbers is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"45"},{"label":"B","text":"55"},{"label":"C","text":"50"},{"label":"D","text":"60"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Sum = n(n+1)/2 = 10×11/2 = 55."},{"id":1139,"text":"The arithmetic mean of 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"5"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"7"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Mean = (2+4+6+8+10)/5 = 30/5 = 6."},{"id":1141,"text":"The value of log2 32 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"5"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"3"}],"answer":1,"solution":"log2 32 = log2 25 = 5."},{"id":1162,"text":"If f(x) = 2x + 1, find f(3):","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"7"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}],"answer":2,"solution":"f(3) = 2(3) + 1 = 7."},{"id":1163,"text":"The slope of line passing through (1,2) and (3,6) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"m = (6-2)/(3-1) = 4/2 = 2."},{"id":1164,"text":"The value of log10 1000 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":2,"solution":"log10 1000 = log10 10³ = 3."},{"id":1167,"text":"The quadratic equation whose roots are 1 and 2 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"x² - 3x - 2 = 0"},{"label":"B","text":"x² - 3x + 2 = 0"},{"label":"C","text":"x² + 3x + 2 = 0"},{"label":"D","text":"x² - x + 2 = 0"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Sum = 3, Product = 2 ⇒ x² - 3x + 2 = 0."},{"id":1168,"text":"The number of permutations of 3 objects taken 2 at a time is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"9"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"³P2 = 3!/(3-2)! = 6."},{"id":1171,"text":"The value of 5! is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60"},{"label":"B","text":"100"},{"label":"C","text":"120"},{"label":"D","text":"24"}],"answer":2,"solution":"5! = 5×4×3×2×1 = 120."},{"id":1192,"text":"The number of ways to arrange letters of word CAT is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"9"}],"answer":2,"solution":"3! = 6 ways (CAT, CTA, ACT, ATC, TCA, TAC)."},{"id":1193,"text":"The value of sin²30° + cos²30° is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1/2"},{"label":"C","text":"1"},{"label":"D","text":"3/4"}],"answer":2,"solution":"sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 (identity)."},{"id":1195,"text":"The solution of 2x + 3 = 7 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"2x = 4 ⇒ x = 2."},{"id":1196,"text":"Which of these is a perfect square?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20"},{"label":"B","text":"24"},{"label":"C","text":"36"},{"label":"D","text":"48"}],"answer":2,"solution":"36 = 6². Others are not perfect squares."},{"id":1197,"text":"The mode of 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":1,"solution":"3 appears 3 times (highest frequency)."},{"id":1198,"text":"The probability of getting a head when a fair coin is tossed is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1/4"},{"label":"C","text":"1/2"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":2,"solution":"P(Head) = 1/2 (favorable outcomes / total outcomes)."},{"id":1199,"text":"The value of i² (where i = v-1) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"-1"},{"label":"C","text":"i"},{"label":"D","text":"-i"}],"answer":1,"solution":"i² = -1 (fundamental property of imaginary numbers)."},{"id":1200,"text":"The range of the function f(x) = x² is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All real numbers"},{"label":"B","text":"[0, 8)"},{"label":"C","text":"(-8, 0]"},{"label":"D","text":"(0, 8)"}],"answer":1,"solution":"x² = 0 for all real x ⇒ range [0, 8)."},{"id":1201,"text":"The sum of first n odd natural numbers is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"n"},{"label":"B","text":"2n"},{"label":"C","text":"n²"},{"label":"D","text":"n(n+1)/2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"1 + 3 + 5 + ... + (2n-1) = n²."},{"id":1208,"text":"The value of ∫₀¹ x² dx is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1/2"},{"label":"C","text":"1/3"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":2,"solution":"∫₀¹ x² dx = [x³/3]₀¹ = 1/3 - 0 = 1/3."},{"id":1216,"text":"The mode of 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":1,"solution":"3 appears 3 times (highest frequency)."},{"id":1221,"text":"The slope of line passing through (1,2) and (3,6) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"m = (6-2)/(3-1) = 4/2 = 2."},{"id":1222,"text":"The value of i² (where i = √-1) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"-1"},{"label":"C","text":"i"},{"label":"D","text":"-i"}],"answer":1,"solution":"i² = -1 (fundamental property of imaginary numbers)."},{"id":1228,"text":"If f(x) = 2x + 1, find f(3):","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"7"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}],"answer":2,"solution":"f(3) = 2(3) + 1 = 7."},{"id":1230,"text":"The sum of first n odd natural numbers is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"n"},{"label":"B","text":"2n"},{"label":"C","text":"n²"},{"label":"D","text":"n(n+1)/2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"1 + 3 + 5 + ... + (2n-1) = n²."},{"id":1235,"text":"The sum of first 10 natural numbers is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"45"},{"label":"B","text":"55"},{"label":"C","text":"50"},{"label":"D","text":"60"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Sum = n(n+1)/2 = 10x11/2 = 55."},{"id":1238,"text":"Find the derivative of f(x) = sin(x^2).","options":[{"label":"A","text":"cos(x^2)"},{"label":"B","text":"2x cos(x)"},{"label":"C","text":"2x cos(x^2)"},{"label":"D","text":"x^2 cos(x^2)"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Using chain rule: d/dx[sin(x^2)] = cos(x^2) . d/dx(x^2) = 2x cos(x^2)."},{"id":1239,"text":"The probability of getting a head when a fair coin is tossed is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1/4"},{"label":"C","text":"1/2"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":2,"solution":"P(Head) = 1/2 (favorable outcomes / total outcomes)."},{"id":1245,"text":"The mode of 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":1,"solution":"3 appears 3 times (highest frequency)."},{"id":1249,"text":"The value of  x^2 dx is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1/2"},{"label":"C","text":"1/3"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":2,"solution":"x^2 dx = [x/3] = 1/3 - 0 = 1/3."},{"id":1252,"text":"The solution of 2x + 3 = 7 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"2x = 4 -> x = 2."},{"id":1259,"text":"The value of 5! is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60"},{"label":"B","text":"100"},{"label":"C","text":"120"},{"label":"D","text":"24"}],"answer":2,"solution":"5! = 5x4x3x2x1 = 120."},{"id":1261,"text":"Find the derivative of f(x) = sin(x^2).","options":[{"label":"A","text":"cos(x^2)"},{"label":"B","text":"2x cos(x)"},{"label":"C","text":"2x cos(x^2)"},{"label":"D","text":"x^2 cos(x^2)"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Using chain rule: d/dx[sin(x^2)] = cos(x^2) . d/dx(x^2) = 2x cos(x^2)."},{"id":1263,"text":"The number of permutations of 3 objects taken 2 at a time is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"9"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"P = 3!/(3-2)! = 6."},{"id":1264,"text":"The value of sin^230 degrees  + cos^230 degrees  is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1/2"},{"label":"C","text":"1"},{"label":"D","text":"3/4"}],"answer":2,"solution":"sin^2 + cos^2 = 1 (identity)."},{"id":1270,"text":"The solution of 2x + 3 = 7 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"2x = 4 -> x = 2."},{"id":1271,"text":"If the equation x^2 + 2x + 1 = 0 has two equal roots, what is the value of the discriminant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The discriminant of a quadratic equation ax^2 + bx + c = 0 is given by b^2 - 4ac. For the given equation x^2 + 2x + 1 = 0, a = 1, b = 2, and c = 1. The discriminant is 2^2 - 4*1*1 = 0."},{"id":1277,"text":"The slope of line passing through (1,2) and (3,6) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"m = (6-2)/(3-1) = 4/2 = 2."},{"id":1281,"text":"The value of 5! is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60"},{"label":"B","text":"100"},{"label":"C","text":"120"},{"label":"D","text":"24"}],"answer":2,"solution":"5! = 5x4x3x2x1 = 120."},{"id":1285,"text":"If the probability of an event A is 0.4 and of B is 0.5, and A and Bare independent, then P(A  B) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.9"},{"label":"B","text":"0.1"},{"label":"C","text":"0.2"},{"label":"D","text":"0.02"}],"answer":2,"solution":"For independent events: P(A  B) = P(A).P(B) = 0.4 x 0.5 = 0.2."},{"id":1291,"text":"Which of these is a perfect square?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20"},{"label":"B","text":"24"},{"label":"C","text":"36"},{"label":"D","text":"48"}],"answer":2,"solution":"36 = 6^2. Others are not perfect squares."},{"id":1296,"text":"If the function f(x) = sin(x) is given, what is the value of x for which f(x) = 1?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"pi/2"},{"label":"C","text":"pi"},{"label":"D","text":"3pi/2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The function f(x) = sin(x) has a value of 1 when x = pi/2. This is because the sine function has a maximum value of 1 at x = pi/2."},{"id":1304,"text":"The solution of 2x + 3 = 7 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"2x = 4 -> x = 2."},{"id":1307,"text":"Find the derivative of f(x) = sin(x^2).","options":[{"label":"A","text":"cos(x^2)"},{"label":"B","text":"2x cos(x)"},{"label":"C","text":"2x cos(x^2)"},{"label":"D","text":"x^2 cos(x^2)"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Using chain rule: d/dx[sin(x^2)] = cos(x^2) . d/dx(x^2) = 2x cos(x^2)."},{"id":1312,"text":"The number of permutations of 3 objects taken 2 at a time is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"9"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"P = 3!/(3-2)! = 6."},{"id":1316,"text":"The sum of first 10 natural numbers is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"45"},{"label":"B","text":"55"},{"label":"C","text":"50"},{"label":"D","text":"60"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Sum = n(n+1)/2 = 10x11/2 = 55."},{"id":1321,"text":"The value of i^2 (where i = sqrt-1) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"-1"},{"label":"C","text":"i"},{"label":"D","text":"-i"}],"answer":1,"solution":"i^2 = -1 (fundamental property of imaginary numbers)."},{"id":1326,"text":"The probability of getting a head when a fair coin is tossed is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1/4"},{"label":"C","text":"1/2"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":2,"solution":"P(Head) = 1/2 (favorable outcomes / total outcomes)."},{"id":1332,"text":"The value of log 32 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"5"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"3"}],"answer":1,"solution":"log 32 = log 2 = 5."},{"id":1337,"text":"If A = {1, 2, 3} and B = {2, 3, 4}, find A  B.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"{1}"},{"label":"B","text":"{2, 3}"},{"label":"C","text":"{2, 3, 4}"},{"label":"D","text":"{1, 2, 3, 4}"}],"answer":1,"solution":"A  B = elements common to both sets = {2, 3}."},{"id":1340,"text":"The range of the function f(x) = x^2 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All real numbers"},{"label":"B","text":"[0, )"},{"label":"C","text":"(-, 0]"},{"label":"D","text":"(0, )"}],"answer":1,"solution":"x^2  0 for all real x -> range [0, )."},{"id":1341,"text":"If f(x) = 2x + 1, find f(3):","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"7"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}],"answer":2,"solution":"f(3) = 2(3) + 1 = 7."},{"id":1342,"text":"If the function f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3 is given, what is the value of x for which f(x) is maximum or minimum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"3"}],"answer":2,"solution":"To find the maximum or minimum of the function f(x), we need to find the critical points by taking the derivative of f(x) and setting it equal to zero. The derivative of f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3 is f'(x) = 2x - 4. Setting f'(x) = 0, we get 2x - 4 = 0, which gives x = 2."},{"id":1352,"text":"The sum of first n odd natural numbers is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"n"},{"label":"B","text":"2n"},{"label":"C","text":"n^2"},{"label":"D","text":"n(n+1)/2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"1 + 3 + 5 + ... + (2n-1) = n^2."},{"id":1356,"text":"If the equation x^2 + 2x + 1 = 0 has two equal roots, what is the value of the discriminant?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The discriminant of a quadratic equation ax^2 + bx + c = 0 is given by b^2 - 4ac. For the given equation x^2 + 2x + 1 = 0, a = 1, b = 2, and c = 1. The discriminant is 2^2 - 4*1*1 = 0."},{"id":1358,"text":"The value of  x^2 dx is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1/2"},{"label":"C","text":"1/3"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":2,"solution":"x^2 dx = [x/3] = 1/3 - 0 = 1/3."},{"id":1362,"text":"The number of ways to arrange letters of word CAT is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"9"}],"answer":2,"solution":"3! = 6 ways (CAT, CTA, ACT, ATC, TCA, TAC)."},{"id":1363,"text":"If the function f(x) = x^3 - 2x^2 - 5x + 1 is given, what is the value of f(-1)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"5"},{"label":"D","text":"7"}],"answer":1,"solution":"To find the value of f(-1), we substitute x = -1 into the function: f(-1) = (-1)^3 - 2(-1)^2 - 5(-1) + 1 = -1 - 2 + 5 + 1 = 3."},{"id":1368,"text":"The value of sin^230 degrees  + cos^230 degrees  is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1/2"},{"label":"C","text":"1"},{"label":"D","text":"3/4"}],"answer":2,"solution":"sin^2 + cos^2 = 1 (identity)."},{"id":1377,"text":"The mode of 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":1,"solution":"3 appears 3 times (highest frequency)."},{"id":1471,"text":"If ω is an on-real cube root of unity, then the value of (1+ω)(1+ω²)(1+ω⁴)(1+ω⁸) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"-1"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Use ω³=1 to reduce powers: ω⁴=ω, ω⁸=ω². Expression becomes [(1+ω)(1+ω²)]². Since 1+ω+ω²=0, (1+ω)=-ω² and (1+ω²)=-ω. Product inside bracket = (-ω²)(-ω)=ω³=1. Square is 1. Step-by-step: Substitute ω⁴=ω, ω⁸=ω². Expand (1+ω)(1+ω²)=1+ω+ω²+ω³=0+1=1. Final value = 1² = 1."},{"id":1473,"text":"The value of the definite integral ∫₀^(π/2) (sin x)/(sin x + cos x) dx is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"π/2"},{"label":"B","text":"π/4"},{"label":"C","text":"π/8"},{"label":"D","text":"π"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Apply King's Property (∫₀ᵃ f(x)dx = ∫₀ᵃ f(a-x)dx). Let I be the integral. Replace x by π/2-x to get I = ∫₀^(π/2) (cos x)/(sin x+cos x) dx. Add both: 2I = ∫₀^(π/2) 1 dx = π/2 ⇒ I = π/4. Step-by-step: I = ∫ sin/(sin+cos). I = ∫ cos/(sin+cos). 2I = ∫ (sin+cos)/(sin+cos) = ∫ 1 dx = π/2. I = π/4."},{"id":1474,"text":"The vectors a = i + 2j + 3k, b = 2i + 3j + 4k, c = 3i + 4j + 5k are:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mutually perpendicular"},{"label":"B","text":"Coplanar"},{"label":"C","text":"Non-coplanar"},{"label":"D","text":"Collinear"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Check linear dependence via row differences. b-a = (1,1,1), c-b = (1,1,1). Since (b-a) and (c-b) are parallel, the three vectors lie in the same plane (coplanar). Step-by-step: Scalar triple product [a b c] = determinant of rows (1,2,3), (2,3,4), (3,4,5). R₂→R₂-R₁, R₃→R₃-R₂ gives rows (1,1,1) and (1,1,1). Two identical rows ⇒ Determinant = 0 ⇒ Vectors are coplanar."},{"id":1475,"text":"The variance of the first 10 natural numbers is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8.25"},{"label":"B","text":"9.25"},{"label":"C","text":"7.25"},{"label":"D","text":"10.25"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Use standard formula for variance of first n natural numbers: σ² = (n²-1)/12. For n=10: (100-1)/12 = 99/12 = 8.25. Step-by-step: Mean μ = (10+1)/2 = 5.5. Σ(xᵢ-μ)² = Σ(k-5.5)² for k=1 to 10 = 82.5. Variance = 82.5/10 = 8.25."},{"id":1519,"text":"Let ω be a complex cube root of unity (ω ≠ 1). The value of the product (1+ω)(1+ω²)(1+ω⁴)(1+ω⁸) ... up to 2n factors is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"-1"},{"label":"C","text":"ω"},{"label":"D","text":"ω²"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Use 1+ω+ω²=0 ⇒ 1+ω = -ω² and 1+ω² = -ω. The product of each consecutive pair (1+ω)(1+ω²) = (-ω²)(-ω) = ω³ = 1. Since ω³=1, the sequence of factors repeats every two terms: (1+ω), (1+ω²), (1+ω), (1+ω²)... For 2n factors, there are n such pairs. Hence the total product is 1ⁿ = 1. Step-by-step: Given ω³=1 and 1+ω+ω²=0. The factors are (1+ω), (1+ω²), (1+ω⁴), (1+ω⁸)... Since ω⁴=ω and ω⁸=ω², the factors alternate between (1+ω) and (1+ω²). Compute one pair: (1+ω)(1+ω²) = 1 + ω + ω² + ω³ = (1+ω+ω²) + 1 = 0 + 1 = 1. There are 2n factors, forming npa irs. Final product = 1ⁿ = 1."},{"id":1520,"text":"The term independent of x in the expansion of (x²ᐟ³ − x⁻¹ᐟ³)¹⁵ is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3003"},{"label":"B","text":"-3003"},{"label":"C","text":"5005"},{"label":"D","text":"-5005"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: General term Tᵣ₊₁ = ¹⁵Cᵣ (x²ᐟ³)¹⁵⁻ʳ (-x⁻¹ᐟ³)ʳ = ¹⁵Cᵣ (-1)ʳ x⁽³⁰⁻²ʳ⁻ʳ⁾ᐟ³ = ¹⁵Cᵣ (-1)ʳ x¹⁰⁻ʳ. For term independent of x, power 10−r = 0 ⇒ r = 10. Coefficient = ¹⁵C₁₀ (-1)¹⁰ = ¹⁵C₅ = 3003. Step-by-step: Tᵣ₊₁ = ¹⁵Cᵣ (x²ᐟ³)¹⁵⁻ʳ (-x⁻¹ᐟ³)ʳ = ¹⁵Cᵣ (-1)ʳ x⁽²ᐟ³⁾⁽¹⁵⁻ʳ⁾ x⁽⁻¹ᐟ³⁾ʳ = ¹⁵Cᵣ (-1)ʳ x⁽³⁰⁻²ʳ⁻ʳ⁾ᐟ³ = ¹⁵Cᵣ (-1)ʳ x¹⁰⁻ʳ. Set exponent 10−r = 0 ⇒ r = 10. The term is ¹⁵C₁₀ (-1)¹⁰ = ¹⁵C₅ = (15×14×13×12×11)/(5×4×3×2×1) = 3003."},{"id":1521,"text":"If the circles x² + y² + 2x + 2ky + 6 = 0 and x² + y² + 2kx + 2y + 6 = 0 cut orthogonally, then the value of k is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Orthogonality condition for circles x²+y²+2g₁x+2f₁y+c₁=0 and x²+y²+2g₂x+2f₂y+c₂=0 is 2g₁g₂ + 2f₁f₂ = c₁ + c₂. Here g₁=1, f₁=k, c₁=6; g₂=k, f₂=1, c₂=6. Substitute: 2(1)(k) + 2(k)(1) = 6+6 ⇒ 4k = 12 ⇒ k = 3. Step-by-step: Circle 1: centre (-1, -k), radius² = 1+k²-6 = k²-5. Circle 2: centre (-k, -1), radius² = k²+1-6 = k²-5. Distance between centres² = (k-1)² + (1-k)² = 2(k-1)². For orthogonal intersection, d² = r₁² + r₂² ⇒ 2(k-1)² = 2(k²-5) ⇒ (k-1)² = k²-5 ⇒ k²-2k+1 = k²-5 ⇒ -2k = -6 ⇒ k = 3."},{"id":1522,"text":"Evaluate the limit: \\(\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\tan 3x - \\sin 3x}{x^3}\\).","options":[{"label":"A","text":"9/2"},{"label":"B","text":"9"},{"label":"C","text":"27/2"},{"label":"D","text":"27"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Use the standard limit \\(\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\tan x - \\sin x}{x^3} = \\frac{1}{2}\\). Substitute \\(x \\to 3x\\) to get \\(\\frac{\\tan 3x - \\sin 3x}{(3x)^3} = \\frac{1}{2}\\), hence the required limit is \\(\\frac{27}{2}\\). Full solution: Expand \\(\\tan 3x = 3x + 9x^3 + \\cdots\\) and \\(\\sin 3x = 3x - \\frac{9}{2}x^3 + \\cdots\\) using Maclaurin series; their difference is \\(\\frac{27}{2}x^3 + \\cdots\\). Dividing by \\(x^3\\) and taking the limit gives \\(\\frac{27}{2}\\)."},{"id":1523,"text":"Let \\(A\\) be a \\(3 \\times 3\\) matrix with \\(\\det(A) = 2\\). Find \\(\\det(\\operatorname{adj}(\\operatorname{adj}(A)))\\).","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"16"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"32"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For any \\(n \\times n\\) matrix, \\(\\det(\\operatorname{adj} A) = (\\det A)^{n-1}\\). For \\(n=3\\), \\(\\det(\\operatorname{adj} A) = (\\det A)^2 = 4\\). Applying the property again, \\(\\det(\\operatorname{adj}(\\operatorname{adj} A)) = (\\det(\\operatorname{adj} A))^2 = 4^2 = 16\\). Full solution: Given \\(\\det A = 2\\). Since \\(\\operatorname{adj} A = (\\det A) A^{-1}\\), \\(\\det(\\operatorname{adj} A) = (\\det A)^{3-1} = 2^2 = 4\\). Then \\(\\operatorname{adj}(\\operatorname{adj} A) = \\det(\\operatorname{adj} A) (\\operatorname{adj} A)^{-1}\\), so its determinant is \\((\\det(\\operatorname{adj} A))^{3-1} = 4^2 = 16\\)."},{"id":1541,"text":"If ω is an on-real cube root of unity, then the value of (1 - ω + ω²)(1 + ω - ω²) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-4"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"0"},{"label":"D","text":"-1"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Use 1 + ω + ω² = 0 ⇒ 1 + ω² = -ω and 1 + ω = -ω². The expression becomes (-ω - ω)(-ω² - ω²) = (-2ω)(-2ω²) = 4ω³ = 4. Full: Substitute ω³ = 1 and 1 + ω + ω² = 0 to simplify each bracket to -2ω and -2ω² respectively; their product is 4ω³ = 4."},{"id":1542,"text":"The value of the definite integral ∫₀ⁿ₂ π/2 (sin³ x) / (sin³ x + cos³ x) dx is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"π/4"},{"label":"B","text":"π/2"},{"label":"C","text":"π/3"},{"label":"D","text":"π/6"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Apply King's Property I = ∫₀ⁿ₂ f(sin x, cos x) dx = ∫₀ⁿ₂ f(cos x, sin x) dx. Adding, 2I = ∫₀ⁿ₂ 1 dx = π/2 ⇒ I = π/4. Full: Let I be the integral. Replace x by π/2 - x to get I = ∫ cos³ x / (cos³ x + sin³ x) dx. Add the two forms of I; the integrand becomes 1, yielding 2I = π/2."},{"id":1543,"text":"A bag contains 4 red and 6 black balls. Two balls are drawn at random without replacement. Given that the second ball drawn is red, the probability that the first ball was also red is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3/10"},{"label":"B","text":"3/5"},{"label":"C","text":"1/3"},{"label":"D","text":"2/5"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: P(R₁|R₂) = P(R₁∩R₂) / P(R₂). By symmetry, P(R₂) = 4/10 = 2/5. P(R₁∩R₂) = (4/10)(3/9) = 2/15. Required probability = (2/15) / (2/5) = 1/3. Full: Use Bayes' theorem or conditional probability definition. Compute joint probability of both red and marginal probability of second red (using law of total probability), then divide."},{"id":1544,"text":"The length of the focal chord of the parabola y² = 8x inclined at an angle of 30° with the positive x-axis is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"8"},{"label":"B","text":"16"},{"label":"C","text":"32"},{"label":"D","text":"64"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Length of focal chord = 4a cosec²θ. For y² = 8x, 4a = 8 ⇒ a = 2. θ = 30°, cosec 30° = 2. Length = 8 × 2² = 32. Full: Focus is (2, 0). Equation of chord: y = tan 30° (x - 2). Substitute in y² = 8x, solve quadratic for x, find difference of roots, and compute distance using slope."},{"id":1545,"text":"\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{e^x - 1 - x - \\frac{x^2}{2}}{x^3}","options":[{"label":"A","text":"\\frac{1}{6}"},{"label":"B","text":"\\frac{1}{3}"},{"label":"C","text":"\\frac{1}{2}"},{"label":"D","text":"0"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Use the Maclaurin series expansion e^x = 1 + x + x^2/2! + x^3/3! + ...; the numerator simplifies to x^3/6 + higher order terms, so the limit is 1/6. Full Solution: Apply L'Hospital's Rule three times: lim (e^x - 1 - x)/3x^2 = lim (e^x - 1)/6x = lim e^x/6 = 1/6."},{"id":1556,"text":"The value of the definite integral ∫₀^π (x sin x) / (1 + cos² x) dx is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"π²/2"},{"label":"B","text":"π²/4"},{"label":"C","text":"π/2"},{"label":"D","text":"π/4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Apply King's Property I = ∫₀^π f(x)dx = ∫₀^π f(π-x)dx. Adding, 2I = π ∫₀^π sin x/(1+cos² x) dx. Substitute t = cos x ⇒ dt = -sin x dx. Integral = π [tan⁻¹(cos x)]_π⁰ = π(π/4 + π/4) = π²/2 ⇒ I = π²/4. Step-by-step: Let I be the integral. Replace x by π-x, add the two equations to remove x from numerator. Evaluate resulting integral using substitution cos x = t."},{"id":1557,"text":"Two fair dice are rolled simultaneously. The probability that the sum of the numbers appearing is a prime number is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5/12"},{"label":"B","text":"1/3"},{"label":"C","text":"7/18"},{"label":"D","text":"5/18"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Total outcomes = 36. Prime sums possible: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11. Favourable pairs: Sum 2 (1), Sum 3 (2), Sum 5 (4), Sum 7 (6), Sum 11 (2). Total favourable = 15. Probability = 15/36 = 5/12. Step-by-step: List all 36 ordered pairs. Identify pairs giving prime sums (2,3,5,7,11). Count frequency: (1,1); (1,2),(2,1); (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1); (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1); (5,6),(6,5). Divide 15 by 36."},{"id":1558,"text":"The area of the triangle formed by the lines x = 0, y = 0 and 3x + 4y = 12 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"12"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"24"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: The line 3x+4y=12 cuts axes at (4,0) and (0,3). With coordinate axes, it forms a right triangle. Area = ½ × x-intercept × y-intercept = ½ × 4 × 3 = 6 sq units. Step-by-step: Find x-intercept by setting y=0 ⇒ 3x=12 ⇒ x=4. Find y-intercept by setting x=0 ⇒ 4y=12 ⇒ y=3. Vertices are (0,0), (4,0), (0,3). Use right triangle area formula ½ × base × height."},{"id":1559,"text":"If z = (1 + i)^6, then the value of |z| is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"8"},{"label":"C","text":"16"},{"label":"D","text":"2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: |1+i| = √2, so |(1+i)^6| = (√2)^6 = 2^3 = 8. Full: (1+i)^2 = 2i, then (1+i)^6 = (2i)^3 = 8i^3 = -8i, whose modulus is 8."},{"id":1560,"text":"The coefficient of x^2 in the expansion of (1 + x)^7 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7"},{"label":"B","text":"14"},{"label":"C","text":"35"},{"label":"D","text":"21"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: The coefficient of x^r in (1+x)^n is nCr; here n=7, r=2 gives 7C2 = 21. Full: Expand using binomial theorem: (1+x)^7 = Σ_{k=0}^7 7Ck x^k; the term with x^2 is 7C2 x^2 = 21 x^2."},{"id":1571,"text":"If \\(\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\sin 5x}{\\tan 3x}=k\\), then \\(k\\) equals:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\frac{5}{3}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\frac{3}{5}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"5"},{"label":"D","text":"3"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Near zero, \\(\\sin θ≈θ\\) and \\(\\tan θ≈θ\\). So \\(\\frac{\\sin 5x}{\\tan 3x}\\approx\\frac{5x}{3x}=\\frac{5}{3}\\). Full solution: Using the standard limits \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin ax}{ax}=1\\) and \\(\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\tan bx}{bx}=1\\), rewrite the expression as \\(\\frac{5x}{3x}\\cdot\\frac{\\sin 5x}{5x}\\cdot\\frac{3x}{\\tan 3x}\\). Both fractions tend to 1, leaving \\(\\frac{5}{3}\\)."},{"id":1572,"text":"Evaluate the determinant \\[\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & k \\\\ 3 & k & 9 \\end{vmatrix}=0\\] Find the value of \\(k\\).","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6"},{"label":"B","text":"9"},{"label":"C","text":"12"},{"label":"D","text":"15"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Notice rows are proportional when k=6 because second row =2×first row except last entry: 2×3=6, so set k=6 makes determinant zero instantly. Full solution: Expand determinant or use row operations: R2→R2-2R1 gives (0,0,k-6). R3→R3-3R1 gives (0,k-6,0). Determinant = (k-6)·(k-6)·1 = (k-6)^2. Setting to zero gives k=6."},{"id":1573,"text":"Evaluate the definite integral \\( \\int_0^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\sin^3 x}{\\sin^3 x + \\cos^3 x} \\, dx \\).","options":[{"label":"A","text":"\\frac{\\pi}{2}"},{"label":"B","text":"\\frac{\\pi}{4}"},{"label":"C","text":"\\frac{\\pi}{6}"},{"label":"D","text":"\\frac{\\pi}{3}"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Apply King's Property \\( I = \\int_a^b f(x) dx = \\int_a^b f(a+b-x) dx \\). Replacing \\( x \\) by \\( \\pi/2 - x \\) converts the integrand to \\( \\cos^3 x/(\\cos^3 x + \\sin^3 x) \\). Adding the original and transformed integrals gives \\( 2I = \\int_0^{\\pi/2} 1 \\, dx = \\pi/2 \\), so \\( I = \\pi/4 \\). Full: Let \\( I = \\int_0^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\sin^3 x}{\\sin^3 x + \\cos^3 x} dx \\). Using \\( x \\to \\pi/2 - x \\), \\( I = \\int_0^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\cos^3 x}{\\cos^3 x + \\sin^3 x} dx \\). Adding: \\( 2I = \\int_0^{\\pi/2} \\frac{\\sin^3 x + \\cos^3 x}{\\sin^3 x + \\cos^3 x} dx = \\int_0^{\\pi/2} dx = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\). Thus \\( I = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\)."},{"id":1574,"text":"Tangents are drawn from a variable point P on the line \\( x + y = 4 \\) to the parabola \\( y^2 = 8x \\). The chord of contact of these tangents always passes through a fixed point. The coordinates of this fixed point are:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(-4, -4)"},{"label":"B","text":"(4, 4)"},{"label":"C","text":"(-4, 4)"},{"label":"D","text":"(4, -4)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Chord of contact from \\( (h,k) \\) to \\( y^2=4ax \\) is \\( ky = 2a(x+h) \\). Here \\( a=2 \\), so \\( ky = 4(x+h) \\). Since \\( P(h,k) \\) lies on \\( x+y=4 \\), \\( h = 4-k \\). Substitute: \\( ky = 4(x+4-k) \\Rightarrow 4x - ky + 16 - 4k = 0 \\Rightarrow 4(x+4) = k(y+4) \\). This family of lines passes through the intersection of \\( x+4=0 \\) and \\( y+4=0 \\), i.e., \\( (-4, -4) \\). Full: For \\( y^2=8x \\), \\( a=2 \\). Chord of contact from \\( (x_1, y_1) \\) is \\( yy_1 = 4(x+x_1) \\). Let \\( P \\equiv (h, k) \\) on \\( x+y=4 \\Rightarrow h=4-k \\). Chord: \\( ky = 4(x+4-k) \\Rightarrow 4x - ky + 16 - 4k = 0 \\Rightarrow 4(x+4) - k(y+4) = 0 \\). This represents a line passing through the fixed point \\( (-4, -4) \\) for all \\( k \\)."},{"id":1575,"text":"A fair coin is tossed 5 times. Given that at least 3 heads have appeared, the probability that exactly 4 heads appear is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"\\frac{5}{32}"},{"label":"B","text":"\\frac{1}{4}"},{"label":"C","text":"\\frac{5}{16}"},{"label":"D","text":"\\frac{1}{2}"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Use conditional probability \\( P(A|B) = P(A)/P(B) \\) since \\( A \\subset B \\). \\( P(\\text{exactly 4 heads}) = {}^5C_4/2^5 = 5/32 \\). \\( P(\\text{at least 3 heads}) = (^{5}C_3 + ^{5}C_4 + ^{5}C_5)/2^5 = 16/32 = 1/2 \\). Required probability \\( = (5/32) \\div (1/2) = 5/16 \\). Full: Total outcomes \\( = 2^5 = 32 \\). Event A (exactly 4 heads): \\( n(A) = {}^5C_4 = 5 \\). Event B (at least 3 heads): \\( n(B) = {}^5C_3 + {}^5C_4 + {}^5C_5 = 10 + 5 + 1 = 16 \\). Since \\( A \\subset B \\), \\( P(A|B) = \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)} = \\frac{P(A)}{P(B)} = \\frac{5/32}{16/32} = \\frac{5}{16} \\)."},{"id":1581,"text":"The sum of the infinite series \\( 1 + \\frac{4}{5} + \\frac{7}{5^2} + \\frac{10}{5^3} + \\dots \\) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"35/16"},{"label":"B","text":"33/16"},{"label":"C","text":"37/16"},{"label":"D","text":"39/16"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Recognize as an Arithmetico-Geometric Progression (AGP) with first term \\(a=1\\), common difference \\(d=3\\), and common ratio \\(r=1/5\\). Use the direct formula \\(S_\\infty = \\frac{a}{1-r} + \\frac{dr}{(1-r)^2} = \\frac{1}{4/5} + \\frac{3(1/5)}{(4/5)^2} = \\frac{5}{4} + \\frac{15}{16} = \\frac{35}{16}\\). Full Solution: Let \\(S = 1 + \\frac{4}{5} + \\frac{7}{25} + \\frac{10}{125} + \\dots\\). Multiply by \\(r=1/5\\): \\(\\frac{S}{5} = \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{4}{25} + \\frac{7}{125} + \\dots\\). Subtract: \\(S - \\frac{S}{5} = 1 + \\frac{3}{5} + \\frac{3}{25} + \\frac{3}{125} + \\dots = 1 + \\frac{3/5}{1-1/5} = 1 + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{7}{4}\\). Thus \\(\\frac{4S}{5} = \\frac{7}{4} \\Rightarrow S = \\frac{35}{16}\\)."},{"id":1582,"text":"What is the unit digit of 7^{2023}?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"7"},{"label":"B","text":"9"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: The unit digits of powers of 7 repeat every 4: 7, 9, 3, 1. Since 2023 mod 4 = 3, the unit digit is the third in the cycle, which is 3. Full solution: 7^1=7, 7^2=49→9, 7^3=343→3, 7^4=2401→1. The cycle length is 4. 2023 = 4×505 + 3, so 7^{2023} has the same unit digit as 7^3 = 3."},{"id":1583,"text":"Find the remainder when 2^{100} is divided by 7.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: By Fermat's little theorem, 2^6 ≡ 1 (mod 7). 100 = 6×16 + 4, so 2^{100} ≡ (2^6)^{16}·2^4 ≡ 1^{16}·16 ≡ 2 (mod 7). Full solution: Compute powers of 2 modulo 7: 2^1=2, 2^2=4, 2^3=8≡1, 2^4=2, 2^5=4, 2^6=1. The cycle length is 3. 100 mod 3 = 1, so 2^{100} ≡ 2^1 = 2 mod 7. Both methods give remainder 2."},{"id":1584,"text":"Using the first two terms of the binomial expansion, the approximate value of (1.01)^5 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.05"},{"label":"B","text":"1.051"},{"label":"C","text":"1.0505"},{"label":"D","text":"1.052"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: For small x, (1+x)^n ≈ 1 + nx. Here x=0.01, n=5, so approximation = 1 + 5×0.01 = 1.05. Full solution: Binomial expansion (1+x)^5 = 1 + 5x + 10x^2 + 10x^3 + 5x^4 + x^5. With x=0.01, the first two terms give 1 + 0.05 = 1.05. The next term 10x^2 = 10×0.0001 = 0.001, so actual value ≈ 1.051. The question asks for the approximation using only the first two terms, which is 1.05."},{"id":1585,"text":"The number of distinct real roots of the cubic equation x^3 - 3x + 1 = 0 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"3"},{"label":"D","text":"0"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Use calculus. f(x)=x^3-3x+1, f'(x)=3x^2-3=3(x-1)(x+1). Critical points at x=±1. f(-1)=3>0, f(1)=-1<0. Since the cubic goes from -∞ to +∞ and has a local maximum positive and local minimum negative, it crosses the x-axis three times. Hence three distinct real roots. Full solution: The discriminant Δ = -4(-3)^3 - 27(1)^2 = 108 - 27 = 81 > 0, confirming three distinct real roots."},{"id":1643,"text":"Let ω be a complex cube root of unity (ω ≠ 1). The value of (1 - ω + ω^2)(1 + ω - ω^2) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Use 1+ω+ω^2=0 ⇒ 1+ω^2 = -ω and 1+ω = -ω^2. Then first factor = -2ω, second = -2ω^2. Product = 4ω^3 = 4. Full: Since ω^3=1 and 1+ω+ω^2=0, we have 1-ω+ω^2 = -2ω and 1+ω-ω^2 = -2ω^2. Multiplying gives 4ω^3 = 4."},{"id":1644,"text":"If A is a 3×3 matrix with determinant 2, then the determinant of adj(A) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"16"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For any n×n matrix, |adj A| = |A|^{n-1}. Here n=3, so |adj A| = 2^{2} = 4. Full: The adjoint satisfies A·adj(A) = |A|I. Taking determinants gives |A|·|adj A| = |A|^3 ⇒ |adj A| = |A|^2 = 4."},{"id":1645,"text":"The value of the definite integral ∫_{-π/2}^{π/2} sin^3 x cos^2 x dx is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"π/4"},{"label":"C","text":"1/2"},{"label":"D","text":"2/3"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: The integrand f(x)=sin^3 x cos^2 x is an odd function because sin^3 x is odd and cos^2 x is even. Integral of an odd function over symmetric interval [-a,a] is zero. Full: Since f(-x) = -f(x), ∫_{-a}^{a} f(x) dx = 0. Here a=π/2, so the integral equals 0."},{"id":1646,"text":"The line y = 2x + c is a tangent to the ellipse x^2/9 + y^2/4 = 1. The value of c^2 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"36"},{"label":"B","text":"20"},{"label":"C","text":"40"},{"label":"D","text":"16"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: For ellipse x^2/a^2 + y^2/b^2 = 1, the line y = mx + c is tangent iff c^2 = a^2 m^2 + b^2. Here a^2=9, b^2=4, m=2 ⇒ c^2 = 9·4 + 4 = 40. Full: Substitute y=2x+c into ellipse equation, set discriminant of resulting quadratic in x to zero. This yields c^2 = a^2 m^2 + b^2 = 40."},{"id":1647,"text":"Let S_n denote the sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic progression. If S_n = 3n^2 + 5n, then the common difference of the AP is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"9"},{"label":"D","text":"12"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For an AP, S_n = (d/2)n^2 + (a - d/2)n; comparing with 3n^2+5n gives d/2=3 ⇒ d=6. Full: a_n = S_n - S_{n-1} = 6n+2, so common difference d = a_n - a_{n-1} = 6."},{"id":1476,"text":"Which of the following is a primitive 12th root of unity?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"cos(π/6) + i sin(π/6)"},{"label":"B","text":"cos(π/3) + i sin(π/3)"},{"label":"C","text":"cos(π/2) + i sin(π/2)"},{"label":"D","text":"cos(2π/3) + i sin(2π/3)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: cos(π/6) + i sin(π/6)."},{"id":1477,"text":"In the binomial expansion of (cos θ + i sin θ)^n, which statement about the terms is correct?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All terms are real for any complex base."},{"label":"B","text":"Cancellation due to i² = –1 often occurs in complex expansions."},{"label":"C","text":"Symmetry of binomial coefficients is lost for complex coefficients."},{"label":"D","text":"Real‑coefficient expansions always have conjugate pairing."}],"answer":1,"solution":"Correct answer: Cancellation due to i² = –1 often occurs in complex expansions.."},{"id":1478,"text":"Express (√3 + i)^10 in the form a + bi.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"512 - 512√3 i"},{"label":"B","text":"512 + 512√3 i"},{"label":"C","text":"-512 + 512√3 i"},{"label":"D","text":"-512 - 512√3 i"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 512 - 512√3 i."},{"id":1479,"text":"Let z = 16(cos π/3 + i sin π/3). The four 4th roots of z form a square on the Argand plane. What is the side length of this square?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2√2"},{"label":"B","text":"4"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"4√2"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 2√2."},{"id":1480,"text":"The coefficient of x^3 in the expansion of (1 + x)^{-1/2} for |x| < 1 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-5/16"},{"label":"B","text":"5/16"},{"label":"C","text":"-3/8"},{"label":"D","text":"3/8"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: -5/16."},{"id":1481,"text":"The factorization of x^6 - 1 into cyclotomic polynomials is (x-1)(x+1)(x^2+x+1)(x^2-x+1). Which of these factors is the 6th cyclotomic polynomial Φ_6(x)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"x-1"},{"label":"B","text":"x+1"},{"label":"C","text":"x^2+x+1"},{"label":"D","text":"x^2-x+1"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Correct answer: x^2+x+1."},{"id":1482,"text":"Using the recurrence T_{n+1}(x) = 2x T_n(x) - T_{n-1}(x) with T_0(x)=1, T_1(x)=x, the polynomial T_3(x) equals:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4x^3 - 3x"},{"label":"B","text":"4x^3 - 4x"},{"label":"C","text":"3x^3 - 3x"},{"label":"D","text":"4x^3 - 2x"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 4x^3 - 3x."},{"id":1483,"text":"Using the roots-of-unity filter, the sum Σ_{k=0}^{2} C(6, 3k) equals:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"22"},{"label":"B","text":"21"},{"label":"C","text":"20"},{"label":"D","text":"23"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 22."},{"id":1484,"text":"The principal value of iⁱ is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"e⁻π/2"},{"label":"B","text":"e⁻π"},{"label":"C","text":"eπ/2"},{"label":"D","text":"eπ"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: e⁻π/2."},{"id":1485,"text":"The value of (1/2 choose 3) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1/16"},{"label":"B","text":"-1/16"},{"label":"C","text":"1/8"},{"label":"D","text":"-1/8"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 1/16."},{"id":1486,"text":"The sum of binomial coefficients C(N, k) where k ≡ r (mod m) can be expressed as:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(1/m) Σⱼ₌₀ᵐ⁻¹ ωₘ⁻ʲʳ (1+ωₘʲ)ᴺ"},{"label":"B","text":"(1/N) Σⱼ₌₀ᴺ⁻¹ ωₙ⁻ʲʳ (1+ωₙʲ)ᵐ"},{"label":"C","text":"(1/m) Σⱼ₌₀ᵐ⁻¹ ωₘʲʳ (1+ωₘ⁻ʲ)ᴺ"},{"label":"D","text":"(1/N) Σⱼ₌₀ᴺ⁻¹ ωₙʲʳ (1+ωₙ⁻ʲ)ᵐ"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: (1/m) Σⱼ₌₀ᵐ⁻¹ ωₘ⁻ʲʳ (1+ωₘʲ)ᴺ."},{"id":1487,"text":"For a non-zero complex number z, the identity (zʷ)ᵛ = zʷᵛ holds:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Always, for any complex w and v."},{"label":"B","text":"Only if w and v are integers."},{"label":"C","text":"Only if w is an integer."},{"label":"D","text":"Only modulo the multi-valued nature, requiring consistent branch selection."}],"answer":3,"solution":"Correct answer: Only modulo the multi-valued nature, requiring consistent branch selection.."},{"id":1591,"text":"The binomial series (1+x)^{-1/2} converges at x = 1:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Absolutely"},{"label":"B","text":"Conditionally"},{"label":"C","text":"Diverges"},{"label":"D","text":"Only for |x|<1"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Correct answer: Conditionally."},{"id":1592,"text":"The image of the real axis under the Möbius transformation f(z) = (z-i)/(z+i) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Unit circle"},{"label":"B","text":"Imaginary axis"},{"label":"C","text":"Line Re(z)=0"},{"label":"D","text":"Circle |z-1|=1"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: Unit circle."},{"id":1593,"text":"The closed form of a_n satisfying a_n = 2 a_{n-1} - 2 a_{n-2}, a_0=1, a_1=1 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2^{n/2} cos(nπ/4)"},{"label":"B","text":"2^{n/2} sin(nπ/4)"},{"label":"C","text":"2^n cos(nπ/4)"},{"label":"D","text":"2^n sin(nπ/4)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 2^{n/2} cos(nπ/4)."},{"id":1594,"text":"The expression of cos^5 θ as a linear combination of cos θ, cos 3θ, cos 5θ is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(10 cos θ + 5 cos 3θ + cos 5θ)/16"},{"label":"B","text":"(5 cos θ + 10 cos 3θ + cos 5θ)/16"},{"label":"C","text":"(cos θ + 5 cos 3θ + 10 cos 5θ)/16"},{"label":"D","text":"(10 cos θ + cos 3θ + 5 cos 5θ)/16"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: (10 cos θ + 5 cos 3θ + cos 5θ)/16."},{"id":1595,"text":"For y=sin(3x^2+1), the derivative dy/dx at x=0 equals:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"-6"},{"label":"D","text":"3"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 0."},{"id":1596,"text":"The equation of the normal to the curve y=x^2-4x+5 at the point where x=3 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"x+2y=7"},{"label":"B","text":"2x+y=7"},{"label":"C","text":"x-2y=7"},{"label":"D","text":"2x-y=7"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: x+2y=7."},{"id":1597,"text":"For f(x)=x^2-4 on the interval [-2,2], the value of c guaranteed by Rolle's theorem is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-2"},{"label":"B","text":"0"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Correct answer: 0."},{"id":1598,"text":"The point of inflection of the curve y = x^3 - 3x^2 + 4 occurs at x = ?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"0"},{"label":"D","text":"-1"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 1."},{"id":1599,"text":"A 10 m ladder rests against a vertical wall. The foot of the ladder is pulled away from the wall at 1 m/s. How fast is the top descending when the foot is 6 m from the wall?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-0.75 m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"-0.5 m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"-1 m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"-0.6 m/s"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: -0.75 m/s."},{"id":1600,"text":"For the parametric curve x = t^2, y = t^3, the value of d^2y/dx^2 at t = 1 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3/4"},{"label":"B","text":"3/2"},{"label":"C","text":"1/2"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 3/4."},{"id":1601,"text":"The first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ln(1+sin x) are:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"x - x^2/2 + x^3/6"},{"label":"B","text":"x - x^2/2 + x^3/3"},{"label":"C","text":"x - x^2/2 + x^3/2"},{"label":"D","text":"x - x^2/2 + x^3/12"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: x - x^2/2 + x^3/6."},{"id":1602,"text":"The radius of curvature of the curve y = x^2 at the point (1,1) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5√5/2"},{"label":"B","text":"5√5/4"},{"label":"C","text":"5√5/3"},{"label":"D","text":"5√5/5"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 5√5/2."},{"id":1603,"text":"The maximum volume of a rectangular box inscribed in the ellipsoid x^2/9 + y^2/16 + z^2/25 = 1 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"160√3/3"},{"label":"B","text":"80√3/3"},{"label":"C","text":"160/3"},{"label":"D","text":"80/3"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 160√3/3."},{"id":1604,"text":"Which of the following functions is uniformly continuous on its domain?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"f(x) = 1/x on (0,1]"},{"label":"B","text":"f(x) = x^2 on R"},{"label":"C","text":"f(x) = sin(x^2) on R"},{"label":"D","text":"f(x) = sin x on R"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Correct answer: f(x) = sin x on R."},{"id":1605,"text":"For f(x) = sin(x^2) on R, which pair of sequences (x_n), (y_n) demonstrates that f is not uniformly continuous via the sequential criterion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"x_n = n, y_n = n + 1/n"},{"label":"B","text":"x_n = sqrt(2π n), y_n = sqrt(2π n + π/2)"},{"label":"C","text":"x_n = 1/n, y_n = 1/(n+1)"},{"label":"D","text":"x_n = n, y_n = n + 1"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Correct answer: x_n = sqrt(2π n), y_n = sqrt(2π n + π/2)."},{"id":1606,"text":"Using the Maclaurin polynomial of degree 3 for sin x, the error in approximating sin(0.1) is at most:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4.2×10^-6"},{"label":"B","text":"1.7×10^-4"},{"label":"C","text":"8.3×10^-5"},{"label":"D","text":"2.1×10^-7"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 4.2×10^-6."},{"id":1607,"text":"The value of the 4th derivative of x^2 e^x at x = 0 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"12"},{"label":"B","text":"24"},{"label":"C","text":"6"},{"label":"D","text":"0"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 12."},{"id":1608,"text":"Consider the series ∑_{n=1}^{∞} x^n / n^2 on the interval [−1, 1]. Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The series converges pointwise but not uniformly on [−1, 1]."},{"label":"B","text":"The series converges uniformly on [−1, 1] and its sum is continuous there."},{"label":"C","text":"Termwise differentiation of the series is valid on (−1, 1) because the original series converges uniformly."},{"label":"D","text":"The Weierstrass M‑test fails because ∑ 1/n^2 diverges."}],"answer":1,"solution":"Correct answer: The series converges uniformly on [−1, 1] and its sum is continuous there.."},{"id":1609,"text":"Let f(x) = √x on [0, 1]. Which of the following correctly describes the continuity properties of f?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"f is Lipschitz continuous on [0, 1] with Lipschitz constant 1."},{"label":"B","text":"f is uniformly continuous on [0, 1] but not Lipschitz continuous."},{"label":"C","text":"f is not uniformly continuous on [0, 1] because its derivative is unbounded near 0."},{"label":"D","text":"f is Hölder continuous of order α = 1/2 on [0, 1] and also Lipschitz continuous."}],"answer":1,"solution":"Correct answer: f is uniformly continuous on [0, 1] but not Lipschitz continuous.."},{"id":1610,"text":"Which LU‑type factorisation guarantees numerical stability without any row pivoting for a given matrix?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doolittle"},{"label":"B","text":"Crout"},{"label":"C","text":"Cholesky"},{"label":"D","text":"Block LU"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Correct answer: Cholesky."},{"id":1611,"text":"Matrix C = [[2, 0], [0, -5]] is subjected to any non‑singular congruence transformation C' = Sᵀ C S. According to Sylvester’s law of inertia, how many positive eigenvalues does C' have?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"3"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Correct answer: 1."},{"id":1612,"text":"Let A be a nilpotent matrix satisfying A³ = 0 but A² ≠ 0. In the power‑series definition e^A = Σ_{k=0}^∞ A^k / k!, up to which power of A must be retained to compute e^A exactly?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"up to A¹"},{"label":"B","text":"up to A²"},{"label":"C","text":"up to A³"},{"label":"D","text":"up to A⁴"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: up to A¹."},{"id":1613,"text":"For a square matrix A, the number of Jordan blocks corresponding to an eigenvalue λ equals:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Algebraic multiplicity of λ"},{"label":"B","text":"Geometric multiplicity of λ"},{"label":"C","text":"Index of λ"},{"label":"D","text":"Size of the largest Jordan block"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Correct answer: Geometric multiplicity of λ."},{"id":1614,"text":"For an eigenvalue λ, the nullities of (A−λI)^m are n₁=2, n₂=3, n₃=4, n₄=4. The algebraic multiplicity is 4. How many Jordan blocks of size exactly 2 are there?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"3"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 0."},{"id":1615,"text":"The principal square root of the matrix [[5,4],[4,5]] is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"[[2,1],[1,2]]"},{"label":"B","text":"[[3,0],[0,1]]"},{"label":"C","text":"[[1,2],[2,1]]"},{"label":"D","text":"[[2,2],[2,2]]"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: [[2,1],[1,2]]."},{"id":1616,"text":"A linear system with an n×n circulant matrix can be solved in O(n log n) operations using:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fast Fourier Transform"},{"label":"B","text":"LU decomposition"},{"label":"C","text":"Cholesky factorization"},{"label":"D","text":"Gaussian elimination"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: Fast Fourier Transform."},{"id":1617,"text":"What is the number of distinct arrangements of the letters in the word MISSISSIPPI?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"34650"},{"label":"B","text":"3465"},{"label":"C","text":"69300"},{"label":"D","text":"17325"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 34650."},{"id":1618,"text":"Let X ~ Binomial(100, 0.3). Using the Chernoff bound P(X ≥ (1+δ)μ) ≤ exp(−μδ²/(2+δ)) with δ = 0.5, the upper bound on P(X ≥ 45) is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.05"},{"label":"B","text":"0.10"},{"label":"C","text":"0.02"},{"label":"D","text":"0.08"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 0.05."},{"id":1619,"text":"Let N be a geometric random variable with success probability 0.2 (support {1,2,…}) independent of i.i.d. variables X_i with mean 5. The expected value of Σ_{i=1}^N X_i is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20"},{"label":"B","text":"25"},{"label":"C","text":"30"},{"label":"D","text":"15"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Correct answer: 25."},{"id":1620,"text":"The number of Dyck paths of semilength 4 (i.e., Catalan number C₄) equals:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"14"},{"label":"B","text":"12"},{"label":"C","text":"16"},{"label":"D","text":"10"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 14."},{"id":1621,"text":"Using Burnside’s lemma for the rotation group of a cube acting on its faces, the number of distinct colourings of the faces with 3 colours is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"57"},{"label":"B","text":"55"},{"label":"C","text":"60"},{"label":"D","text":"50"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 57."},{"id":1622,"text":"The solution set of the inequality (x^2 - 4x + 4)/(x - 2) > 0 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(2, ∞)"},{"label":"B","text":"(-∞, 2)"},{"label":"C","text":"R \\ {2}"},{"label":"D","text":"∅"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: (2, ∞)."},{"id":1623,"text":"For the series Σ_{n=1}^∞ a_n with a_n = n!/n^n, the limit L = lim_{n→∞} a_{n+1}/a_n equals:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1/e"},{"label":"B","text":"e"},{"label":"C","text":"1"},{"label":"D","text":"0"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 1/e."},{"id":1624,"text":"Using the identity ∫_0^1 x^{n-1}(1-x) dx = 1/(n(n+1)), the sum Σ_{n=1}^∞ 1/(n(n+1)) evaluates to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"1/2"},{"label":"D","text":"0"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 1."},{"id":1625,"text":"Let a_1, a_2, ..., a_n be positive real numbers such that Σ_{k=1}^n a_k = 1. The minimum value of Σ_{k=1}^n (k^2 / a_k) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"n^2 (n+1)^2 / 4"},{"label":"B","text":"n(n+1)/2"},{"label":"C","text":"n^2 (n+1)^2 / 2"},{"label":"D","text":"n(n+1)(2n+1)/6"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: n^2 (n+1)^2 / 4."},{"id":1626,"text":"The linear recurrence a_n = 3 a_{n-1} - 2 a_{n-2} for n ≥ 2, with initial values a_0 = 1, a_1 = 2, can be represented by the matrix equation [a_n; a_{n-1}] = M [a_{n-1}; a_{n-2}]. The companion matrix M is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"[[3, -2], [1, 0]]"},{"label":"B","text":"[[3, 2], [1, 0]]"},{"label":"C","text":"[[3, -2], [0, 1]]"},{"label":"D","text":"[[2, -3], [1, 0]]"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: [[3, -2], [1, 0]]."},{"id":1627,"text":"The fundamental solution (x, y) in positive integers to the Pell equation x^2 - 2 y^2 = 1 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(3, 2)"},{"label":"B","text":"(1, 1)"},{"label":"C","text":"(2, 1)"},{"label":"D","text":"(5, 3)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: (3, 2)."},{"id":1628,"text":"The reflection of the point P(1,2,3) in the plane 2x − y + 2z = 3 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(−1/3, 8/3, 5/3)"},{"label":"B","text":"(1/3, 7/3, 7/3)"},{"label":"C","text":"(5/3, 4/3, 1/3)"},{"label":"D","text":"(−1/3, 5/3, 8/3)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: (−1/3, 8/3, 5/3)."},{"id":1629,"text":"The volume of the tetrahedron with vertices A(0,0,0), B(1,2,3), C(2,1,1), D(1,1,2) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1/3"},{"label":"B","text":"1/6"},{"label":"C","text":"1/2"},{"label":"D","text":"2/3"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 1/3."},{"id":1630,"text":"For the helix r(t) = (3 cos t, 3 sin t, 4t), the curvature κ is given by κ = |r' × r''| / |r'|^3. Compute κ.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.12"},{"label":"B","text":"0.15"},{"label":"C","text":"0.10"},{"label":"D","text":"0.08"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: 0.12."},{"id":1631,"text":"Given basis vectors a = (1,0,0), b = (1,1,0), c = (1,1,1), the reciprocal vector a* = (b × c) / [a b c] equals:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(1, -1, 0)"},{"label":"B","text":"(1, 1, 0)"},{"label":"C","text":"(0, 1, -1)"},{"label":"D","text":"(-1, 1, 0)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: (1, -1, 0)."},{"id":1632,"text":"The vertices of a tetrahedron are A(0,0,0), B(1,0,0), C(0,1,0), D(0,0,1). The barycentric coordinates of its centroid G are:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(1/4, 1/4, 1/4, 1/4)"},{"label":"B","text":"(1/3, 1/3, 1/3, 0)"},{"label":"C","text":"(1/2, 1/2, 0, 0)"},{"label":"D","text":"(0, 0, 0, 1)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Correct answer: (1/4, 1/4, 1/4, 1/4)."}]},{"name":"Chemistry","count":121,"questions":[{"id":1118,"text":"How many moles of O2 are required for complete combustion of 1 mole of C4H10?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"5"},{"label":"C","text":"6.5"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}],"answer":2,"solution":"C4H10 + 6.5O2 → 4CO2 + 5H2O. So 6.5 moles of O2 are needed."},{"id":1119,"text":"Which of the following shows +R effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-OH"},{"label":"B","text":"-NO2"},{"label":"C","text":"-CN"},{"label":"D","text":"-COOH"}],"answer":0,"solution":"-OH, -NH2, -OR show +R (resonance) effect as they donate electrons. -NO2, -CN, -COOH show -R effect."},{"id":1120,"text":"The pH of 10⁻³ M HCl solution is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"7"},{"label":"D","text":"11"}],"answer":1,"solution":"[H⁺] = 10⁻³ M. pH = -log[H⁺] = -log(10⁻³) = 3."},{"id":1121,"text":"The coordination number in NaCl crystal structure is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"12"}],"answer":1,"solution":"NaCl has face-centered cubic (FCC) structure with each Na⁺ surrounded by 6 Cl⁻ and vice versa."},{"id":1132,"text":"Which of the following is an aromatic compound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclohexane"},{"label":"B","text":"Benzene"},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclohexene"},{"label":"D","text":"1,3-Butadiene"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Benzene follows Hückel's rule (4n+2 p electrons, n=1) and is planar with delocalized electrons."},{"id":1133,"text":"The oxidation state of Mn in KMnO4 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+5"},{"label":"B","text":"+6"},{"label":"C","text":"+7"},{"label":"D","text":"+4"}],"answer":2,"solution":"K(+1) + Mn + 4O(-2) = 0 ⇒ +1 + Mn -8 = 0 ⇒ Mn = +7."},{"id":1134,"text":"Which gas is known as \"Laughing Gas\"?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NO2"},{"label":"B","text":"N2O"},{"label":"C","text":"CO"},{"label":"D","text":"CO2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Nitrous oxide (N2O) is called laughing gas due to its euphoric effects when inhaled."},{"id":1135,"text":"Shape of water molecule is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Linear"},{"label":"B","text":"Trigonal planar"},{"label":"C","text":"Bent/V-shaped"},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrahedral"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Water has 2 bond pairs + 2 lone pairs → bent shape with ~104.5° bond angle."},{"id":1136,"text":"Which element has the highest ionization energy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li"},{"label":"B","text":"Na"},{"label":"C","text":"He"},{"label":"D","text":"Ne"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Helium has the highest ionization energy due to its full 1s² configuration and small atomic size."},{"id":1152,"text":"Which of the following is a Lewis acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NH3"},{"label":"B","text":"AlCl3"},{"label":"C","text":"H2O"},{"label":"D","text":"OH⁻"}],"answer":1,"solution":"AlCl3 is electron deficient (can accept electron pair), hence a Lewis acid."},{"id":1153,"text":"The number of moles in 18 g of H2O is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"18"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Molar mass of H2O = 18 g/mol. n = 18/18 = 1 mol."},{"id":1154,"text":"Which of these is NOT a greenhouse gas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CO2"},{"label":"B","text":"CH4"},{"label":"C","text":"O2"},{"label":"D","text":"H2Ova por"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Oxygen (O2) is not a greenhouse gas. CO2, CH4, and H2Ova por are GHGs."},{"id":1155,"text":"The hybridization of carbon in methane is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"sp"},{"label":"B","text":"sp²"},{"label":"C","text":"sp³"},{"label":"D","text":"dsp²"}],"answer":2,"solution":"CH4 has 4 bond pairs + 0 lone pairs → sp³ hybridization, tetrahedral shape."},{"id":1156,"text":"The most abundant element in Earth's crust is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron"},{"label":"B","text":"Oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"Silicon"},{"label":"D","text":"Aluminum"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Oxygen (~46.6%) is the most abundant element in Earth's crust."},{"id":1158,"text":"The color of phenolphthalein in basic medium is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colorless"},{"label":"B","text":"Pink"},{"label":"C","text":"Blue"},{"label":"D","text":"Yellow"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Phenolphthalein is colorless IN acid and pink IN base (pH > 8.3)."},{"id":1159,"text":"The number of isomeric pentanes (C5H12) possible is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Pentane has 3 isomers: n-pentane, isopentane, neopentane."},{"id":1160,"text":"Which metal does not react with dilute HCl?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zn"},{"label":"B","text":"Mg"},{"label":"C","text":"Cu"},{"label":"D","text":"Fe"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Copper is below hydrogen in reactivity series, so does not react with dilute HCl."},{"id":1161,"text":"The functional group in alcohol is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-CHO"},{"label":"B","text":"-COOH"},{"label":"C","text":"-OH"},{"label":"D","text":"-NH2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Alcohols contain the hydroxyl (-OH) functional group."},{"id":1182,"text":"Atomic number of oxygen is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"16"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Oxygen has atomic number 8 (configuration 1s²2s²2p4)."},{"id":1183,"text":"Which of the following is a strong acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CH3COOH"},{"label":"B","text":"HCl"},{"label":"C","text":"H2CO3"},{"label":"D","text":"H2S"}],"answer":1,"solution":"HCl completely dissociates in water → strong acid."},{"id":1184,"text":"The gas used in welding is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methane"},{"label":"B","text":"Ethane"},{"label":"C","text":"Acetylene"},{"label":"D","text":"Propane"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Acetylene (C2H2) produces high temperature flame used in oxy-acetylene welding."},{"id":1185,"text":"The molar mass of CaCO3 is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 g/mol"},{"label":"B","text":"84 g/mol"},{"label":"C","text":"100 g/mol"},{"label":"D","text":"150 g/mol"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Ca(40) + C(12) + 3O(48) = 100 g/mol."},{"id":1186,"text":"Which of the following elements is liquid at room temperature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"Mercury"},{"label":"C","text":"Gold"},{"label":"D","text":"Silver"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Mercury (Hg) and bromine (Br2) are the only elements liquid at room temp."},{"id":1187,"text":"The chemical name of common salt is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium nitrate"},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium chloride"},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium carbonate"},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium hydroxide"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Common salt is NaCl — Sodium chloride."},{"id":1188,"text":"The number of valence electrons in carbon is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Carbon (Z=6) has electronic configuration 2,4 → 4 valence electrons."},{"id":1189,"text":"Which process is used to separate crude oil into useful fractions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distillation"},{"label":"B","text":"Crystallization"},{"label":"C","text":"Fractional distillation"},{"label":"D","text":"Chromatography"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Fractional distillation separates petroleum into fractions based on boiling points."},{"id":1190,"text":"The pH of 0.001 M NaOH is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"10"},{"label":"D","text":"11"}],"answer":3,"solution":"[OH⁻] = 10⁻³, pOH = 3, pH = 14 - 3 = 11."},{"id":1191,"text":"Which of these is a noble gas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cl2"},{"label":"B","text":"N2"},{"label":"C","text":"Xenon"},{"label":"D","text":"O2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Xenon (Xe) is a noble gas. Cl2, N2, O2 are diatomic gases."},{"id":1202,"text":"Which of these is a noble gas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cl₂"},{"label":"B","text":"N₂"},{"label":"C","text":"Xenon"},{"label":"D","text":"O₂"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Xenon (Xe) is a noble gas. Cl₂, N₂, O₂ are diatomic gases."},{"id":1204,"text":"The coordination number in NaCl crystal structure is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"12"}],"answer":1,"solution":"NaCl has face-centered cubic (FCC) structure with each Na⁺ surrounded by 6 Cl⁻ and vice versa."},{"id":1205,"text":"The color of phenolphthalein in basic medium is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colorless"},{"label":"B","text":"Pink"},{"label":"C","text":"Blue"},{"label":"D","text":"Yellow"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Phenolphthalein is colorless in acid and pink in base (pH > 8.3)."},{"id":1207,"text":"Which gas is known as \"Laughing Gas\"?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NO₂"},{"label":"B","text":"N₂O"},{"label":"C","text":"CO"},{"label":"D","text":"CO₂"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Nitrous oxide (N₂O) is called laughing gas due to its euphoric effects when inhaled."},{"id":1212,"text":"The most abundant element in Earth's crust is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron"},{"label":"B","text":"Oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"Silicon"},{"label":"D","text":"Aluminum"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Oxygen (~46.6%) is the most abundant element in Earth's crust."},{"id":1220,"text":"The number of moles in 18 g of H₂O is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"18"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Molar mass of H₂O = 18 g/mol. n = 18/18 = 1 mol."},{"id":1225,"text":"The oxidation state of Mn in KMnO₄ is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+5"},{"label":"B","text":"+6"},{"label":"C","text":"+7"},{"label":"D","text":"+4"}],"answer":2,"solution":"K(+1) + Mn + 4O(-2) = 0 → +1 + Mn -8 = 0 → Mn = +7."},{"id":1227,"text":"Which of the following is an aromatic compound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclohexane"},{"label":"B","text":"Benzene"},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclohexene"},{"label":"D","text":"1,3-Butadiene"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Benzene follows Hückel's rule (4n+2 π electrons, n=1) and is planar with delocalized electrons."},{"id":1229,"text":"The functional group in alcohol is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-CHO"},{"label":"B","text":"-COOH"},{"label":"C","text":"-OH"},{"label":"D","text":"-NH₂"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Alcohols contain the hydroxyl (-OH) functional group."},{"id":1236,"text":"Which element has the highest ionization energy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li"},{"label":"B","text":"Na"},{"label":"C","text":"He"},{"label":"D","text":"Ne"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Helium has the highest ionization energy due to its full 1s^2 configuration and small atomic size."},{"id":1242,"text":"Atomic number of oxygen is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"16"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Oxygen has atomic number 8 (configuration 1s^22s^22p)."},{"id":1243,"text":"Which of the following is a strong acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CHCOOH"},{"label":"B","text":"HCl"},{"label":"C","text":"HCO"},{"label":"D","text":"HS"}],"answer":1,"solution":"HCl completely dissociates in water -> strong acid."},{"id":1244,"text":"The molar mass of CaCO is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 g/mol"},{"label":"B","text":"84 g/mol"},{"label":"C","text":"100 g/mol"},{"label":"D","text":"150 g/mol"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Ca(40) + C(12) + 3O(48) = 100 g/mol."},{"id":1246,"text":"The functional group in alcohol is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-CHO"},{"label":"B","text":"-COOH"},{"label":"C","text":"-OH"},{"label":"D","text":"-NH"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Alcohols contain the hydroxyl (-OH) functional group."},{"id":1251,"text":"How many moles of O are required for complete combustion of 1 mole of CH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"5"},{"label":"C","text":"6.5"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}],"answer":2,"solution":"CH + 6.5O -> 4CO + 5HO. So 6.5 moles of O are needed."},{"id":1253,"text":"The number of valence electrons in carbon is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Carbon (Z=6) has electronic configuration 2,4 -> 4 valence electrons."},{"id":1255,"text":"Which of the following elements is liquid at room temperature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"Mercury"},{"label":"C","text":"Gold"},{"label":"D","text":"Silver"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Mercury (Hg) and bromine (Br) are the only elements liquid at room temp."},{"id":1265,"text":"Which of the following is an aromatic compound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclohexane"},{"label":"B","text":"Benzene"},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclohexene"},{"label":"D","text":"1,3-Butadiene"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Benzene follows Hckel's rule (4n+2 pi electrons, n=1) and is planar with delocalized electrons."},{"id":1266,"text":"The color of phenolphthalein in basic medium is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colorless"},{"label":"B","text":"Pink"},{"label":"C","text":"Blue"},{"label":"D","text":"Yellow"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Phenolphthalein is colorless in acid and pink in base (pH > 8.3)."},{"id":1268,"text":"What is the pH of a 0.1 M solution of NaOH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"12"},{"label":"C","text":"13"},{"label":"D","text":"14"}],"answer":2,"solution":"NaOH is a strong base, so it completely dissociates in water to give OH- ions. The concentration of OH- ions is equal to the concentration of NaOH, which is 0.1 M. Using the equation pOH = -log[OH-], we get pOH = -log(0.1) = 1. Then, pH + pOH = 14, so pH = 14 - pOH = 14 - 1 = 13."},{"id":1269,"text":"The molar mass of CaCO is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"50 g/mol"},{"label":"B","text":"84 g/mol"},{"label":"C","text":"100 g/mol"},{"label":"D","text":"150 g/mol"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Ca(40) + C(12) + 3O(48) = 100 g/mol."},{"id":1274,"text":"Atomic number of oxygen is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"16"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Oxygen has atomic number 8 (configuration 1s^22s^22p)."},{"id":1282,"text":"What is the type of hybridization of the central atom in the molecule CO2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"sp"},{"label":"B","text":"sp2"},{"label":"C","text":"sp3"},{"label":"D","text":"dsp2"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The central atom in CO2 is carbon, which is bonded to two oxygen atoms. Since carbon has two equivalent bonds and no lone pairs, its hybridization is sp."},{"id":1284,"text":"Atomic number of oxygen is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"16"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Oxygen has atomic number 8 (configuration 1s^22s^22p)."},{"id":1286,"text":"Which bond has the highest bond energy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C-C"},{"label":"B","text":"C=C"},{"label":"C","text":"CC"},{"label":"D","text":"All equal"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Triple bond (CC) has highest bond energy - 839 kJ/mol vs 614 for double, 348 for single."},{"id":1287,"text":"What is the type of bond formed between two atoms of the same element, such as in the molecule O2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ionic bond"},{"label":"B","text":"Covalent bond"},{"label":"C","text":"Hydrogen bond"},{"label":"D","text":"Van der Waals bond"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The bond formed between two atoms of the same element, such as in the molecule O2, is a covalent bond. This is because the two oxygen atoms share electrons to form a bond."},{"id":1288,"text":"Which of the following elements is liquid at room temperature?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"Mercury"},{"label":"C","text":"Gold"},{"label":"D","text":"Silver"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Mercury (Hg) and bromine (Br) are the only elements liquid at room temp."},{"id":1289,"text":"The coordination number in NaCl crystal structure is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"8"},{"label":"D","text":"12"}],"answer":1,"solution":"NaCl has face-centered cubic (FCC) structure with each Na surrounded by 6 Cl and vice versa."},{"id":1294,"text":"Which element has the highest ionization energy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Li"},{"label":"B","text":"Na"},{"label":"C","text":"He"},{"label":"D","text":"Ne"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Helium has the highest ionization energy due to its full 1s^2 configuration and small atomic size."},{"id":1295,"text":"Which of the following shows +R effect?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-OH"},{"label":"B","text":"-NO"},{"label":"C","text":"-CN"},{"label":"D","text":"-COOH"}],"answer":0,"solution":"-OH, -NH, -OR show +R (resonance) effect as they donate electrons. -NO, -CN, -COOH show -R effect."},{"id":1300,"text":"The color of phenolphthalein in basic medium is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Colorless"},{"label":"B","text":"Pink"},{"label":"C","text":"Blue"},{"label":"D","text":"Yellow"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Phenolphthalein is colorless in acid and pink in base (pH > 8.3)."},{"id":1302,"text":"The number of moles in 18 g of HO is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5"},{"label":"B","text":"1"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"18"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Molar mass of HO = 18 g/mol. n = 18/18 = 1 mol."},{"id":1305,"text":"What is the pH of a 0.1 M solution of NaOH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"12"},{"label":"C","text":"13"},{"label":"D","text":"14"}],"answer":2,"solution":"NaOH is a strong base, so it completely dissociates in water to give OH- ions. The concentration of OH- ions is equal to the concentration of NaOH, which is 0.1 M. Using the equation pOH = -log[OH-], we get pOH = -log(0.1) = 1. Then, pH + pOH = 14, so pH = 14 - pOH = 14 - 1 = 13."},{"id":1306,"text":"The oxidation state of Mn in KMnO is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+5"},{"label":"B","text":"+6"},{"label":"C","text":"+7"},{"label":"D","text":"+4"}],"answer":2,"solution":"K(+1) + Mn + 4O(-2) = 0 -> +1 + Mn -8 = 0 -> Mn = +7."},{"id":1309,"text":"The most abundant element in Earth's crust is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron"},{"label":"B","text":"Oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"Silicon"},{"label":"D","text":"Aluminum"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Oxygen (~46.6%) is the most abundant element in Earth's crust."},{"id":1310,"text":"The number of valence electrons in carbon is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Carbon (Z=6) has electronic configuration 2,4 -> 4 valence electrons."},{"id":1311,"text":"The pH of 10 M HCl solution is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"7"},{"label":"D","text":"11"}],"answer":1,"solution":"[H] = 10 M. pH = -log[H] = -log(10) = 3."},{"id":1315,"text":"Shape of water molecule is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Linear"},{"label":"B","text":"Trigonal planar"},{"label":"C","text":"Bent/V-shaped"},{"label":"D","text":"Tetrahedral"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Water has 2 bond pairs + 2 lone pairs -> bent shape with ~104.5 degrees  bond angle."},{"id":1318,"text":"The chemical name of common salt is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Sodium nitrate"},{"label":"B","text":"Sodium chloride"},{"label":"C","text":"Sodium carbonate"},{"label":"D","text":"Sodium hydroxide"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Common salt is NaCl - Sodium chloride."},{"id":1319,"text":"Which of the following is an aromatic compound?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclohexane"},{"label":"B","text":"Benzene"},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclohexene"},{"label":"D","text":"1,3-Butadiene"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Benzene follows Hckel's rule (4n+2 pi electrons, n=1) and is planar with delocalized electrons."},{"id":1324,"text":"What is the molecular formula of the compound with the empirical formula CH2O and a molecular weight of 180 g/mol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C6H12O6"},{"label":"B","text":"C3H6O3"},{"label":"C","text":"C2H4O2"},{"label":"D","text":"C4H8O4"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The empirical formula of the compound is CH2O, and its molecular weight is 180 g/mol. The empirical formula weight is 30 g/mol (12 for C, 2 for H, and 16 for O). To find the molecular formula, we divide the molecular weight by the empirical formula weight: 180/30 = 6. Therefore, the molecular formula is (CH2O)6, which is C6H12O6."},{"id":1325,"text":"Which of the following is a strong acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CHCOOH"},{"label":"B","text":"HCl"},{"label":"C","text":"HCO"},{"label":"D","text":"HS"}],"answer":1,"solution":"HCl completely dissociates in water -> strong acid."},{"id":1336,"text":"How many moles of O are required for complete combustion of 1 mole of CH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"5"},{"label":"C","text":"6.5"},{"label":"D","text":"8"}],"answer":2,"solution":"CH + 6.5O -> 4CO + 5HO. So 6.5 moles of O are needed."},{"id":1338,"text":"Which of the following is a Lewis acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NH"},{"label":"B","text":"AlCl"},{"label":"C","text":"HO"},{"label":"D","text":"OH"}],"answer":1,"solution":"AlCl is electron deficient (can accept electron pair), hence a Lewis acid."},{"id":1348,"text":"The number of isomeric pentanes (CH) possible is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"3"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"5"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Pentane has 3 isomers: n-pentane, isopentane, neopentane."},{"id":1349,"text":"Which metal does not react with dilute HCl?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zn"},{"label":"B","text":"Mg"},{"label":"C","text":"Cu"},{"label":"D","text":"Fe"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Copper is below hydrogen in reactivity series, so does not react with dilute HCl."},{"id":1354,"text":"What is the IUPAC name of the compound with the formula CH3CH2CH2OH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propan-1-ol"},{"label":"B","text":"Propan-2-ol"},{"label":"C","text":"Butan-1-ol"},{"label":"D","text":"Butan-2-ol"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The IUPAC name of the compound with the formula CH3CH2CH2OH is Propan-1-ol."},{"id":1355,"text":"What is the molecular formula of the compound with the empirical formula CH2O and a molecular weight of 120 g/mol?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C2H4O2"},{"label":"B","text":"C3H6O3"},{"label":"C","text":"C4H8O4"},{"label":"D","text":"C5H10O5"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The empirical formula is CH2O, and its molecular weight is 30 g/mol. To find the molecular formula, we need to multiply the empirical formula by a factor that will give us the molecular weight of 120 g/mol. The factor is 120 / 30 = 4. So, the molecular formula is (CH2O) * 4 = C4H8O4 * (3/4) = C3H6O3."},{"id":1357,"text":"The gas used in welding is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Methane"},{"label":"B","text":"Ethane"},{"label":"C","text":"Acetylene"},{"label":"D","text":"Propane"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Acetylene (CH) produces high temperature flame used in oxy-acetylene welding."},{"id":1360,"text":"Which of these is NOT a greenhouse gas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CO"},{"label":"B","text":"CH"},{"label":"C","text":"O"},{"label":"D","text":"HO vapor"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Oxygen (O) is not a greenhouse gas. CO, CH, and HO vapor are GHGs."},{"id":1366,"text":"Which of these is a noble gas?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cl"},{"label":"B","text":"N"},{"label":"C","text":"Xenon"},{"label":"D","text":"O"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Xenon (Xe) is a noble gas. Cl, N, O are diatomic gases."},{"id":1370,"text":"What is the oxidation number of chromium in the compound Cr2O7^2-?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+3"},{"label":"B","text":"+4"},{"label":"C","text":"+6"},{"label":"D","text":"+8"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Let's denote the oxidation number of chromium as x. The oxidation number of oxygen is -2. The compound has a charge of -2, so the sum of the oxidation numbers of all the atoms must be equal to -2. Therefore, 2x + 7(-2) = -2. Simplifying the equation, we get 2x - 14 = -2. Then 2x = 12, and x = 6."},{"id":1371,"text":"The functional group in alcohol is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-CHO"},{"label":"B","text":"-COOH"},{"label":"C","text":"-OH"},{"label":"D","text":"-NH"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Alcohols contain the hydroxyl (-OH) functional group."},{"id":1372,"text":"Which process is used to separate crude oil into useful fractions?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Distillation"},{"label":"B","text":"Crystallization"},{"label":"C","text":"Fractional distillation"},{"label":"D","text":"Chromatography"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Fractional distillation separates petroleum into fractions based on boiling points."},{"id":1373,"text":"What is the hybridization of the carbon atom in the molecule CH4?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"sp"},{"label":"B","text":"sp^2"},{"label":"C","text":"sp^3"},{"label":"D","text":"sp^3d"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The molecule CH4 has a tetrahedral geometry, which corresponds to an sp^3 hybridization of the carbon atom."},{"id":1374,"text":"What is the hybridization of the central atom in the molecule CH4?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"sp"},{"label":"B","text":"sp2"},{"label":"C","text":"sp3"},{"label":"D","text":"dsp2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The central atom inch4 is carbon, which is bonded to four hydrogen atoms. Since carbon has four equivalent bonds, its hybridization is sp3."},{"id":1380,"text":"The pH of 0.001 M NaOH is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3"},{"label":"B","text":"7"},{"label":"C","text":"10"},{"label":"D","text":"11"}],"answer":3,"solution":"[OH] = 10, pOH = 3, pH = 14 - 3 = 11."},{"id":1381,"text":"What is the pH of a solution with a hydrogen ion concentration of 2 * 10^(-5) M?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4.7"},{"label":"B","text":"5.5"},{"label":"C","text":"6.5"},{"label":"D","text":"7.5"}],"answer":0,"solution":"pH = -log[H+] = -log(2 * 10^(-5)) ≈ 4.7."},{"id":1466,"text":"10 g of H2 and 64 g of O2 are allowed to react in a 10 L vessel at 300 K to form water vapour. What is the pressure inside the vessel after the reaction goes to completion? (Assume ideal gas behaviour, R = 0.0821 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"6.15 atm"},{"label":"B","text":"12.3 atm"},{"label":"C","text":"18.45 atm"},{"label":"D","text":"24.6 atm"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Compare stoichiometric mole ratios (n_H2/2 = 2.5 vs n_O2/1 = 2); O2 is limiting. Remaining gas moles = excess H2 (5-4=1 mol) + product H2O (4 mol) = 5 mol. Full: Moles H2 = 5, O2 = 2. Reaction 2H2 + O2 → 2H2O consumes 4 mol H2 and 2 mol O2, producing 4 mol H2O. Total moles = 1 + 4 = 5. P = nRT/V = 5 × 0.0821 × 300 / 10 = 12.315 atm ≈ 12.3 atm."},{"id":1467,"text":"For a first-order reaction, the rate constant is 2.303 × 10⁻³ s⁻¹. The time required for 90% completion of the reaction is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1000 s"},{"label":"B","text":"500 s"},{"label":"C","text":"2000 s"},{"label":"D","text":"2303 s"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: For 90% completion in 1st order, fraction remaining = 0.1, so t = (2.303/k) log(1/0.1) = 2.303/k. Substituting k = 2.303×10⁻³ gives t = 1000 s directly. Full: Integrated rate law t = (2.303/k) log(a/(a-x)). For 90% completion, (a/(a-x)) = 100/10 = 10. log 10 = 1. Thus t = 2.303 / (2.303 × 10⁻³) = 10³ s = 1000 s."},{"id":1468,"text":"The EMF of the cell Zn(s) | Zn²⁺(0.1 M) || Cu²⁺(0.01 M) | Cu(s) at 298 K is: (Given: E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = -0.76 V, E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.10 V"},{"label":"B","text":"1.07 V"},{"label":"C","text":"1.13 V"},{"label":"D","text":"0.97 V"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: E°cell = 0.34 - (-0.76) = 1.10 V. Q = [Zn²⁺]/[Cu²⁺] = 10. At 298 K, (0.0591/2)log₁₀Q ≈ 0.03 V. Since Q > 1, Ecell = E°cell - 0.03 = 1.07 V. Full: Ecell = E°cell - (0.0591/2) log₁₀([Zn²⁺]/[Cu²⁺]) = 1.10 - 0.02955 × 1 = 1.07045 V ≈ 1.07 V."},{"id":1469,"text":"6 g of an on-electrolyte solute dissolved in 100 g of water elevates the boiling point by 0.26 K. The molar mass of the solute is: (Kb for water = 0.52 K kg mol⁻¹)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30 g/mol"},{"label":"B","text":"60 g/mol"},{"label":"C","text":"120 g/mol"},{"label":"D","text":"240 g/mol"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: ΔTb = Kb × m → m = 0.26/0.52 = 0.5 mol/kg. Moles of solute = 0.5 × 0.1 kg = 0.05 mol. Molar mass = 6 g / 0.05 mol = 120 g/mol. Full: ΔTb = i·Kb·m (i=1 for non-electrolyte). Molality m = ΔTb/Kb = 0.26/0.52 = 0.5 mol kg⁻¹. Amount of solute = m × mass of solvent(kg) = 0.5 × 0.1 = 0.05 mol. Molar mass = mass/moles = 6 g / 0.05 mol = 120 g/mol."},{"id":1470,"text":"A buffer solution is prepared by mixing 0.1 M acetic acid (CH3COOH) and 0.1 M sodium acetate (CH3COONa). The pKa of acetic acid is 4.74. What is the pH of the buffer solution?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4.44"},{"label":"B","text":"4.74"},{"label":"C","text":"5.04"},{"label":"D","text":"5.74"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For a buffer with equal concentrations of weak acid and its conjugate base, pH = pKa directly (since log([salt]/[acid]) = log 1 = 0). Full solution: Using Henderson–Hasselbalch equation, pH = pKa + log([CH3COO-]/[CH3COOH]) = 4.74 + log(0.1/0.1) = 4.74 + 0 = 4.74."},{"id":1514,"text":"What is the maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated in a subshell with principal quantum number n = 4 and azimuthal quantum number l = 2?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"10"},{"label":"D","text":"14"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: For a given azimuthal quantum number (l), maximum electrons = 2(2l+1). Here l=2 (d-subshell), so 2(5)=10. Full Solution: The quantum numbers n=4, l=2 define the 4d subshell. A d-subshell has 5 orbitals (magnetic quantum numbers -2 to +2). Each orbital holds 2 electrons (opposite spins). Total capacity = 5 × 2 = 10 electrons."},{"id":1515,"text":"Which of the following coordination complexes exhibits optical isomerism?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺"},{"label":"B","text":"[Co(en)₃]³⁺ (en = ethylenediamine)"},{"label":"C","text":"[Co(NH₃)₅Cl]²⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"[CoCl₄]²⁻"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Complexes of type [M(AA)₃] (AA = bidentate ligand) are chiral and show optical isomerism due ton on-superimposable mirror images (Δ and Λ forms). Full Solution: [Co(en)₃]³⁺ has three bidentate 'en' ligands in an octahedral geometry, creating a chiral propeller shape with no plane/center of symmetry. [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺ (MA₄B₂) shows geometrical (cis/trans) isomerism but cis-form has a plane of symmetry. [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]²⁺ (MA₅B) and [CoCl₄]²⁻ (MA₄, tetrahedral) are symmetrical."},{"id":1516,"text":"The correct decreasing order of reactivity towards SN1 reaction for the given alkyl halides is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(CH₃)₃CCl > (CH₃)₂CHCl > CH₃CH₂Cl > CH₃Cl"},{"label":"B","text":"CH₃Cl > CH₃CH₂Cl > (CH₃)₂CHCl > (CH₃)₃CCl"},{"label":"C","text":"(CH₃)₂CHCl > (CH₃)₃CCl > CH₃CH₂Cl > CH₃Cl"},{"label":"D","text":"CH₃CH₂Cl > (CH₃)₂CHCl > (CH₃)₃CCl > CH₃Cl"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: SN1 rate ∝ Carbocation stability. Stability order: 3° > 2° > 1° > Methyl. Full Solution: SN1 proceeds via carbocation formation. (CH₃)₃CCl forms stable 3° carbocation; (CH₃)₂CHCl forms 2°; CH₃CH₂Cl forms 1°; CH₃Cl forms least stable CH₃⁺. Hence reactivity follows carbocation stability: 3° > 2° > 1° > Methyl."},{"id":1517,"text":"18 g of glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) is dissolved in 178.2 g of water. The vapour pressure of pure water at 100°C is 760 mmhg. The vapour pressure of the solution is approximately: (Molar mass: glucose = 180 g mol⁻¹, water = 18 g mol⁻¹)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"759.6 mmhg"},{"label":"B","text":"752.4 mmhg"},{"label":"C","text":"760.4 mmhg"},{"label":"D","text":"748.0 mmhg"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For dilute solutions, relative lowering of vapour pressure (ΔP/P°) ≈ moles of solute / moles of solvent. Moles glucose = 18/180 = 0.1; moles water = 178.2/18 = 9.9. Ratio ≈ 0.0101. ΔP ≈ 760 × 0.0101 ≈ 7.68 mmhg. P_solution ≈ 760 - 7.68 = 752.3 mmhg. Option B is closest. Full Solution: Mole fraction of water = (9.9) / (9.9 + 0.1) = 0.99. Applying Raoult's law, P_solution = 0.99 × 760 = 752.4 mmhg."},{"id":1518,"text":"The spin-only magnetic moment (in Bohr Magneton) of a divalent transition metal ion (Atomic Number = 25) in its high-spin aqueous complex is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.73"},{"label":"B","text":"2.83"},{"label":"C","text":"3.87"},{"label":"D","text":"5.92"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Atomic number 25 is Mn. Mn²⁺ has 3d⁵ configuration. High-spin d⁵ has 5 unpaired electrons (n=5). Memorize spin-only values: n=1→1.73, n=2→2.83, n=3→3.87, n=4→4.90, n=5→5.92. Select 5.92. Full Solution: Mn (Z=25) = [Ar] 3d⁵4s². Mn²⁺ = [Ar] 3d⁵. In high-spin octahedral (aqueous) field, electrons occupy t₂g³ eg² with all spins parallel (n=5). μ_so = √[n(n+2)] = √[5×7] = √35 ≈ 5.92 BM."},{"id":1536,"text":"Which of the following diatomic species has the highest bond order according to Molecular Orbital Theory?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"O₂"},{"label":"B","text":"O₂⁺"},{"label":"C","text":"O₂⁻"},{"label":"D","text":"O₂²⁻"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Bond Order = ½(Nb - Na). For O₂ (12 valence e⁻): BO = 2. For O₂⁺ (11 e⁻): one electron removed from antibonding π* orbital → BO = 2.5. For O₂⁻ (13 e⁻): one electron added to π* → BO = 1.5. For O₂²⁻ (14 e⁻): two electrons added to π* → BO = 1. Highest Bois 2.5 for O₂⁺. Step-by-step: Write MO configuration for each species (σ2s² σ*2s² σ2pz² π2px²=π2py² π*2px¹=π*2py¹ for O₂). Calculate bonding (Nb) and antibonding (Na) electrons. Apply formula. O₂⁺ has configuration ending in π*2px¹, giving Nb=8, Na=3, BO=2.5."},{"id":1537,"text":"For a reaction at 300 K, the standard Gibbs free energy change ΔG° is -5.74 kJ mol⁻¹. The value of the equilibrium constant Kp is approximately: (R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10"},{"label":"B","text":"100"},{"label":"C","text":"1000"},{"label":"D","text":"1"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: At 300 K, 2.303RT ≈ 2.303 × 8.314 × 300 ≈ 5740 J/mol = 5.74 kJ/mol. Use ΔG° = -2.303RT log K. Substituting -5.74 = -5.74 log K ⇒ log K = 1 ⇒ K = 10. Step-by-step: Convert ΔG° to J/mol (-5740 J/mol). Use formula ΔG° = -RT lnk = -2.303RT log₁₀ K. Calculate 2.303 × 8.314 × 300 = 5740 J/mol. Solve -5740 = -5740 log K → log K = 1 → K = 10¹ = 10."},{"id":1538,"text":"The Crystal Field Stabilization Energy (CFSE) for a low-spin d⁶ octahedral complex in terms of Δo is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-0.4 Δo"},{"label":"B","text":"-1.2 Δo"},{"label":"C","text":"-2.4 Δo"},{"label":"D","text":"-0.6 Δo"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Low-spin d⁶ configuration is t2g⁶ eg⁰. CFSE = [(-0.4 × n_t2g) + (0.6 × n_eg)] Δo = (-0.4 × 6) Δo = -2.4 Δo. Step-by-step: In an octahedral field, d-orbitals split into t2g (lower, -0.4 Δo each) and eg (higher, +0.6 Δo each). For low-spin d⁶, electrons fill t2g completely before occupying eg (strong field ligand). All 6 electrons occupy t2g orbitals. CFSE = 6 × (-0.4 Δo) + 0 × (0.6 Δo) = -2.4 Δo."},{"id":1539,"text":"Which of the following compounds is the weakest Brønsted acid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acetic acid (CH₃COOH)"},{"label":"B","text":"Phenol (C₆H₅OH)"},{"label":"C","text":"Water (H₂O)"},{"label":"D","text":"Ethanol (C₂H₅OH)"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Acidic strength order: Carboxylic acid > Phenol > Water > Alcohol. Stability of conjugate base: Carboxylate (resonance, 2 equiv. structures) > Phenoxide (resonance, charge on O/C) > Hydroxide (small size, high charge density) > Alkoxide (destabilized by +I effect of alkyl group). Weakest acid has least stable conjugate base → Ethanol. Step-by-step: Compare conjugate bases: CH₃COO⁻ (resonance stabilized), C₆H₅O⁻ (resonance stabilized but less than carboxylate), OH⁻ (no resonance, high charge density), C₂H₅O⁻ (electron-donating ethyl group increases electron density on O, destabilizing anion). Least stable anion = strongest base = weakest acid = Ethanol."},{"id":1540,"text":"The shape of the XeF₄ molecule as predicted by VSEPR theory is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetrahedral"},{"label":"B","text":"Square Planar"},{"label":"C","text":"See-saw"},{"label":"D","text":"Trigonal Bipyramidal"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Xe has 8 valence electrons. 4 bonds to Fuse 4 electrons. Remaining 4 electrons = 2 lone pairs. Steric Number = 4 bond pairs + 2 lone pairs = 6 (sp³d² hybridization). Electron geometry = Octahedral. Molecular geometry with 2 lone pairs at axial positions (180° apart) = Square Planar. Step-by-step: Calculate total valence electrons = 8 (Xe) + 4×7 (F) = 36. Xe forms 4 single bonds (8 electrons used). Remaining 28 electrons: 24 on F atoms (6 each), 4 on Xe (2 lone pairs). Steric number = 6. Arrangement: Octahedral. Lone pairs occupy axial positions to minimize lp-lp repulsion. Resulting shape: Square Planar."},{"id":1551,"text":"For a first-order reaction, the half-life is 20 minutes. The time required for 75% completion of the reaction is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 min"},{"label":"B","text":"20 min"},{"label":"C","text":"60 min"},{"label":"D","text":"80 min"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: 75% completion corresponds to two half-lives (50% in first, 25% in second). Time = 2 × t₁/₂ = 2 × 20 = 40 min. Full Solution: For a first-order reaction, t₁/₂ = 0.693/k. Fraction remaining after time t is (1/2)^(t/t₁/₂). For 75% completion, fraction remaining = 0.25 = (1/2)², so t/t₁/₂ = 2 → t = 40 min."},{"id":1552,"text":"Which of the following carbocations is most stabilized by hyperconjugation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CH₃⁺"},{"label":"B","text":"CH₃CH₂⁺"},{"label":"C","text":"(CH₃)₂CH⁺"},{"label":"D","text":"(CH₃)₃C⁺"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Hyperconjugation stability ∝ number of α-hydrogens. CH₃⁺ (0 α-H), CH₃CH₂⁺ (3 α-H), (CH₃)₂CH⁺ (6 α-H), (CH₃)₃C⁺ (9 α-H). Maximum α-H gives maximum stability. Full Solution: Hyperconjugation involves delocalization of σ-electrons of C-H bonds (α to the positive charge) into the empty p-orbital. The tert-butyl cation (CH₃)₃C⁺ has 9 α-hydrogens, the highest among the options, leading to the greatest charge dispersal and stability."},{"id":1553,"text":"The correct decreasing order of acidic strength for the oxoacids of chlorine is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"HClO > HClO₂ > HClO₃ > HClO₄"},{"label":"B","text":"HClO₄ > HClO₃ > HClO₂ > HClO"},{"label":"C","text":"HClO₃ > HClO₄ > HClO₂ > HClO"},{"label":"D","text":"HClO₂ > HClO > HClO₃ > HClO₄"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Acidic strength of oxoacids increases with the oxidation state of the central atom (or number of terminal oxygen atoms). Oxidation states: HClO (+1), HClO₂ (+3), HClO₃ (+5), HClO₄ (+7). Order: HClO₄ > HClO₃ > HClO₂ > HClO. Full Solution: Higher oxidation state increases electron withdrawal from the O-H bond, facilitating proton release. The conjugate base stability also increases with more terminal oxygens (greater resonance delocalization of negative charge). Thus, perchloric acid (HClO₄) is the strongest."},{"id":1554,"text":"The standard electrode potentials are: Zn²⁺/Zn = –0.76 V, Cu²⁺/Cu = +0.34 V. For the cell Zn(s) | Zn²⁺(0.1 M) || Cu²⁺(0.01 M) | Cu(s) at 298 K, the EMF of the cell is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.10 V"},{"label":"B","text":"1.07 V"},{"label":"C","text":"1.03 V"},{"label":"D","text":"0.99 V"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: E°cell = 0.34 – (–0.76) = 1.10 V. Since the reaction quotient Q = [Zn²⁺]/[Cu²⁺] = 10, the Nernst term at 298 K is (0.0591/2)log10 = 0.02955 V. Subtracting gives 1.10 – 0.02955 ≈ 1.07 V. Stepwise: E°cell = 1.10 V; n = 2; E = E° – (0.0591/n) log Q = 1.10 – 0.02955 × 1 = 1.07045 V ≈ 1.07 V."},{"id":1555,"text":"The decreasing order of reactivity towards SN2 reaction for the following alkyl bromides is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"CH₃Br > CH₃CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHBr > (CH₃)₃CBr"},{"label":"B","text":"(CH₃)₃CBr > (CH₃)₂CHBr > CH₃CH₂Br > CH₃Br"},{"label":"C","text":"CH₃CH₂Br > CH₃Br > (CH₃)₂CHBr > (CH₃)₃CBr"},{"label":"D","text":"(CH₃)₂CHBr > CH₃Br > CH₃CH₂Br > (CH₃)₃CBr"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: SN2 reactivity decreases with increasing steric hindrance: methyl > primary > secondary > tertiary. Option A follows this order exactly. Stepwise: CH₃Br (methyl) least hindered, fastest; CH₃CH₂Br (primary) next; (CH₃)₂CHBr (secondary) slower; (CH₃)₃CBr (tertiary) essentially no SN2. Hence the correct sequence is CH₃Br > CH₃CH₂Br > (CH₃)₂CHBr > (CH₃)₃CBr."},{"id":1566,"text":"The solubility product (Ksp) of AgCl is 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ at 298 K. What is the molar solubility of AgCl in a 0.1 M NaCl solution? (Assume volume change is negligible)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.8 × 10⁻⁹ M"},{"label":"B","text":"1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰ M"},{"label":"C","text":"1.34 × 10⁻⁵ M"},{"label":"D","text":"1.8 × 10⁻¹¹ M"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: In presence of a common ion (Cl⁻ from NaCl), solubility S ≈ Ksp / [Common Ion] = 1.8×10⁻¹⁰ / 0.1 = 1.8×10⁻⁹ M. Full Solution: Let S be solubility. [Ag⁺] = S, [Cl⁻] = 0.1 + S ≈ 0.1. Ksp = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] = S × 0.1 = 1.8×10⁻¹⁰ ⇒ S = 1.8×10⁻⁹ M."},{"id":1567,"text":"An element with atomic mass 108 g mol⁻¹ crystallizes in an FCC lattice. If the density of the crystal is 10.5 g cm⁻³, what is the edge length of the unit cell? (Nₐ = 6.022 × 10²³ mol⁻¹)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"300 pm"},{"label":"B","text":"409 pm"},{"label":"C","text":"500 pm"},{"label":"D","text":"288 pm"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For FCC, Z=4. Use ρ = (Z×M)/(Nₐ×a³) ⇒ a³ = (4×108)/(10.5×6.022×10²³) = 6.83×10⁻²³ cm³ ⇒ a = 4.09×10⁻⁸ cm = 409 pm. Full Solution: Rearrange density formula for a³. Substitute Z=4 (FCC), M=108, ρ=10.5, Nₐ=6.022×10²³. Calculate a³ = 6.83×10⁻²³ cm³. Cube root gives a ≈ 4.09×10⁻⁸ cm = 409 pm."},{"id":1568,"text":"Butanamide (CH₃CH₂CH₂CONH₂) is treated with Br₂ and NaOH (Hofmann Bromamide reaction). The major organic product formed is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Butanamine (CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₂NH₂)"},{"label":"B","text":"Propanamine (CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂)"},{"label":"C","text":"Propanoic acid (CH₃CH₂COOH)"},{"label":"D","text":"Butanoic acid (CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH)"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Hofmann rearrangement degrades amide to amine with ONE LESS carbon (loss of carbonyl carbon as CO₂). Butanamide (C4) → Propanamine (C3). Full Solution: R-CONH₂ + Br₂ + 4NaOH → R-NH₂ + Na₂CO₃ + 2NaBr + 2H₂O. The alkyl group R migrates to nitrogen. For Butanamide, R = CH₃CH₂CH₂⁻ (propyl). Product is CH₃CH₂CH₂NH₂ (Propanamine)."},{"id":1569,"text":"The half-life of a reaction is found to be 10 minutes when the initial concentration of the reactant is 0.1 M. When the initial concentration is reduced to 0.05 M, the half-life becomes 20 minutes. The order of the reaction is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"2"},{"label":"C","text":"0"},{"label":"D","text":"3"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For any order n, t₁/₂ ∝ 1/[A]₀ⁿ⁻¹. Here concentration halved (ratio 1/2) and t₁/₂ doubled (ratio 2). So 2 = (1/2)ⁿ⁻¹ ⇒ 2 = 2¹⁻ⁿ ⇒ 1 = 1−n ⇒ n=2. Full Solution: Using the half-life dependence on initial concentration, t₁/₂ = k' / [A]₀ⁿ⁻¹. Taking ratio: t₁/₂(2)/t₁/₂(1) = ([A]₀(1)/[A]₀(2))ⁿ⁻¹. Substituting 20/10 = (0.1/0.05)ⁿ⁻¹ gives 2 = 2ⁿ⁻¹, hence n−1 = 1 and n = 2."},{"id":1570,"text":"The quantity of electricity (in Coulombs) required to deposit 27 g of aluminium (Atomic mass = 27 g mol⁻¹) from an aqueous solution of Al³⁺ ions is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"289500"},{"label":"B","text":"96500"},{"label":"C","text":"193000"},{"label":"D","text":"386000"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Equivalents = Mass / Equivalent Weight. Eq. wt of Al = 27/3 = 9. Equivalents = 27/9 = 3. Charge = Equivalents × F = 3 × 96500 = 289500 C. Full Solution: The reduction half-reaction is Al³⁺ + 3e⁻ → Al. 3 moles of electrons (3F) are required to deposit 1 mole (27 g) of Al. Using Faraday'sse cond law, Q = (mass × n × F) / M = (27 × 3 × 96500) / 27 = 3 × 96500 = 289500 C."},{"id":1586,"text":"An electron is accelerated from rest through a potential difference of 150 V. The de Broglie wavelength associated with the electron is approximately: (Use h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, mₑ = 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg, e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.00 Å"},{"label":"B","text":"1.23 Å"},{"label":"C","text":"0.85 Å"},{"label":"D","text":"1.50 Å"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Use the direct formula λ(Å) = 12.27/√V. For V = 150 V, λ = 12.27/√150 ≈ 12.27/12.25 ≈ 1.00 Å. Full Solution: Kinetic energy E = eV = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ × 150 = 2.4×10⁻¹⁷ J. Momentum p = √(2mE) = √(2 × 9.1×10⁻³¹ × 2.4×10⁻¹⁷) ≈ 6.62×10⁻²⁴ kg m/s. λ = h/p = 6.63×10⁻³⁴ / 6.62×10⁻²⁴ ≈ 1.00×10⁻¹⁰ m = 1.00 Å."},{"id":1587,"text":"The spin-only magnetic moment (in Bohr Magneton) of the complex ion [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺ is closest to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0 (Diamagnetic)"},{"label":"B","text":"2.84 BM"},{"label":"C","text":"4.90 BM"},{"label":"D","text":"5.92 BM"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Fe²⁺ is d⁶. H₂O is a weak field ligand → high spin configuration (t₂g⁴ eg²) → 4 unpaired electrons. Use μ = √[n(n+2)] = √[4×6] = √24 ≈ 4.90 BM. Full Solution: Electronic configuration of Fe²⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁶. In an octahedral field with weak field ligand H₂O, pairing energy > CFSE, so high spin: t₂g⁴ eg². Number of unpaired electrons n = 4. Spin-only formula μ = √[n(n+2)] BM = √[4(6)] = √24 = 4.899 ≈ 4.90 BM."},{"id":1588,"text":"For a reaction at 298 K, ΔH = +50 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS = +100 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. The minimum temperature (in K) above which the reaction becomes spontaneous is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"300 K"},{"label":"B","text":"400 K"},{"label":"C","text":"500 K"},{"label":"D","text":"600 K"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Spontaneity requires ΔG < 0. At equilibrium ΔG = 0 = ΔH - TΔS → T = ΔH/ΔS. Convert ΔH to J: 50,000 J mol⁻¹. T = 50,000 / 100 = 500 K. Reaction spontaneous for T > 500 K. Full Solution: Gibbs free energy ΔG = ΔH - TΔS. For spontaneity, ΔG < 0 ⇒ ΔH - TΔS < 0 ⇒ T > ΔH/ΔS. Given ΔH = +50 kJ mol⁻¹ = 50,000 J mol⁻¹ and ΔS = +100 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. T = 50,000 J mol⁻¹ / 100 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹ = 500 K. Thus, above 500 K the reaction is spontaneous."},{"id":1589,"text":"Which of the following compounds will NOT give a positive iodoform test (yellow precipitate of CHI₃) with I₂/NaOH?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH)"},{"label":"B","text":"Acetaldehyde (CH₃CHO)"},{"label":"C","text":"Acetone (CH₃COCH₃)"},{"label":"D","text":"Benzaldehyde (C₆H₅CHO)"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Iodoform test is given by compounds containing CH₃CO– (methyl ketone) or CH₃CH(OH)– (secondary alcohol with methyl adjacent to carbinol carbon) which oxidize to methyl ketones. Ethanol → CH₃CHO (gives test). Acetaldehyde → CH₃CHO (gives test). Acetone → CH₃COCH₃ (gives test). Benzaldehyde (PhCHO) lacks CH₃CO– group → No test. Full Solution: The iodoform reaction requires a CH₃C=O or CH₃CH(OH) group. Ethanol oxidizes to acetaldehyde (CH₃CHO) which has CH₃CO–. Acetaldehyde has CH₃CO–. Acetone is CH₃COCH₃. Benzaldehyde is C₆H₅CHO; the carbonyl carbon is attached to Hand Ph, notch₃. Hence, it does not respond."},{"id":1590,"text":"Which of the following molecules has a net dipole moment of zero?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"NH₃"},{"label":"B","text":"BF₃"},{"label":"C","text":"H₂O"},{"label":"D","text":"SO₂"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Check molecular geometry symmetry. BF₃ is trigonal planar (sp², 120°) with three identical B–F bonds → vector sum = 0. NH₃ (pyramidal), H₂O (bent), SO₂ (bent) are asymmetrical → net μ > 0. Full Solution: Dipole moment is a vector quantity. BF₃ has trigonal planar geometry (D₃h symmetry); the three B–F bond dipoles are at 120° and cancel each other resulting in μ = 0. NH₃ (C₃v, pyramidal), H₂O (C₂v, bent), and SO₂ (C₂v, bent) have resultant dipole moments due to their asymmetric shapes and lone pairs."},{"id":1638,"text":"As per the table, the percentage yield of a reaction was calculated using the mass of product obtained. Which of the following statements is a correct shortcut to quickly estimate the percentage yield? Table: | Theoretical mass (g) | Actual mass (g) | |----------------------|-----------------| | 45.0 | 36.0 |","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Multiply actual mass by 100 and divide by theoretical mass."},{"label":"B","text":"Subtract actual mass from theoretical mass, then divide by theoretical mass and multiply by 100."},{"label":"C","text":"Add actual and theoretical masses, then divide by 2 and multiply by 100."},{"label":"D","text":"Divide theoretical mass by actual mass and multiply by 100."}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: % yield = (actual / theoretical) ×100; just a direct ratio, no extra steps. Full solution: % yield = (36.0 g / 45.0 g) ×100 = 0.8×100 = 80 %. Option A states the exact shortcut."},{"id":1639,"text":"The following data represent the absorbance (A) of a solution measured at different concentrations (c, mol L⁻¹). Using Beer's law (A = εlc, l = 1 cm), which concentration gives the highest absorbance? Table: | c (mol L⁻¹) | A | |------------|---| | 0.10 | 0.45 | | 0.25 | 1.12 | | 0.40 | 1.78 | | 0.55 | 2.45 |","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.10 mol L⁻¹"},{"label":"B","text":"0.25 mol L⁻¹"},{"label":"C","text":"0.40 mol L⁻¹"},{"label":"D","text":"0.55 mol L⁻¹"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: In Beer's law absorbance is directly proportional to concentration; the largest concentration gives the largest absorbance. Full solution: Compare the A values – they increase with c. The highest A (2.45) corresponds to c = 0.55 mol L⁻¹. Hence option D (index 3)."},{"id":1640,"text":"The following data give the rate of formation of product (mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹) at different concentrations of reactant A (mol L⁻¹). Using the method of initial rates, determine the order of reaction with respect to A. Table: | [A] (mol L⁻¹) | Rate (×10⁻³ mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹) | |---------------|--------------------------| | 0.10 | 2.0 | | 0.20 | 8.0 | | 0.40 | 32.0 |","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zero order"},{"label":"B","text":"First order"},{"label":"C","text":"Second order"},{"label":"D","text":"Third order"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Compare how rate changes when concentration doubles. If rate quadruples, order is 2 (since 2²=4). Full solution: From 0.10→0.20 M, [A] doubles, rate goes from 2.0→8.0 (×4). Doubling again (0.20→0.40 M) gives rate 8.0→32.0 (×4). Consistent factor of 4 for each doubling ⇒ rate ∝ [A]². Hence second order. Option C (index 2)."},{"id":1641,"text":"An aqueous solution of a weak binary electrolyte AB (concentration 0.1 mol L⁻¹) exhibits an osmotic pressure of 3.0 atm at 300 K. Assuming the gas constant R = 0.0821 L atm K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, the degree of dissociation of AB is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.10"},{"label":"B","text":"0.22"},{"label":"C","text":"0.35"},{"label":"D","text":"0.45"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Calculate van't Hoff factor i = Π/CRT. CRT = 0.1 × 0.0821 × 300 ≈ 2.463. i = 3.0/2.463 ≈ 1.22. For AB ⇌ A⁺ + B⁻ (n=2), α = (i−1)/(n−1) = 0.22. Worked: Osmotic pressure Π = iCRT ⇒ i = Π/CRT = 3.0 / (0.1 × 0.0821 × 300) = 1.218. Degree of dissociation α = (i−1)/(ν−1) where ν=2 (ions produced). α = 0.218 ≈ 0.22."},{"id":1642,"text":"The rate constant of a reaction increases by a factor of 4 when the temperature is raised from 300 K to 310 K. The activation energy (Eₐ) of the reaction in kJ mol⁻¹ is closest to: (Use R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹, ln 2 = 0.693)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"53.6"},{"label":"B","text":"76.4"},{"label":"C","text":"107.2"},{"label":"D","text":"121.8"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Near 300 K, a 10 K rise doubles the rate for Eₐ ≈ 53.6 kJ mol⁻¹ (using ln2 = Eₐ/R × 10/300²). Since rate increases 4-fold (doubles twice), Eₐ ≈ 2 × 53.6 = 107.2 kJ mol⁻¹. Worked: Arrhenius equation: ln(k₂/k₁) = (Eₐ/R)(1/T₁ − 1/T₂). ln 4 = 2 ln 2 = 1.386. (1/300 − 1/310) = 10/93000 = 1/9300. Eₐ = 1.386 × 8.314 × 9300 ≈ 107,200 J mol⁻¹ = 107.2 kJ mol⁻¹."}]},{"name":"Physics","count":108,"questions":[{"id":1112,"text":"A particle is moving with velocity v = 2t² + 3t. Find its acceleration at t = 2s.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"11 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"10 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"8 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"14 m/s²"}],"answer":0,"solution":"a = dv/dt = d(2t² + 3t)/dt = 4t + 3. At t = 2: a = 4(2) + 3 = 11 m/s²."},{"id":1113,"text":"A convex lens has focal length 20 cm. At what distance from the lens should an object be placed to get a real image twice the size of object?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"30 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"20 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"60 cm"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Magnification m = -2 (real image). m = v/u = -2 ⇒ v = -2u. Lens formula: 1/f = 1/v - 1/u. 1/20 = 1/(-2u) - 1/u = -3/(2u). u = -30 cm. So object distance = 30 cm."},{"id":1114,"text":"The escape velocity of a body from Earth is 11.2 km/s. What is the escape velocity from a planet having mass 4 times and radius twice that of Earth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"22.4 km/s"},{"label":"B","text":"5.6 km/s"},{"label":"C","text":"15.8 km/s"},{"label":"D","text":"11.2 km/s"}],"answer":2,"solution":"v_e = v(2GM/R). For new planet: v_e' = v(2G·4M/2R) = v(2·2GM/R) = v2·v(2GM/R) = v2 × 11.2 = 15.84 km/s."},{"id":1115,"text":"A wire of resistance R is stretched to double its length. New resistance will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R/2"},{"label":"B","text":"R"},{"label":"C","text":"4R"},{"label":"D","text":"2R"}],"answer":2,"solution":"When length is doubled (L' = 2L), cross-sectional area halves (A' = A/2). R' = ρL'/A' = ρ(2L)/(A/2) = 4ρL/A = 4R."},{"id":1116,"text":"The de Broglie wavelength of an electron moving with velocity v is λ. If velocity is doubled, wavelength becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2λ"},{"label":"B","text":"λ/2"},{"label":"C","text":"4λ"},{"label":"D","text":"λ/4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"λ = h/p = h/(mv). If v doubles, λ' = h/(m·2v) = λ/2."},{"id":1128,"text":"The equivalent resistance of three 6 Ω resistors connected in parallel is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"18 Ω"},{"label":"B","text":"6 Ω"},{"label":"C","text":"2 Ω"},{"label":"D","text":"3 Ω"}],"answer":2,"solution":"1/Rp = 1/6 + 1/6 + 1/6 = 3/6 = 1/2 ⇒ Rp = 2 Ω."},{"id":1129,"text":"The photon energy of light with wavelength 500 nm is (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ Js):","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.48 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"},{"label":"B","text":"3.98 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"},{"label":"C","text":"1.99 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"},{"label":"D","text":"4.97 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"}],"answer":1,"solution":"E = hc/λ = (6.63×10⁻³⁴ × 3×10⁸)/(500×10⁻⁹) = 3.98×10⁻¹⁹ J."},{"id":1130,"text":"In SHM, the acceleration is maximum at:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean position"},{"label":"B","text":"Equilibrium position"},{"label":"C","text":"Extreme position"},{"label":"D","text":"Same everywhere"}],"answer":2,"solution":"a = -ω²x. Acceleration is maximum at extreme positions (x = ±A) and zero at mean position."},{"id":1131,"text":"First law of thermodynamics is based on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conservation of momentum"},{"label":"B","text":"Conservation of energy"},{"label":"C","text":"Conservation of mass"},{"label":"D","text":"Conservation of charge"}],"answer":1,"solution":"ΔU = Q - W. First law is the law of conservation of energy for thermodynamic systems."},{"id":1142,"text":"A car accelerates uniformly from rest to 20 m/s in 10 s. The acceleration is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"2 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"4 m/s²"}],"answer":1,"solution":"a = (v-u)/t = (20-0)/10 = 2 m/s²."},{"id":1143,"text":"The magnetic field inside a solenoid is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zero"},{"label":"B","text":"Uniform"},{"label":"C","text":"Varying linearly"},{"label":"D","text":"Maximum at ends"}],"answer":1,"solution":"B = µ0nI inside a long solenoid — uniform field."},{"id":1144,"text":"A body is projected vertically upward with velocity 20 m/s. Maximum height reached is (g=10 m/s²):","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 m"},{"label":"B","text":"20 m"},{"label":"C","text":"40 m"},{"label":"D","text":"5 m"}],"answer":1,"solution":"H = u²/2g = 400/20 = 20 m."},{"id":1145,"text":"Which of the following has the highest penetrating power?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"α-rays"},{"label":"B","text":"β-rays"},{"label":"C","text":"γ-rays"},{"label":"D","text":"UV rays"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Gamma rays have the highest penetrating power due to their high energy and no charge."},{"id":1146,"text":"The efficiency of a Carnot engine operating between 500 K and 300 K is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60%"},{"label":"B","text":"40%"},{"label":"C","text":"50%"},{"label":"D","text":"30%"}],"answer":1,"solution":"η = 1 - T2/T1 = 1 - 300/500 = 0.4 = 40%."},{"id":1147,"text":"The unit of electric current in SI is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coulomb"},{"label":"B","text":"Ampere"},{"label":"C","text":"Volt"},{"label":"D","text":"Ohm"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Electric current is measured in Amperes (A)."},{"id":1148,"text":"Two resistors 4 Ω and 6 Ω are connected in series. The total resistance is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.4 Ω"},{"label":"B","text":"10 Ω"},{"label":"C","text":"24 Ω"},{"label":"D","text":"4 Ω"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Rₛ = 4 + 6 = 10 Ω."},{"id":1149,"text":"The momentum of a photon of energy E is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E/c²"},{"label":"B","text":"E/c"},{"label":"C","text":"Ec"},{"label":"D","text":"hE/c"}],"answer":1,"solution":"p = E/c for a photon (since E = pc)."},{"id":1150,"text":"The temperature at which Celsius and Fahrenheit scales read the same is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0°"},{"label":"B","text":"32°"},{"label":"C","text":"-40°"},{"label":"D","text":"100°"}],"answer":2,"solution":"C/5 = (F-32)/9. Set C = F = x: x/5 = (x-32)/9 ⇒ 9x = 5x-160 ⇒ x = -40."},{"id":1151,"text":"Young's modulus is defined for which type of deformation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shear"},{"label":"B","text":"Longitudinal strain"},{"label":"C","text":"Volume strain"},{"label":"D","text":"Torsion"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Y = stress/strain for longitudinal (tensile/compressive) deformation."},{"id":1172,"text":"A ball dropped from height h hits the ground with speed v. The value of v is proportional to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"h"},{"label":"B","text":"vh"},{"label":"C","text":"h²"},{"label":"D","text":"1/h"}],"answer":1,"solution":"v = √(2gh) ⇒ v ∝ √h."},{"id":1173,"text":"If the distance between two charges is halved, the force between them becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Half"},{"label":"B","text":"Double"},{"label":"C","text":"Four times"},{"label":"D","text":"Same"}],"answer":2,"solution":"F ∝ 1/r². If r → r/2, F → 4F."},{"id":1174,"text":"SI unit of resistance is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Volt"},{"label":"B","text":"Ampere"},{"label":"C","text":"Coulomb"},{"label":"D","text":"Ohm"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Resistance is measured in Ohms (O)."},{"id":1176,"text":"The Doppler effect in sound is observed when there is relative motion between source and:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Observer only"},{"label":"B","text":"Medium only"},{"label":"C","text":"Observer or source"},{"label":"D","text":"Neither"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Doppler effect occurs when source or observer is in motion relative to each other."},{"id":1177,"text":"The SI unit of magnetic flux is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tesla"},{"label":"B","text":"Gauss"},{"label":"C","text":"Weber"},{"label":"D","text":"Henry"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Magnetic flux is measured in Webers (Wb)."},{"id":1178,"text":"A body of mass 1 kg has weight approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 N"},{"label":"B","text":"10 N"},{"label":"C","text":"100 N"},{"label":"D","text":"0.1 N"}],"answer":1,"solution":"W = mg = 1 × 10 = 10 N (approx)."},{"id":1179,"text":"The phenomenon of splitting of white light into its components is called:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reflection"},{"label":"B","text":"Refraction"},{"label":"C","text":"Dispersion"},{"label":"D","text":"Diffraction"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Dispersion is the splitting of white light into its constituent colors (VIBGYOR)."},{"id":1180,"text":"Transformer works on the principle of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Self induction"},{"label":"B","text":"Mutual induction"},{"label":"C","text":"Electromagnetic induction"},{"label":"D","text":"Both Band C"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Transformer works on electromagnetic induction via mutual induction between coils."},{"id":1181,"text":"The speed of sound is maximum in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Air"},{"label":"B","text":"Water"},{"label":"C","text":"Steel"},{"label":"D","text":"Vacuum"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Sound travels fastest in solids (steel ~5000 m/s) due to close molecular arrangement."},{"id":1203,"text":"A ball dropped from height h hits the ground with speed v. The value of v is proportional to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"h"},{"label":"B","text":"√h"},{"label":"C","text":"h²"},{"label":"D","text":"1/h"}],"answer":1,"solution":"v = √(2gh) → v ∝ √h."},{"id":1206,"text":"A wire of resistance R is stretched to double its length. New resistance will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R/2"},{"label":"B","text":"R"},{"label":"C","text":"4R"},{"label":"D","text":"2R"}],"answer":2,"solution":"When length is doubled (L' = 2L), cross-sectional area halves (A' = A/2). R' = ρL'/A' = ρ(2L)/(A/2) = 4ρL/A = 4R."},{"id":1210,"text":"In SHM, the acceleration is maximum at:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean position"},{"label":"B","text":"Equilibrium position"},{"label":"C","text":"Extreme position"},{"label":"D","text":"Same everywhere"}],"answer":2,"solution":"a = -ω²x. Acceleration is maximum at extreme positions (x = ±A) and zero at mean position."},{"id":1211,"text":"A car accelerates uniformly from rest to 20 m/s in 10 s. The acceleration is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"2 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"4 m/s²"}],"answer":1,"solution":"a = (v-u)/t = (20-0)/10 = 2 m/s²."},{"id":1213,"text":"The escape velocity of a body from Earth is 11.2 km/s. What is the escape velocity from a planet having mass 4 times and radius twice that of Earth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"22.4 km/s"},{"label":"B","text":"5.6 km/s"},{"label":"C","text":"15.8 km/s"},{"label":"D","text":"11.2 km/s"}],"answer":2,"solution":"v_e = √(2GM/R). For new planet: v_e' = √(2G·4M/2R) = √(2·2GM/R) = √2·√(2GM/R) = √2 × 11.2 = 15.84 km/s."},{"id":1214,"text":"SI unit of resistance is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Volt"},{"label":"B","text":"Ampere"},{"label":"C","text":"Coulomb"},{"label":"D","text":"Ohm"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Resistance is measured in Ohms (Ω)."},{"id":1215,"text":"A particle is moving with velocity v = 2t² + 3t. Find its acceleration at t = 2s.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"11 m/s²"},{"label":"B","text":"10 m/s²"},{"label":"C","text":"8 m/s²"},{"label":"D","text":"14 m/s²"}],"answer":0,"solution":"a = dv/dt = d(2t² + 3t)/dt = 4t + 3. At t = 2: a = 4(2) + 3 = 11 m/s²."},{"id":1219,"text":"A body of mass 1 kg has weight approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 N"},{"label":"B","text":"10 N"},{"label":"C","text":"100 N"},{"label":"D","text":"0.1 N"}],"answer":1,"solution":"W = mg = 1 × 10 = 10 N (approx)."},{"id":1224,"text":"First law of thermodynamics is based on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conservation of momentum"},{"label":"B","text":"Conservation of energy"},{"label":"C","text":"Conservation of mass"},{"label":"D","text":"Conservation of charge"}],"answer":1,"solution":"ΔU = Q - W. First law is the law of conservation of energy for thermodynamic systems."},{"id":1232,"text":"Young's modulus is defined for which type of deformation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shear"},{"label":"B","text":"Longitudinal strain"},{"label":"C","text":"Volume strain"},{"label":"D","text":"Torsion"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Y = stress/strain for longitudinal (tensile/compressive) deformation."},{"id":1233,"text":"A convex lens has focal length 20 cm. At what distance from the lens should an object be placed to get a real image twice the size of object?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"30 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"20 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"60 cm"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Magnification m = -2 (real image). m = v/u = -2 -> v = -2u. Lens formula: 1/f = 1/v - 1/u. 1/20 = 1/(-2u) - 1/u = -3/(2u). u = -30 cm. So object distance = 30 cm."},{"id":1234,"text":"A wire of resistance R is stretched to double its length. New resistance will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R/2"},{"label":"B","text":"R"},{"label":"C","text":"4R"},{"label":"D","text":"2R"}],"answer":2,"solution":"When length is doubled (L' = 2L), cross-sectional area halves (A' = A/2). R' = L'/A' = (2L)/(A/2) = 4L/A = 4R."},{"id":1241,"text":"The momentum of a photon of energy E is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E/c^2"},{"label":"B","text":"E/c"},{"label":"C","text":"Ec"},{"label":"D","text":"hE/c"}],"answer":1,"solution":"p = E/c for a photon (since E = pc)."},{"id":1247,"text":"The speed of sound is maximum in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Air"},{"label":"B","text":"Water"},{"label":"C","text":"Steel"},{"label":"D","text":"Vacuum"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Sound travels fastest in solids (steel ~5000 m/s) due to close molecular arrangement."},{"id":1250,"text":"The de Broglie wavelength of an electron moving with velocity v is . If velocity is doubled, wavelength becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"/2"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"/4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"= h/p = h/(mv). If v doubles, ' = h/(m.2v) = /2."},{"id":1256,"text":"A particle is moving with velocity v = 2t^2 + 3t. Find its acceleration at t = 2s.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"11 m/s^2"},{"label":"B","text":"10 m/s^2"},{"label":"C","text":"8 m/s^2"},{"label":"D","text":"14 m/s^2"}],"answer":0,"solution":"a = dv/dt = d(2t^2 + 3t)/dt = 4t + 3. At t = 2: a = 4(2) + 3 = 11 m/s^2."},{"id":1258,"text":"The phenomenon of splitting of white light into its components is called:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reflection"},{"label":"B","text":"Refraction"},{"label":"C","text":"Dispersion"},{"label":"D","text":"Diffraction"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Dispersion is the splitting of white light into its constituent colors (VIBGYOR)."},{"id":1273,"text":"The escape velocity of a body from Earth is 11.2 km/s. What is the escape velocity from a planet having mass 4 times and radius twice that of Earth?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"22.4 km/s"},{"label":"B","text":"5.6 km/s"},{"label":"C","text":"15.8 km/s"},{"label":"D","text":"11.2 km/s"}],"answer":2,"solution":"v_e = sqrt(2GM/R). For new planet: v_e' = sqrt(2G.4M/2R) = sqrt(2.2GM/R) = sqrt2.sqrt(2GM/R) = sqrt2 x 11.2 = 15.84 km/s."},{"id":1275,"text":"Young's modulus is defined for which type of deformation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shear"},{"label":"B","text":"Longitudinal strain"},{"label":"C","text":"Volume strain"},{"label":"D","text":"Torsion"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Y = stress/strain for longitudinal (tensile/compressive) deformation."},{"id":1276,"text":"A Carnot engine operates between two reservoirs at temperatures 500 K and 300 K. What is the efficiency of the engine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.2"},{"label":"B","text":"0.4"},{"label":"C","text":"0.6"},{"label":"D","text":"0.8"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The efficiency of a Carnot engine is given by the equation eta = 1 - (T2/T1), where T1 and T2 are the temperatures of the two reservoirs. For the given temperatures, eta = 1 - (300/500) = 1 - 0.6 = 0.4."},{"id":1279,"text":"A convex lens has a focal length of 10 cm. If an object is placed 20 cm from the lens, what is the image distance?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"20 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"-10 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"-20 cm"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Lens formula: 1/f = 1/do + 1/di, with f = +10 cm (convex) and do = 20 cm. So 1/di = 1/10 - 1/20 = 1/20, giving di = +20 cm - a real, inverted image formed on the opposite side of the lens."},{"id":1280,"text":"Young's modulus is defined for which type of deformation?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Shear"},{"label":"B","text":"Longitudinal strain"},{"label":"C","text":"Volume strain"},{"label":"D","text":"Torsion"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Y = stress/strain for longitudinal (tensile/compressive) deformation."},{"id":1283,"text":"A ball dropped from height h hits the ground with speed v. The value of v is proportional to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"h"},{"label":"B","text":"sqrth"},{"label":"C","text":"h^2"},{"label":"D","text":"1/h"}],"answer":1,"solution":"v = sqrt(2gh) -> v  sqrth."},{"id":1292,"text":"The temperature at which Celsius and Fahrenheit scales read the same is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0 degrees"},{"label":"B","text":"32 degrees"},{"label":"C","text":"-40 degrees"},{"label":"D","text":"100 degrees"}],"answer":2,"solution":"C/5 = (F-32)/9. Set C = F = x: x/5 = (x-32)/9 -> 9x = 5x-160 -> x = -40."},{"id":1293,"text":"Transformer works on the principle of:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Self induction"},{"label":"B","text":"Mutual induction"},{"label":"C","text":"Electromagnetic induction"},{"label":"D","text":"Both Band C"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Transformer works on electromagnetic induction via mutual induction between coils."},{"id":1297,"text":"A car accelerates uniformly from rest to 20 m/s in 10 s. The acceleration is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 m/s^2"},{"label":"B","text":"2 m/s^2"},{"label":"C","text":"0.5 m/s^2"},{"label":"D","text":"4 m/s^2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"a = (v-u)/t = (20-0)/10 = 2 m/s^2."},{"id":1299,"text":"A ray of light passes from air into a medium with refractive index 1.5. If the angle of incidence is 30 degrees, what is the angle of refraction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 degrees"},{"label":"B","text":"30 degrees"},{"label":"C","text":"40 degrees"},{"label":"D","text":"50 degrees"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Using Snell's law, n1 sin(theta1) = n2 sin(theta2), we can find the angle of refraction. For air, n1 = 1, and for the medium, n2 = 1.5. Given theta1 = 30 degrees, we have 1*sin(30) = 1.5*sin(theta2), which gives theta2 = 20 degrees."},{"id":1303,"text":"The equivalent resistance of three 6  resistors connected in parallel is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"18"},{"label":"B","text":"6"},{"label":"C","text":"2"},{"label":"D","text":"3"}],"answer":2,"solution":"1/Rp = 1/6 + 1/6 + 1/6 = 3/6 = 1/2 -> Rp = 2 ."},{"id":1320,"text":"In SHM, the acceleration is maximum at:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean position"},{"label":"B","text":"Equilibrium position"},{"label":"C","text":"Extreme position"},{"label":"D","text":"Same everywhere"}],"answer":2,"solution":"a = -^2x. Acceleration is maximum at extreme positions (x = A) and zero at mean position."},{"id":1322,"text":"A body is projected vertically upward with velocity 20 m/s. Maximum height reached is (g=10 m/s^2):","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 m"},{"label":"B","text":"20 m"},{"label":"C","text":"40 m"},{"label":"D","text":"5 m"}],"answer":1,"solution":"H = u^2/2g = 400/20 = 20 m."},{"id":1323,"text":"The Doppler effect in sound is observed when there is relative motion between source and:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Observer only"},{"label":"B","text":"Medium only"},{"label":"C","text":"Observer or source"},{"label":"D","text":"Neither"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Doppler effect occurs when source or observer is in motion relative to each other."},{"id":1327,"text":"A wire of resistance R is stretched to double its length. New resistance will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R/2"},{"label":"B","text":"R"},{"label":"C","text":"4R"},{"label":"D","text":"2R"}],"answer":2,"solution":"When length is doubled (L' = 2L), cross-sectional area halves (A' = A/2). R' = L'/A' = (2L)/(A/2) = 4L/A = 4R."},{"id":1331,"text":"The speed of sound is maximum in:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Air"},{"label":"B","text":"Water"},{"label":"C","text":"Steel"},{"label":"D","text":"Vacuum"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Sound travels fastest in solids (steel ~5000 m/s) due to close molecular arrangement."},{"id":1333,"text":"The de Broglie wavelength of an electron moving with velocity v is . If velocity is doubled, wavelength becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2"},{"label":"B","text":"/2"},{"label":"C","text":"4"},{"label":"D","text":"/4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"= h/p = h/(mv). If v doubles, ' = h/(m.2v) = /2."},{"id":1334,"text":"If the distance between two charges is halved, the force between them becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Half"},{"label":"B","text":"Double"},{"label":"C","text":"Four times"},{"label":"D","text":"Same"}],"answer":2,"solution":"F  1/r^2. If r -> r/2, F -> 4F."},{"id":1335,"text":"A block of mass 2 kg is moving with a velocity of 3 m/s on a frictionless surface. What is the kinetic energy of the block?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"9 J"},{"label":"B","text":"6 J"},{"label":"C","text":"12 J"},{"label":"D","text":"4 J"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Kinetic energy = (1/2)mv^2 = (1/2)*2*3^2 = 9 J."},{"id":1339,"text":"The SI unit of magnetic flux is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tesla"},{"label":"B","text":"Gauss"},{"label":"C","text":"Weber"},{"label":"D","text":"Henry"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Magnetic flux is measured in Webers (Wb)."},{"id":1344,"text":"A body of mass 1 kg has weight approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1 N"},{"label":"B","text":"10 N"},{"label":"C","text":"100 N"},{"label":"D","text":"0.1 N"}],"answer":1,"solution":"W = mg = 1 x 10 = 10 N (approx)."},{"id":1346,"text":"A thermodynamic system undergoes a process where the internal energy change is 100 J and the heat added to the system is 200 J. What is the work done by the system?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-100 J"},{"label":"B","text":"100 J"},{"label":"C","text":"200 J"},{"label":"D","text":"300 J"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The first law of thermodynamics states that ΔU = Q - W, where ΔU is the internal energy change, Q is the heat added, and W is the work done. Rearranging the equation to solve for W, we get W = Q - ΔU = 200 J - 100 J = 100 J. However, the work done 'by' the system is in the opposite direction, so the work done by the system is -100 J."},{"id":1347,"text":"The efficiency of a Carnot engine operating between 500 K and 300 K is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"60%"},{"label":"B","text":"40%"},{"label":"C","text":"50%"},{"label":"D","text":"30%"}],"answer":1,"solution":"= 1 - T/T = 1 - 300/500 = 0.4 = 40%."},{"id":1351,"text":"First law of thermodynamics is based on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Conservation of momentum"},{"label":"B","text":"Conservation of energy"},{"label":"C","text":"Conservation of mass"},{"label":"D","text":"Conservation of charge"}],"answer":1,"solution":"U = Q - W. First law is the law of conservation of energy for thermodynamic systems."},{"id":1353,"text":"A Carnot engine operates between two reservoirs at temperatures 500 K and 300 K. What is the efficiency of the engine?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.2"},{"label":"B","text":"0.4"},{"label":"C","text":"0.6"},{"label":"D","text":"0.8"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The efficiency of a Carnot engine is given by the equation eta = 1 - (T2/T1), where T1 and T2 are the temperatures of the two reservoirs. For the given temperatures, eta = 1 - (300/500) = 1 - 0.6 = 0.4."},{"id":1359,"text":"The momentum of a photon of energy E is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"E/c^2"},{"label":"B","text":"E/c"},{"label":"C","text":"Ec"},{"label":"D","text":"hE/c"}],"answer":1,"solution":"p = E/c for a photon (since E = pc)."},{"id":1361,"text":"A block of mass 5 kg is moving with a velocity of 2 m/s. What is the magnitude of its momentum?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5 kg m/s"},{"label":"B","text":"10 kg m/s"},{"label":"C","text":"15 kg m/s"},{"label":"D","text":"20 kg m/s"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The momentum of an object is given by the product of its mass and velocity. So, the magnitude of the momentum is p = mv = 5 kg * 2 m/s = 10 kg m/s."},{"id":1365,"text":"An electron in the ground state of a hydrogen atom has an energy of -13.6 eV. What is the energy of the electron in the first excited state?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"-3.4 eV"},{"label":"B","text":"-6.8 eV"},{"label":"C","text":"-10.2 eV"},{"label":"D","text":"-13.6 eV"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The energy of the electron in the nth state of a hydrogen atom is given by En = -13.6 eV / n^2. For the first excited state, n = 2. So, E2 = -13.6 eV / 2^2 = -13.6 eV / 4 = -3.4 eV."},{"id":1367,"text":"A convex lens has focal length 20 cm. At what distance from the lens should an object be placed to get a real image twice the size of object?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"40 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"30 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"20 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"60 cm"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Magnification m = -2 (real image). m = v/u = -2 -> v = -2u. Lens formula: 1/f = 1/v - 1/u. 1/20 = 1/(-2u) - 1/u = -3/(2u). u = -30 cm. So object distance = 30 cm."},{"id":1369,"text":"The phenomenon of splitting of white light into its components is called:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reflection"},{"label":"B","text":"Refraction"},{"label":"C","text":"Dispersion"},{"label":"D","text":"Diffraction"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Dispersion is the splitting of white light into its constituent colors (VIBGYOR)."},{"id":1375,"text":"A block of mass 2 kg is placed on a horizontal surface. The coefficient of kinetic friction between the block and the surface is 0.5. If a horizontal force of 3 N is applied on the block, what is the acceleration of the block?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"0.5 m/s^2"},{"label":"B","text":"1 m/s^2"},{"label":"C","text":"1.5 m/s^2"},{"label":"D","text":"2 m/s^2"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The force of kinetic friction is given by F = μN = μmg = 0.5 * 2 * 9.8 = 9.8 N. The net force acting on the block is F_net = 3 N - 9.8 N (friction) = -6.8 N (in the opposite direction). Therefore, the acceleration is given by a = F_net / m = -6.8 / 2 = -3.4 m/s^2. However, the applied force is not enough to move the block, so we consider the force of static friction instead, which is less than or equal to the kinetic friction. Since the applied force is less than the maximum static friction (which is also 9.8 N), the block does not move. But if we were to consider the case where the block does move (for example, with a greater applied force), we would use the kinetic friction value to calculate the acceleration, which in this case is not the correct scenario."},{"id":1376,"text":"A ray of light passes from air into a glass slab with a refractive index of 1.5. If the angle of incidence is 30 degrees, what is the angle of refraction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"19.5 degrees"},{"label":"B","text":"20 degrees"},{"label":"C","text":"45 degrees"},{"label":"D","text":"60 degrees"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Using Snell's law, n1 sin(θ1) = n2 sin(θ2), where n1 = 1 (air) and n2 = 1.5 (glass). Rearranging the equation to solve for θ2, we get sin(θ2) = (n1 / n2) * sin(θ1) = (1 / 1.5) * sin(30) = (2/3) * 0.5 = 1/3. Then θ2 = arcsin(1/3) ≈ 19.5 degrees."},{"id":1379,"text":"Two resistors 4  and 6  are connected in series. The total resistance is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2.4"},{"label":"B","text":"10"},{"label":"C","text":"24"},{"label":"D","text":"4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"R = 4 + 6 = 10 ."},{"id":1461,"text":"A uniform disc of mass Mand radius R has a circular hole of radius R/2 cut out from it, touching the disc's circumference. The moment of inertia of the remaining part about an axis passing through the centre of the original disc and perpendicular to its plane is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"MR²/2"},{"label":"B","text":"13MR²/32"},{"label":"C","text":"3MR²/8"},{"label":"D","text":"MR²/4"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Mass of removed part m = M/4 (area ratio 1:4). Its Mia bout disc centre via parallel axis: I_rem = m[(R/2)²/2 + (R/2)²] = (M/4)(3R²/8) = 3MR²/32. Subtract from full disc MI (MR²/2 = 16MR²/32) to get 13MR²/32. Full: I_disc = ½ MR². Mass of cut portion m = M × (π(R/2)²)/(πR²) = M/4. MI of cut portion about its own centre = ½ m(R/2)² = MR²/32. Distance between axes = R/2. By parallel axis theorem, MI of cut portion about disc centre = MR²/32 + m(R/2)² = 3MR²/32. Required MI = ½ MR² - 3MR²/32 = 13MR²/32."},{"id":1462,"text":"Four point charges +q, +q, -q, -q are placed at the corners A, B, C, D of a square of side 'a' in clockwise order. The electric potential at the centre O of the square is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zero"},{"label":"B","text":"2kq/a"},{"label":"C","text":"4kq/a"},{"label":"D","text":"kq/a"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Potential is a scalar quantity. Net charge sum = +q + q - q - q = 0. Since distance from centre to each corner is same (a/√2), V_net = k(Σq)/r = 0. Full: Distance from centre to corner r = a/√2. Potential due to +q charges = 2kq/r. Potential due to -q charges = -2kq/r. Total V = 2kq/r - 2kq/r = 0."},{"id":1463,"text":"Two long parallel wires separated by distance 'd' carry currents I₁ and I₂ in the same direction. The force per unit length on wire 2 due to wire 1 is F. If both currents are doubled and the separation is halved, the new force per unit length becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2F"},{"label":"B","text":"4F"},{"label":"C","text":"8F"},{"label":"D","text":"16F"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Force per unit length f ∝ I₁I₂/d. New currents 2I₁, 2I₂ (factor 4). New distance d/2 (factor 2). Total factor = 4 × 2 = 8. So F' = 8F. Full: F = μ₀ I₁ I₂ / (2π d). F' = μ₀ (2I₁)(2I₂) / (2π (d/2)) = 8 μ₀ I₁ I₂ / (2π d) = 8F."},{"id":1464,"text":"In a Young's double slit experiment, the fringe width is β when performed in air. The entire apparatus is immersed in a liquid of refractive index 4/3. The new fringe width becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"3β/4"},{"label":"B","text":"4β/3"},{"label":"C","text":"β"},{"label":"D","text":"β/2"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Fringe width β = λD/d. In medium, wavelength λ' = λ/μ. Since D and d unchanged, β' = β/μ = β/(4/3) = 3β/4. Full: β_air = λD/d. In liquid, λ_liquid = λ_air / μ. New fringe width β_liquid = λ_liquid D/d = (λ_air/μ) D/d = β_air / μ = β / (4/3) = 3β/4."},{"id":1465,"text":"An electron and a proton are accelerated from rest through the same potential difference V. The ratio of their de Broglie wavelengths (λe / λp) is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1"},{"label":"B","text":"√(m_p/m_e)"},{"label":"C","text":"√(m_e/m_p)"},{"label":"D","text":"m_p/m_e"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Kinetic energy K = qV (same for both). Momentum p = √(2mK). de Broglie wavelength λ = h/p ∝ 1/√m. Ratio λe/λp = √(m_p/m_e). Full: For electron, λe = h/√(2m_e eV). For proton, λp = h/√(2m_p eV). Ratio = √(m_p/m_e). Since m_p ≈ 1836 m_e, ratio ≈ 43."},{"id":1512,"text":"The threshold wavelength for photoelectric emission from a metal surface is λ₀. Light of wavelength λ₀/2 is incident on the surface. The maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons is: (Take work function = φ)","options":[{"label":"A","text":"φ"},{"label":"B","text":"2φ"},{"label":"C","text":"φ/2"},{"label":"D","text":"3φ/2"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Work function φ = hc/λ₀. Incident photon energy E = hc/(λ₀/2) = 2hc/λ₀ = 2φ. By Einstein's photoelectric equation: K_max = E - φ = 2φ - φ = φ. Full Solution: Threshold wavelength λ₀ implies hc/λ₀ = φ. Incident wavelength λ = λ₀/2. Energy of incident photon = hc/λ = hc/(λ₀/2) = 2hc/λ₀ = 2φ. Maximum kinetic energy K_max = Photon Energy - Work Function = 2φ - φ = φ."},{"id":1513,"text":"A satellite is revolving around the Earth in a circular orbit of radius R with orbital speed v. If the radius of the orbit is increased to 4R by firing rockets, the new orbital speed of the satellite will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"v/4"},{"label":"B","text":"v/2"},{"label":"C","text":"v/√2"},{"label":"D","text":"2v"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For a circular orbit, orbital speed v = √(GM/r). Speed is inversely proportional to the square root of radius (v ∝ 1/√r). If radius becomes 4 times (r₂ = 4r₁), speed becomes 1/√4 = 1/2 times the original. New speed = v/2. Full Solution: Centripetal force is provided by gravity: mv²/r = GMm/r² ⇒ v = √(GM/r). For initial orbit: v₁ = √(GM/R) = v. For final orbit: v₂ = √(GM/4R) = (1/2)√(GM/R) = v/2."},{"id":1524,"text":"Two capacitors of capacitances 2 μFand 4 μF are charged to 10 Vand 20 V respectively. They are then connected in parallel with plates of like polarity joined together. The heat produced (in μJ) during the redistribution of charge is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"66.7"},{"label":"B","text":"33.3"},{"label":"C","text":"100"},{"label":"D","text":"133.3"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Use the direct formula for energy loss when two capacitors are connected in parallel: ΔU = ½ × (C₁C₂/(C₁+C₂)) × (V₁−V₂)². Plugging C₁=2, C₂=4, V₁=10, V₂=20 gives ΔU = ½ × (8/6) × 100 = 200/3 ≈ 66.7 μJ. Full Solution: Initial charges Q₁=20 μC, Q₂=80 μC. Total charge = 100 μC. Equivalent capacitance = 6 μF. Common voltage V = 100/6 = 50/3 V. Initial energy = ½×2×10² + ½×4×20² = 100 + 800 = 900 μJ. Final energy = ½×6×(50/3)² = 2500/3 ≈ 833.3 μJ. Heat loss = 900 − 833.3 = 66.7 μJ."},{"id":1525,"text":"A block of mass m is attached to two identical springs of spring constant k each, arranged in series. The block is displaced slightly and released. The time period of oscillation is T. If the same springs are rearranged in parallel with the same mass attached, the new time period of oscillation becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T/2"},{"label":"B","text":"T/√2"},{"label":"C","text":"T√2"},{"label":"D","text":"2T"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: For two identical springs, the effective spring constant in series is k/2 and in parallel is 2k. Since time period T ∝ 1/√k, the ratio T_parallel/T_series = √(k_series/k_parallel) = √((k/2)/(2k)) = √(1/4) = 1/2. Hence T_parallel = T/2. Full solution: Series combination gives 1/k_eq = 1/k + 1/k = 2/k ⇒ k_eq = k/2. Then T = 2π√(m/(k/2)) = 2π√(2m/k). Parallel combination gives k_eq = k + k = 2k. Then T' = 2π√(m/(2k)) = 2π√(m/(2k)) = (1/2)·2π√(2m/k) = T/2."},{"id":1531,"text":"A Carnot engine operates between a hot reservoir at 500 K and a cold reservoir at 300 K. If the engine absorbs 1000 J of heat from the hot reservoir in each cycle, the work done per cycle is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"200 J"},{"label":"B","text":"400 J"},{"label":"C","text":"600 J"},{"label":"D","text":"800 J"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Carnot efficiency η = 1 - Tc/Th = 1 - 300/500 = 0.4. Work = η × Qh = 0.4 × 1000 J = 400 J. Full solution: For a Carnot engine, η = 1 - T2/T1 = 1 - 300/500 = 0.4. The work output per cycle is W = η Q1 = 0.4 × 1000 J = 400 J."},{"id":1532,"text":"A diffraction grating has 5000 lines per centimetre. Light of wavelength 600 nm is incident normally. The angle of the first-order principal maximum is approximately:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"17.5°"},{"label":"B","text":"30°"},{"label":"C","text":"45°"},{"label":"D","text":"60°"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Grating spacing d = 1/(5000 cm⁻¹) = 2×10⁻⁶ m. For first order, sinθ = λ/d = 600×10⁻⁹ / 2×10⁻⁶ = 0.3 → θ ≈ 17.5°. Full solution: Using the grating equation d sinθ = mλ with m=1, d = 1/(5×10⁵ m⁻¹) = 2×10⁻⁶ m, λ = 600×10⁻⁹ m. sinθ = (1×600×10⁻⁹)/(2×10⁻⁶) = 0.3, giving θ = sin⁻¹(0.3) ≈ 17.5°."},{"id":1533,"text":"An electric dipole of moment 2 × 10⁻²⁹ C·m is placed in vacuum. The magnitude of the electric field at a point on the axial line at a distance 0.1 m from the centre of the dipole is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.8 × 10⁻¹⁶ N/C"},{"label":"B","text":"7.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ N/C"},{"label":"C","text":"3.6 × 10⁻¹⁶ N/C"},{"label":"D","text":"9.0 × 10⁻¹⁶ N/C"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: On the axial line, E = (1/4πϵ₀)(2p/r³). 1/4πϵ₀ ≈ 9×10⁹, 2p = 4×10⁻²⁹, r³ = (0.1)³ = 10⁻³. E ≈ 9×10⁹ × 4×10⁻²⁹ / 10⁻³ = 3.6×10⁻¹⁶ N/C. Full solution: Using the formula for the electric field of a dipole on its axis, E = (1/4πϵ₀) * (2p/r³). Substituting p = 2×10⁻²⁹ C·m, r = 0.1 m, and 1/4πϵ₀ = 9×10⁹ N·m²/C² gives E = 9×10⁹ × (2×2×10⁻²⁹) / (0.1)³ = 9×10⁹ × 4×10⁻²⁹ / 10⁻³ = 3.6×10⁻¹⁶ N/C."},{"id":1535,"text":"A series LCR circuit is connected to an AC source. At resonance, the voltage across the inductor is 80 Vand the voltage across the capacitor is 80 V. The resistance of the circuit is 10 Ω and the current flowing at resonance is 2 A. The RMS voltage of the AC source is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20 V"},{"label":"B","text":"80 V"},{"label":"C","text":"100 V"},{"label":"D","text":"160 V"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: At resonance, inductive reactance equals capacitive reactance (X_L = X_C). The voltages across Land Care equal in magnitude but 180° out of phase, cancelling each other. The circuit behaves as purely resistive. Source voltage V_S = V_R = I × R = 2 A × 10 Ω = 20 V. Full Solution: At resonance, impedance Z = R. The voltage across the resistor is V_R = I R = 2 × 10 = 20 V. The voltages across the inductor and capacitor are V_L = I X_L = 80 Vand V_C = I X_C = 80 V. Since V_Land V_Care in phase opposition, their phasor sum is zero. The source voltage is the phasor sum of V_R, V_L, and V_C: V_S = √[V_R² + (V_L - V_C)²] = √[20² + 0²] = 20 V."},{"id":1546,"text":"A block of mass 2 kg rests on a rough horizontal surface. A horizontal force of 10 N is applied. The coefficient of static friction μs is 0.3. Using the quick check that maximum static friction = μs·mg, will the block move?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yes, because applied force > maximum static friction"},{"label":"B","text":"No, because applied force < maximum static friction"},{"label":"C","text":"Yes, because kinetic friction is lower"},{"label":"D","text":"No, because the surface is frictionless"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Compute max static friction = μs·mg = 0.3·2·10 = 6 N. Since applied 10 N > 6 N, block will start moving. Full steps: f_max = μs N = μs mg = 0.3×2×9.8 ≈ 5.88 N (≈6 N). 10 N > 5.88 N, so motion occurs. Hence option A is correct."},{"id":1548,"text":"A uniform disc of mass Mand radius R has a concentric circular hole of radius R/2 cut out from it. The moment of inertia of the remaining part about an axis perpendicular to its plane and passing through its centre is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"(1/2) MR^2"},{"label":"B","text":"(3/8) MR^2"},{"label":"C","text":"(15/32) MR^2"},{"label":"D","text":"(7/16) MR^2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: The removed disc has mass M/4 (area ratio) and MI = 1/2*(M/4)*(R/2)^2 = MR^2/32. Full disc MI = 1/2 MR^2 = 16/32 MR^2. Subtracting gives 15/32 MR^2. Full solution: MI of full disc = ½MR². Mass of hole = M*(π(R/2)²)/(πR²) = M/4. MI of hole about same axis = ½*(M/4)*(R/2)² = MR²/32. MI remaining = ½MR² - MR²/32 = (16/32 - 1/32)MR² = 15/32 MR²."},{"id":1549,"text":"One mole of a monoatomic ideal gas (γ = 5/3) expands adiabatically from volume V to 2V. If the initial temperature is T0, the final temperature is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T0 / 2^(2/3)"},{"label":"B","text":"T0 / 2^(3/2)"},{"label":"C","text":"T0 / 2^(1/3)"},{"label":"D","text":"T0 / 2^(5/3)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: For adiabatic process TV^(γ-1)=constant. Here γ-1 = 2/3, so T_f = T0 (V_i/V_f)^(2/3) = T0/2^(2/3). Full solution: Adiabatic relation T V^(γ-1) = constant. With γ = 5/3, γ-1 = 2/3. Initial state: T0 V^(2/3). Final state: T_f (2V)^(2/3). Equating gives T_f = T0 (V/(2V))^(2/3) = T0/2^(2/3)."},{"id":1550,"text":"The de Broglie wavelength of a particle of mass m moving with kinetic energy K is λ. If the kinetic energy is increased to 4K, the new wavelength becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"λ/4"},{"label":"B","text":"λ/2"},{"label":"C","text":"λ"},{"label":"D","text":"2λ"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: λ = h/√(2mK) ∝ 1/√K. Quadrupling K makes λ half. Full solution: de Broglie wavelength λ = h/p, with p = √(2mK). So λ = h/√(2mK). If K → 4K, new λ' = h/√(2m·4K) = h/(2√(2mK)) = λ/2."},{"id":1562,"text":"A point charge q is placed at one corner of a cube of side a. The electric flux through each of the three faces meeting at that corner is zero. What is the electric flux through each of the remaining three faces of the cube?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"q/(24ε₀)"},{"label":"B","text":"q/(8ε₀)"},{"label":"C","text":"q/(6ε₀)"},{"label":"D","text":"q/(3ε₀)"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Enclose the charge symmetrically by imagining 8 such cubes forming a larger cube with the charge at the centre. Total flux = q/ε₀. Flux through one small cube = q/(8ε₀). The 3 faces touching the charge have zero flux (field lines parallel). The remaining flux q/(8ε₀) is shared equally by the 3 opposite faces. Flux per face = q/(24ε₀). Full Solution: By Gauss's law, total flux from charge q is q/ε₀. Placing the charge at the corner of a cube means it is shared by 8 identical cubes meeting at that corner. Flux entering the given cube = q/(8ε₀). The electric field is radial; hence it is parallel to the three faces meeting at the corner, giving zero flux through them. The entire flux q/(8ε₀) must exit through the three opposite faces. By symmetry, each of these three faces gets one-third: (1/3) × q/(8ε₀) = q/(24ε₀)."},{"id":1563,"text":"Two long straight parallel wires separated by a distance d carry currents I and 2I respectively in the same direction. The magnetic force per unit length experienced by the wire carrying current I is F. If the current in the first wire is doubled and the separation between the wires is halved, the new force per unit length on the first wire becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"2F"},{"label":"B","text":"4F"},{"label":"C","text":"8F"},{"label":"D","text":"16F"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Force per unit length f ∝ (I₁ I₂)/d. Initially f = F ∝ (I × 2I)/d = 2I²/d. New: I₁' = 2I, I₂' = 2I (unchanged), d' = d/2. New force f' ∝ (2I × 2I)/(d/2) = 4I² × (2/d) = 8I²/d. Ratio f'/F = (8I²/d) / (2I²/d) = 4. Answer is 4F. Full Solution: The force per unit length between two parallel currents is given by f = (μ₀/2π) (I₁ I₂/d). Initial force F = (μ₀/2π) (I × 2I)/d = (μ₀/2π) (2I²/d). After changes: I₁ = 2I, I₂ = 2I, d = d/2. New force F' = (μ₀/2π) (2I × 2I)/(d/2) = (μ₀/2π) (4I² × 2/d) = (μ₀/2π) (8I²/d) = 4 × [(μ₀/2π) (2I²/d)] = 4F."},{"id":1564,"text":"In a Young's double slit experiment using light of wavelength λ, the central bright fringe is observed at point O on the screen. A thin transparent sheet of refractive index μ = 1.5 and thickness t is introduced in front of the upper slit. Consequently, the central maximum shifts to a position originally occupied by the 5tho rder bright fringe. The thickness t of the sheet in terms of λ is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"5λ"},{"label":"B","text":"20λ"},{"label":"C","text":"10λ"},{"label":"D","text":"15λ"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Shift of central maximum by n fringes implies path difference introduced = nλ. Path difference by sheet = (μ - 1)t. Equate: (μ - 1)t = nλ. Here n=5, μ=1.5. (0.5)t = 5λ ⇒ t = 10λ. Full Solution: Introduction of a sheet of thickness t and refractive index μ in front of a slit increases the optical path by (μ - 1)t. For the central maximum to shift to the position of the 5th bright fringe, the path difference must equal 5λ. Thus, (μ - 1)t = 5λ. Substituting μ = 1.5 gives (1.5 - 1)t = 5λ ⇒ 0.5t = 5λ ⇒ t = 10λ."},{"id":1565,"text":"A metallic rod of length L rotates with a constant angular velocity ω in a uniform magnetic field B perpendicular to the plane of rotation, about an axis passing through one end. The emf induced between the ends of the rod is ε. The rod is replaced by another rod of length 2L made of the same material, rotating at angular velocity ω/2 in the same magnetic field. The new induced emf is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"ε"},{"label":"B","text":"ε/2"},{"label":"C","text":"4ε"},{"label":"D","text":"2ε"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Motional emf in a rotating rod ε = (1/2) B ω L². ε ∝ ω L². New ω' = ω/2 (factor 1/2). New L' = 2L (factor 4). Net factor = (1/2) × 4 = 2. New emf = 2ε. Full Solution: For a rod rotating about one end in a perpendicular magnetic field, the emf induced between the ends is derived by integrating the motional emf of elements: ε = ∫₀ᴸ B ω r dr = (1/2) B ω L². For the new rod, L' = 2Land ω' = ω/2. New emf ε' = (1/2) B (ω/2) (2L)² = (1/2) B (ω/2) (4L²) = (1/2) B ω L² × 2 = 2ε."},{"id":1576,"text":"Two identical balls A and Bare moving along the same straight line with velocities +v and -2v respectively. They undergo a perfectly elastic head-on collision. The velocities of A and B after the collision are:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+2v, -v"},{"label":"B","text":"+v, -2v"},{"label":"C","text":"-2v, +v"},{"label":"D","text":"-v, +2v"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: In a 1D elastic collision between equal masses, velocities are exchanged. Worked: Let u₁ = +v, u₂ = -2v. Conservation of momentum: v₁ + v₂ = -v. Coefficient of restitution e=1: v₂ - v₁ = u₁ - u₂ = 3v. Solving gives v₁ = -2v, v₂ = +v."},{"id":1577,"text":"Two thin lenses of focal lengths +20 cm and -30 cm are placed in contact. The equivalent focal length of the combination is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"+60 cm"},{"label":"B","text":"-60 cm"},{"label":"C","text":"+12 cm"},{"label":"D","text":"-12 cm"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: Use power addition in Dioptres. P = P1 + P2 = (100/20) + (100/-30) = 5 - 3.33 = +1.67 D. Equivalent focal length f = 100/P = 100/1.67 ≈ +60 cm. Full Solution: For thin lenses in contact, 1/F = 1/f1 + 1/f2. Substituting f1 = +20 cm, f2 = -30 cm: 1/F = 1/20 - 1/30 = (3-2)/60 = 1/60. Thus F = +60 cm. The positive sign indicates a converging combination."},{"id":1578,"text":"In a potentiometer experiment, a cell of emf 1.5 V gives a balancing length of 60 cm. If this cell is replaced by another cell of emf 2.0 V, the new balancing length (in cm) will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"45"},{"label":"B","text":"80"},{"label":"C","text":"90"},{"label":"D","text":"75"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Balancing length L is directly proportional to emf E (L ∝ E) for a constant potential gradient. L2 = L1 × (E2/E1) = 60 × (2.0/1.5) = 60 × (4/3) = 80 cm. Full Solution: Potential gradient k = E1/L1 = 1.5/60 = 0.025 V/cm. For the second cell, E2 = k × L2 ⇒ L2 = E2/k = 2.0 / 0.025 = 80 cm."},{"id":1580,"text":"Two long coaxial solenoids of same length l and cross-sectional area A have N1 and N2 turns respectively. The mutual inductance M between them is proportional to:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"N1 + N2"},{"label":"B","text":"N1 × N2"},{"label":"C","text":"(N1 × N2) / l"},{"label":"D","text":"N1 × N2 × A / l"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: Mutual inductance M = μ0 N1 N2 A / l for long coaxial solenoids. Thus M ∝ N1 N2 A / l. Option elimination: M must depend on geometry (A, l) and product of turns (N1 N2), not sum. Full Solution: Magnetic field due to inner solenoid B = μ0 n1 I1 = μ0 (N1/l) I1. Flux through each turn of outer solenoid Φ = B A = μ0 (N1/l) I1 A. Total flux linkage with outer solenoid = N2 Φ = μ0 N1 N2 A I1 / l. By definition M = (Flux linkage)/I1 = μ0 N1 N2 A / l."},{"id":1633,"text":"A projectile is launched with an initial speed u at an angle θ with the horizontal and attains a range R. If the launch angle is changed to (90°−θ) while keeping the speed same, the new range will be:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"R"},{"label":"B","text":"2R"},{"label":"C","text":"R/2"},{"label":"D","text":"0"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: For complementary angles θ and (90°−θ), sin(2θ) = sin(180°−2θ), so the range formula R = u² sin(2θ)/g gives the same range. Full solution: Using R = u² sin(2θ)/g, the new range R' = u² sin(2(90°−θ))/g = u² sin(180°−2θ)/g = u² sin(2θ)/g = R."},{"id":1634,"text":"A mass m attached to a spring of force constant k oscillates with a time period T. The mass is replaced by 4m and the spring constant is changed to k/2. The new time period of oscillation is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"T"},{"label":"B","text":"2T"},{"label":"C","text":"2√2 T"},{"label":"D","text":"4T"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Time period T ∝ √(m/k); the ratio T'/T = √((4m)/(k/2) ÷ (m/k)) = √8 = 2√2. Full solution: T = 2π√(m/k); new T' = 2π√(4m/(k/2)) = 2π√(8m/k) = √8 × 2π√(m/k) = 2√2 T."},{"id":1635,"text":"Two identical capacitors each of capacitance C are connected in series. This series combination is then connected in parallel with a third identical capacitor C. The equivalent capacitance of the entire arrangement is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"C/2"},{"label":"B","text":"3C/2"},{"label":"C","text":"2C"},{"label":"D","text":"5C/2"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: Two capacitors in series give C/2; adding a third in parallel adds C, yielding C/2 + C = 3C/2. Full solution: Series equivalent C_s = (C×C)/(C+C) = C/2. Parallel with C gives C_eq = C_s + C = C/2 + C = 3C/2."},{"id":1636,"text":"A radioactive sample has a half‑life of T. After a time interval of 3T, the activity of the sample becomes:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A₀/2"},{"label":"B","text":"A₀/4"},{"label":"C","text":"A₀/6"},{"label":"D","text":"A₀/8"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Shortcut: After n half‑lives activity reduces by (1/2)ⁿ; for n=3, factor = 1/8. Full solution: Activity A = A₀(1/2)^(t/T); with t=3T, A = A₀(1/2)³ = A₀/8."},{"id":1637,"text":"A Carnot engine has an efficiency of 40%. If the temperature of the cold reservoir is 300 K, the temperature of the hot reservoir is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"400 K"},{"label":"B","text":"450 K"},{"label":"C","text":"500 K"},{"label":"D","text":"600 K"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Efficiency η = 1 − T_c/T_h ⇒ T_h = T_c/(1−η) = 300/(0.6) = 500 K. Full solution: 0.40 = 1 − 300/T_h → 300/T_h = 0.60 → T_h = 300/0.60 = 500 K."}]},{"name":"Aptitude Test","count":50,"questions":[{"id":1382,"text":"In an argument, if the premises are true but the conclusion is false, what can be said about the validity of the argument?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The argument is valid"},{"label":"B","text":"The argument is invalid"},{"label":"C","text":"The argument is sound"},{"label":"D","text":"The argument is inductive"}],"answer":1,"solution":"An argument is defined as valid if and only if it is impossible for the premises to be true and the conclusion false. Since the scenario describes true premises leading to a false conclusion, the argument must be invalid by definition."},{"id":1383,"text":"If 'Some cats are dogs' is false, which of the following statements must necessarily be true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All cats are dogs"},{"label":"B","text":"No cats are dogs"},{"label":"C","text":"Some dogs are not cats"},{"label":"D","text":"All dogs are cats"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The statement 'Some cats are dogs' implies an intersection between the set of cats and the set of dogs. If this intersection is empty (making the statement false), then no member of the cat set belongs to the dog set. Therefore, the universal negative statement 'No cats are dogs' must be true."},{"id":1384,"text":"Which type of reasoning involves drawing a probable conclusion based on specific observations rather than guaranteeing the truth of the conclusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Deductive Reasoning"},{"label":"B","text":"Inductive Reasoning"},{"label":"C","text":"Abductive Reasoning"},{"label":"D","text":"Analogical Reasoning"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Inductive reasoning moves from specific observations to broader generalizations and is characterized by conclusions that are probable but not certain. Deductive reasoning guarantees the conclusion if premises are true, while abductive reasoning seeks the best explanation."},{"id":1385,"text":"In a debate about climate change, Speaker A says: 'The government should ban fossil fuels because they cause pollution.' Speaker B replies: 'You are an environmentalist with a bias against industry.' This is an example of which fallacy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ad Hominem"},{"label":"B","text":"Straw Man"},{"label":"C","text":"Red Herring"},{"label":"D","text":"Slippery Slope"}],"answer":0,"solution":"An Ad Hominem fallacy occurs when one attacks the person making the argument (Speaker Battack ing Speaker A'sid entity as an 'environmentalist') rather than addressing the substance of the argument (the link between fossil fuels and pollution)."},{"id":1386,"text":"If every integer is either even or odd, and 10 is an integer, then:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"10 is neither even nor odd"},{"label":"B","text":"10 must be prime"},{"label":"C","text":"10 is either even or odd"},{"label":"D","text":"10 cannot be divisible by 2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The statement establishes a universal property for the set of integers: every element belongs to one of two disjoint subsets (even or odd). Since 10 is an integer, it must satisfy this condition. Therefore, 10 is either even or odd."},{"id":1387,"text":"Which of the following best describes a 'Circular Argument'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Using irrelevant evidence to support a claim"},{"label":"B","text":"Assuming the truth of the conclusion within the premises"},{"label":"C","text":"Attacking the opponent's character instead of their argument"},{"label":"D","text":"Presenting two false choices"}],"answer":1,"solution":"A circular argument (petitio principii) occurs when the premise of an argument is merely a restatement of the conclusion, thus assuming what needs to be proven. The other options describe Ad Hominem, False Dilemma, and Red Herring fallacies respectively."},{"id":1388,"text":"In the syllogism: 'All squares are rectangles. Some rectangles are blue. Therefore, some squares are blue.' Is the conclusion valid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yes, it is valid"},{"label":"B","text":"No, it is invalid"},{"label":"C","text":"It depends on the color of the paper"},{"label":"D","text":"Only if some squares are not white"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The argument is invalid because the premises do not guarantee the conclusion. Just because some rectangles are blue does not mean those specific rectangles are squares. The intersection of 'squares' and 'blue things' cannot be deduced solely from the overlap between 'rectangles' and 'blue things'."},{"id":1389,"text":"What is the primary function of a counter-example in logical analysis?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To prove a universal statement true"},{"label":"B","text":"To disprove a universal statement"},{"label":"C","text":"To introduce ambiguity into an argument"},{"label":"D","text":"To support a premise with emotional appeal"}],"answer":1,"solution":"A single counter-example (an instance where the antecedent is true but the consequent is false, or a member of the subject set that fails to have the predicate) is sufficient to disprove a universal affirmative statement. It does not prove anything about other instances."},{"id":1390,"text":"If 'No mammals are fish' and 'All whales are mammals', then it logically follows that:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Some whales are fish"},{"label":"B","text":"No whales are fish"},{"label":"C","text":"Some fish are not mammals"},{"label":"D","text":"All fish are mammals"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Using syllogistic logic: If the set of mammals and the set of fish are disjoint (no overlap), and whales are entirely within the mammal set, then whales must also be entirely outside the fish set. Thus, no whales are fish."},{"id":1391,"text":"Which statement represents the logical negation of 'All students passed the exam'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No student passed the exam"},{"label":"B","text":"Some students did not pass the exam"},{"label":"C","text":"All students failed the exam"},{"label":"D","text":"Most students passed the exam"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The negation of a universal affirmative ('All S are P') is a particular negative ('Some S are not P'). Saying 'No student passed' is too strong (it denies even one passing), whereas only failing at least one student disproves the claim that *all* passed."},{"id":1392,"text":"In critical thinking, distinguishing between a 'fact' and an 'opinion' primarily relies on:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Whether the statement is supported by evidence and can be verified"},{"label":"B","text":"The tone of voice used when stating it"},{"label":"C","text":"The number of people who agree with it"},{"label":"D","text":"The complexity of the vocabulary used"}],"answer":0,"solution":"A fact is a statement that can be proven true or false through observation, measurement, or evidence. An opinion expresses a belief or feeling that cannot be objectively verified. The distinction rests on verifiability and evidentiary support."},{"id":1393,"text":"If the premises of an argument are true and the conclusion is also true, the argument is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Valid"},{"label":"B","text":"Invalid"},{"label":"C","text":"Sound"},{"label":"D","text":"Neither valid nor invalid"}],"answer":2,"solution":"While 'valid' means the conclusion follows logically from the premises regardless of their truth value, a 'sound' argument requires both validity AND the actual truth of the premises. Since the problem states premises are true and the structure leads to a true conclusion (implying validity), the argument meets all criteria for being sound."},{"id":1394,"text":"If the statement 'All roses are flowers' is true, which of the following statements must necessarily be false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Some roses are not flowers"},{"label":"B","text":"No roses are flowers"},{"label":"C","text":"Some flowers are roses"},{"label":"D","text":"All flowers are roses"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The statement 'All roses are flowers' implies that the set of roses is a subset of the set of flowers. Therefore, it is impossible for 'No roses are flowers' to be true simultaneously; if all roses are flowers, then none can exist outside that category. Options A contradicts the premise directly but 'No roses are flowers' is the strict negation making it false in this specific logical context where the universal affirmative holds."},{"id":1397,"text":"If 'No birds are mammals' is true, which of the following statements must also be true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Some birds are not mammals"},{"label":"B","text":"No mammals are birds"},{"label":"C","text":"All mammals are birds"},{"label":"D","text":"Some mammals are birds"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The statement 'No birds are mammals' establishes a complete disjoint between the two sets. By the law of conversion fore -propositions (universal negatives), if no A is B, then no B is A must also hold true. Options A, C, and D imply some intersection or subset relationship that contradicts the universal exclusion stated in the premise."},{"id":1399,"text":"If 'Some cats are black' is true, which of the following statements could be false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No cats are black"},{"label":"B","text":"All cats are black"},{"label":"C","text":"Some black things are not cats"},{"label":"D","text":"Some cats are not black"}],"answer":1,"solution":"If 'Some cats are black' is true, it implies that there is at least one black cat. Consequently, 'All cats are black' could be false because the existence of non-black cats would still allow the original statement to remain true while making this option false. Option A is directly contradicted by the premise, but 'could be false' usually asks for a possibility; however, in strict logic, if some are black, 'all are black' is not guaranteed and thus could be false."},{"id":1400,"text":"In a syllogism, if the major premise states 'All Mare P' and the minor premise states 'No S are M', what is the valid conclusion regarding the relationship between Sand P?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All S are P"},{"label":"B","text":"No S are P"},{"label":"C","text":"Some S are P"},{"label":"D","text":"No conclusion can be drawn"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The major premise establishes that the set M is entirely contained within set P. The minor premise states that set Sand set M have no intersection. Since Scan not touch M, and all of M is inside P, it logically follows that no part of Scan be inside P (assuming standard existential import where terms aren on-empty). Thus, 'No S are P' is the valid conclusion. Option A contradicts this exclusion. Option C implies an intersection that doesn'tex ist. Option D is incorrect because a definitive negative conclusion follows from these universal premises."},{"id":1401,"text":"In a debate, Person A states: 'All birds can fly.' Person B points out that penguins are birds but cannot fly. Which logical principle is being violated by Person A's initial statement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The Principle of Non-Contradiction"},{"label":"B","text":"Existential Import (or the need for counter-examples to refute universal claims)"},{"label":"C","text":"The Law of Identity"},{"label":"D","text":"Modus Ponens"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The statement 'All birds can fly' is false because a single counter-example (penguins) exists. This relates to Existential Import in the sense that a universal claim ('All') requires no exceptions; finding one exception falsifies it immediately. The other options are incorrect: Non-Contradiction deals with mutually exclusive properties, Identity deals with A=A, and Modus Ponens is a valid inference rule, not a principle of truth-value verification."},{"id":1402,"text":"If the statement 'Some integers are even' is true, which of the following statements must necessarily be false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All integers are odd"},{"label":"B","text":"No integers are even"},{"label":"C","text":"Some integers are not even"},{"label":"D","text":"At least one integer is even"}],"answer":0,"solution":"If 'Some integers are even' is true, then it is impossible for 'No integers are even' to be true (as that would contradict the existence of an even integer). Therefore, 'No integers are even' must be false. The other options ('All integers are odd', 'Some integers are not even') can coexist with the truth of the premise or are simply not necessarily false based solely on the existence of *some* even numbers."},{"id":1403,"text":"Which type of fallacy is committed when someone argues: 'You cannot trust his testimony about the accident because he was seen arguing with one of the drivers yesterday.'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ad Hominem Circumstantial"},{"label":"B","text":"Straw Man"},{"label":"C","text":"Red Herring"},{"label":"D","text":"Appeal to Authority"}],"answer":0,"solution":"This is an Ad Hominem fallacy (specifically circumstantial) because it attacks the person's character or circumstances (arguing with a driver) rather than addressing the actual evidence of what happened in the accident. Straw Man involves misrepresenting an argument, Red Herring introduces irrelevant topics, and Appeal to Authority relies on an expert opinion instead of reasoning."},{"id":1404,"text":"Consider the argument: 'If it rains, the ground gets wet. The ground is not wet. Therefore, it did not rain.' Is this argument valid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yes, it follows the form Modus Tollens"},{"label":"B","text":"No, because the ground could be wet for other reasons too"},{"label":"C","text":"Yes, but only if we assume no sprinklers were used"},{"label":"D","text":"No, because denying the antecedent leads to a false conclusion"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The argument is valid. It follows the logical structure of Modus Tollens: If P then Q; Not Q; therefore Not P. Since the ground is not wet (Not Q), and rain causes wetness (P->Q), it logically concludes that rain did not occur (Not P). The other options describe invalid reasoning or incorrect interpretations of the conditional relationship."},{"id":1405,"text":"In a logical system where 'A implies B' is true, if A is false, what can be determined about the truth value of B?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"B must be true"},{"label":"B","text":"B must be false"},{"label":"C","text":"B can be either true or false"},{"label":"D","text":"B cannot be determined without knowing C"}],"answer":2,"solution":"If 'A implies B' is true and A is false, B can be either true or false. In formal logic, a conditional statement with a false antecedent (A) is automatically considered true regardless of the consequent (B). Therefore, knowing A is false gives no information about the actual truth value of B. The other options incorrectly assert that B must have a specific fixed value."},{"id":1406,"text":"Which of the following statements is logically equivalent to 'No cats are dogs'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All cats are not dogs"},{"label":"B","text":"Some cats are not dogs"},{"label":"C","text":"All dogs are not cats"},{"label":"D","text":"Some dogs are not cats"}],"answer":0,"solution":"'No cats are dogs' means there is no overlap between the set of cats and the set of dogs. This is logically equivalent to 'All cats are not dogs' (meaning every cat falls outside the dog category). The other options ('Some...') represent weaker statements that allow for partial overlap, which contradicts the absolute exclusion in 'No...'."},{"id":1407,"text":"An argument states: 'Either the suspect is at home or he is at the park. He is not at the park. Therefore, he must be at home.' What logical law justifies this conclusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Law of Excluded Middle"},{"label":"B","text":"Law of Non-Contradiction"},{"label":"C","text":"Disjunctive Syllogism"},{"label":"D","text":"Constructive Dilemma"}],"answer":2,"solution":"This is a classic Disjunctive Syllogism: Given 'A or B' and 'Not B', we conclude 'A'. It relies on eliminating one option of a disjunction to affirm the other. The Law of Excluded Middle states A or Not-A, Non-Contradiction states not both A and B, and Constructive Dilemma involves two conditionals."},{"id":1408,"text":"If 'All roses are flowers' is true and 'Some flowers are red' is true, what can be concluded about roses?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All roses are red"},{"label":"B","text":"Some roses are red"},{"label":"C","text":"No roses are red"},{"label":"D","text":"Nothing definite can be concluded about the color of roses"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Nothing definite can be concluded. While all roses are in the set of flowers, and some flowers are red, we do not know if the specific subset of flowers that are red overlaps with the subset of roses. The 'some' in the second premise does not guarantee an intersection with 'roses'."},{"id":1410,"text":"In the statement 'If 2+2=4, then 4 is an even number', what is the truth value of the conditional if the hypothesis (2+2=4) is true and the conclusion (4 is even) is also true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"True"},{"label":"B","text":"False"},{"label":"C","text":"Undefined"},{"label":"D","text":"Dependent on the definition of even"}],"answer":0,"solution":"In classical logic, a conditional statement 'If P then Q' is True whenever both P and Q are true. Since 2+2=4 is true and 4 is even is true, the entire implication holds as True. The other options misunderstand the truth table for material implication where T->T results in T."},{"id":1411,"text":"What is the logical negation of the statement: 'For every student x, if x studies hard, then x passes'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"There exists a student who does not study hard and fails"},{"label":"B","text":"There exists a student who studies hard but does not pass"},{"label":"C","text":"All students study hard and all fail"},{"label":"D","text":"No student studies hard"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The negation of a universal quantifier ('For every') is an existential quantifier ('There exists'). The negation of 'If P then Q' is 'P and Not Q'. Thus, the negation is: There exists a student (x) such that x studies hard AND x does not pass. Option A keeps the implication structure which is incorrect for negation."},{"id":1412,"text":"Which scenario best illustrates an 'Appeal to Ignorance' fallacy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Claiming a ghost exists because no one has proven it doesn'tex ist"},{"label":"B","text":"Stating that water boils at 100C at sea level based on physics"},{"label":"C","text":"Arguing that a law is just because everyone voted for it"},{"label":"D","text":"Saying the earth is round because satellites have photographed it"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Appeal to Ignorance occurs when one argues that a proposition is true simply because it has not been proven false (or vice versa). Option A claims existence based on lack of disproof. The other options rely on empirical evidence, majority vote (Ad Populum), or scientific observation."},{"id":1438,"text":"Consider the statement: 'All squares are rectangles.' If this statement is true, which of the following statements must also be true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Some rectangles are not squares."},{"label":"B","text":"No squares are circles."},{"label":"C","text":"All rectangles are squares."},{"label":"D","text":"Some squares are rectangles."}],"answer":3,"solution":"The original statement establishes that the set of squares is a subset of the set of rectangles. Therefore, any element in the set of squares is also in the set of rectangles, making 'Some squares are rectangles' necessarily true. The other options are incorrect: 'Some rectangles are not squares' is likely true but not logically forced by the premise alone (though practically true), 'No squares are circles' is a separate fact not derived from the premise, and 'All rectangles are squares' is false as there aren on-square rectangles."},{"id":1439,"text":"In an argument, Person X says: 'Everyone in this room likes pizza; therefore, John must like pizza.' This reasoning assumes which of the following unstated premises?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"John is not in this room."},{"label":"B","text":"John is part of the group 'everyone in this room'."},{"label":"C","text":"Pizza is a popular food worldwide."},{"label":"D","text":"John has never visited a restaurant before."}],"answer":1,"solution":"The conclusion about John relies entirely on John being included in the universal set defined by the premise ('everyone in this room'). If John were not in the room, the premise would not apply to him. The other options are irrelevant to the logical connection between the general statement and the specific conclusion."},{"id":1440,"text":"Which of the following pairs represents a valid 'Disjunctive Syllogism'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"If it rains, the ground is wet. It is raining. So, the ground is wet."},{"label":"B","text":"Either the light is on or the switch is broken. The light is not on. Therefore, the switch is broken."},{"label":"C","text":"All dogs are mammals. This animal is a dog. Therefore, this animal is a mammal."},{"label":"D","text":"Some birds can fly. Penguins are birds. Therefore, penguins can fly."}],"answer":1,"solution":"A disjunctive syllogism has the form: 'Either P or Q; not P; therefore Q.' Option 1 is a Modus Ponens (conditional). Option 3 is a universal instantiation/syllogism. Option 4 commits a fallacy by ignoring exceptions (penguins cannot fly). Only Option 2 follows the strict disjunctive syllogism structure where one alternative is eliminated to affirm the other."},{"id":1441,"text":"If the statement 'No mammals are reptiles' is true, which of the following must be false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Some mammals are reptiles."},{"label":"B","text":"All reptiles are mammals."},{"label":"C","text":"Some reptiles are not mammals."},{"label":"D","text":"No reptiles are mammals."}],"answer":0,"solution":"The original statement asserts that the intersection of mammals and reptiles is empty. Therefore, claiming 'Some mammals are reptiles' directly contradicts this and must be false. Option 1 is a fallacy of equivocation or confusion. Options 2 and 3 can be true without contradicting the premise (if all reptiles aren on-mammals, then no mammal is a reptile)."},{"id":1442,"text":"Which of the following is a logical equivalent of the statement: 'It is not the case that all integers are positive'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No integers are positive."},{"label":"B","text":"Some integers are positive."},{"label":"C","text":"Some integers are not positive."},{"label":"D","text":"All integers are negative."}],"answer":2,"solution":"The negation of a universal affirmative ('All A are B') is a particular negative ('Some A are not B'). The statement 'It is not the case that all integers are positive' means there exists at least one integer that is not positive. This corresponds exactly to 'Some integers are not positive'. Option 0 is too strong (denies all), Option 1 affirms some are positive (which is true but doesn't capture the negation of 'all'), and Option 4 is factually false."},{"id":1443,"text":"In a debate, a speaker argues: 'You should support Plan A because Plan Ble ads to disaster.' What type of reasoning error might this represent if the link between Plan A and avoiding disaster is not established?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ad Hominem."},{"label":"B","text":"Appeal to Consequence."},{"label":"C","text":"False Dilemma."},{"label":"D","text":"Slippery Slope."}],"answer":1,"solution":"This argument attacks the alternative (Plan B) by highlighting its negative consequences (disaster) rather than addressing the merits of Plan A directly. This is an Appeal to Consequence (specifically Appeal to Negative Consequences or Fear Mongering). Ad Hominem attacks the person. False Dilemma presents only two options when more exist. Slippery Slope claims a chain reaction of events without evidence."},{"id":1444,"text":"If 'P implies Q' is true, and 'Q' is found to be false, what must be the truth value of 'P'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P must be true."},{"label":"B","text":"P must be false."},{"label":"C","text":"P can be either true or false."},{"label":"D","text":"P cannot be determined."}],"answer":1,"solution":"By Modus Tollens: If P -> Q is true and Q is false, then P must be false. If P were true, Q would have to be true (given the implication), which contradicts the fact that Q is false. Therefore, P must be false. Option 0 contradicts the implication. Option 2 is incorrect because if P were true, Q would be true, creating a contradiction."},{"id":1445,"text":"Which of the following statements correctly defines a 'Counterexample' in logic?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An example that supports the hypothesis."},{"label":"B","text":"An instance that disproves a universal claim."},{"label":"C","text":"A statistical average of a dataset."},{"label":"D","text":"A hypothetical scenario with no real-world basis."}],"answer":1,"solution":"A counterexample is a specific instance that contradicts a general statement (universal claim). If someone says 'All swans are white', finding one black swan provides a counterexample, disproving the universal claim. Option 0 supports, not disproves. Option 3 and 4 are unrelated to the definition of a logical counterexample."},{"id":1446,"text":"If the statement 'For every x, if x is a bird, then x can fly' is false, what must be true?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"No birds can fly."},{"label":"B","text":"All birds can fly."},{"label":"C","text":"Some birds cannot fly."},{"label":"D","text":"There are no birds."}],"answer":2,"solution":"The negation of a universal quantifier ('All') is an existential quantifier ('Some'). If 'All birds can fly' is false, it means there is at least one bird that does not satisfy the condition (cannot fly). Thus, 'Some birds cannot fly' must be true. Option 0 is too strong; Option 1 is the original statement; Option 4 is irrelevant to the truth value of the conditional."},{"id":1447,"text":"Which logical fallacy is committed when someone argues: 'If we allow this new policy, soon everyone will be unemployed and society will collapse'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ad Hominem."},{"label":"B","text":"Straw Man."},{"label":"C","text":"Slippery Slope."},{"label":"D","text":"Circular Reasoning."}],"answer":2,"solution":"The argument assumes that a small first step (allowing the policy) will inevitably lead to extreme negative outcomes (society collapse) without providing evidence for each intermediate step. This is the definition of a Slippery Slope fallacy. Ad Hominem attacks the person. Straw Man misrepresents an argument. Circular Reasoning repeats the conclusion as a premise."},{"id":1450,"text":"Consider the argument: 'All squares are rectangles. All rectangles have four sides. Therefore, all squares have four sides.' Which logical rule validates this deduction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Modus Ponens"},{"label":"B","text":"Hypothetical Syllogism (Transitive Property)"},{"label":"C","text":"Disjunctive Syllogism"},{"label":"D","text":"Affirming the Consequent"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The argument follows a chain of implication where if A implies Band B implies C, then A implies C. Here, 'Square' -> 'Rectangle' and 'Rectangle' -> 'Four Sides', so 'Square' -> 'Four Sides'. This is the definition of Hypothetical Syllogism or Transitive Property. Modus Ponens requires a conditional premise and an affirmation of the antecedent to conclude the consequent directly, which isn't the primary structure here. Disjunctive Syllogism deals with 'or' statements. Affirming the Consequent is a fallacy."},{"id":1452,"text":"Which of the following best describes an argument that commits the fallacy of 'Begging the Question'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"An argument where the conclusion is assumed within the premises without independent evidence"},{"label":"B","text":"An argument based on irrelevant emotional reasoning"},{"label":"C","text":"An argument that ignores contrary evidence to support a conclusion"},{"label":"D","text":"An argument that attacks the character of the opponent instead of their argument"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Begging the Question (Circular Reasoning) occurs when the premise assumes the truth of the conclusion, creating a loop. Option A describes this perfectly. Option B describes an Appeal to Emotion. Option Cde scribes Ignoring Evidence or Special Pleading. Option D describes Ad Hominem."},{"id":1453,"text":"If the conditional statement 'If P then Q' is true, and we know that Q is false, what can be logically concluded about P?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"P must be true"},{"label":"B","text":"P must be false"},{"label":"C","text":"P cannot be determined from the given information"},{"label":"D","text":"P must be true if and only if Q is true"}],"answer":1,"solution":"By Modus Tollens, if 'If P then Q' is true and Q is false, then P must be false. If P were true, Q would have to be true, which contradicts the given that Q is false. Therefore, P cannot be true; it must be false. Option A is incorrect because if P were true, Q would be true. Option C is incorrect because we can determine P's status. Option D describes a biconditional relationship, not implied by a simple conditional."},{"id":1454,"text":"Which of the following statements is logically equivalent to the negation of 'There exists an x such that x is both even and prime'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"For all x, if x is even then x is not prime"},{"label":"B","text":"For all x, x is either not even or not prime"},{"label":"C","text":"There exists an x such that x is not even and not prime"},{"label":"D","text":"There exists an x such that x is neither even nor prime"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The original statement is an existential quantification: ∃x (Even(x) ∧ Prime(x)). Its negation is ∀x ¬(Even(x) ∧ Prime(x)). By De Morgan's Law, ¬(A ∧ B) is equivalent to ¬A ∨ ¬B. Thus, the negation is 'For all x, not (Even or Prime)', which translates to 'For all x, x is either not even or not prime'. Option A uses implication which changes the meaning. Options Cand D use existential quantifiers instead of universal ones."},{"id":1455,"text":"An argument states: 'Either the project will succeed or it will fail. The project will not fail. Therefore, the project will succeed.' What logical law justifies this conclusion?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Law of Excluded Middle"},{"label":"B","text":"Law of Identity"},{"label":"C","text":"Law of Non-Contradiction"},{"label":"D","text":"Principle of Induction"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The argument relies on the Law of Excluded Middle (P ∨ ¬P) combined with Disjunctive Syllogism. The premise establishes that exactly one of two exhaustive possibilities must be true (Success or Failure). Since 'Failure' is denied, 'Success' must follow. Law of Identity states A=A. Law of Non-Contradiction states not both A and not-A. Principle of Induction is for mathematical proofs."},{"id":1456,"text":"Consider the statement: 'If a number is divisible by 4, then it is divisible by 2.' If we find a number that is divisible by 2 but NOT divisible by 4, what does this tell us about the converse of the original statement?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The converse ('If divisible by 2, then divisible by 4') is true."},{"label":"B","text":"The converse ('If divisible by 2, then divisible by 4') is false."},{"label":"C","text":"The contrapositive is false."},{"label":"D","text":"The original implication was incorrect."}],"answer":1,"solution":"The original statement (P -> Q) is true. The example given (Q is true, P is false) is a counter-example to the converse (Q -> P). Since we found a case where Q is true but P is false, the converse 'If divisible by 2, then divisible by 4' is demonstrably false. The original implication remains valid regardless of the converse's truth value."},{"id":1457,"text":"In the context of formal logic, consider the statement: 'If it rains, the ground gets wet.' If we observe that the ground is dry, what can be logically concluded about the weather?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It must be raining."},{"label":"B","text":"It cannot be raining."},{"label":"C","text":"The ground condition is irrelevant to the rain."},{"label":"D","text":"We cannot determine if it is raining based on this information alone."}],"answer":1,"solution":"By Modus Tollens, if 'If P then Q' is true and Q is false (ground is dry), then P must be false (it is not raining). Option A contradicts the logical deduction. Options Cand D ignore the strict implication where the falsity of the consequent necessitates the falsity of the antecedent in a valid conditional."},{"id":1458,"text":"In a set of statements involving quantifiers, if the statement 'For all integers n, if n is even then n^2 is even' is true, what is the truth value of the statement 'There exists an integer k such that k is odd and k^2 is odd'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"True"},{"label":"B","text":"False"},{"label":"C","text":"Undecidable without knowing specific values"},{"label":"D","text":"It depends on the definition of integers"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The original statement establishes a property for even numbers. The new statement concerns odd numbers. Since the square of an odd number is always odd (a standard mathematical fact), the existence of such a k is true. The first statement does not negate the properties of odd numbers; they are independent domains here. Options B, C, and D are incorrect because the property holds universally for odd integers."},{"id":1459,"text":"Consider the argument: 'Either the team will win or draw. They did not draw. Therefore, the team won.' Which logical law validates this deduction?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Law of Contradiction"},{"label":"B","text":"Law of Excluded Middle combined with Disjunctive Syllogism"},{"label":"C","text":"Modus Ponens"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypothetical Syllogism"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The argument presents a disjunction (A or B) and denies one part (not B), concluding the other (A). This is the structure of Disjunctive Syllogism, which relies on the Law of Excluded Middle (that A or not-A covers all possibilities in this binary context). Option A refers to contradictory statements being mutually exclusive. Option C requires an implication, not a disjunction. Option D links two conditionals."},{"id":1409,"text":"Which statement correctly identifies a necessary condition for an argument to be considered 'sound'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The premises must be true and the reasoning must be valid"},{"label":"B","text":"The conclusion must be true regardless of the premises"},{"label":"C","text":"The premises must be false but lead to a true conclusion"},{"label":"D","text":"The argument must contain no fallacies but can have false premises"}],"answer":0,"solution":"A sound argument requires two things: valid logical structure (reasoning) AND all premises must actually be true. If either condition fails, the argument is not sound. Option B describes an inference that might happen by coincidence, not soundness. Option D describes a valid but unsound argument."},{"id":1448,"text":"Consider the argument: 'All squares are rectangles. Some shapes are not rectangles. Therefore, some shapes are not squares.' Is this syllogism valid?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Yes, it is valid because the conclusion necessarily follows from the premises."},{"label":"B","text":"No, it is invalid because the middle term 'rectangles' is not distributed at least once in the premises."},{"label":"C","text":"No, it is invalid because the conclusion makes an affirmative claim while a premise is negative."},{"label":"D","text":"Yes, it is valid because squares are a subset of rectangles by definition."}],"answer":1,"solution":"The argument is invalid due to the fallacy of the undistributed middle. The term 'rectangles' appears as the predicate in both premises; in standard categorical logic, the predicate of an affirmative proposition (All squares are rectangles) and the predicate of a negative proposition (Some shapes are not rectangles) are not distributed. Since the middle term is never distributed, no valid connection can be established between the subject 'shapes' and the predicate 'squares'. Options A and Din correctly assume validity, while Option Cmisidentifies the specific logical error."},{"id":1451,"text":"In a logical system, if the statement 'Some mammals are not pets' is true, which of the following must necessarily be false?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"All mammals are pets"},{"label":"B","text":"No mammals are pets"},{"label":"C","text":"Some mammals are pets"},{"label":"D","text":"Some non-pets are mammals"}],"answer":0,"solution":"If 'Some mammals are not pets' is true, it means there exists at least one mammal that is not a pet. This directly contradicts the universal affirmative statement 'All mammals are pets', making it necessarily false. 'No mammals are pets' could still be false (since some might be pets). 'Some mammals are pets' is consistent with the premise. 'Some non-pets are mammals' is actually equivalent to the premise, so it would be true."},{"id":1460,"text":"In formal logic, consider the argument: 'All metals are conductors. All conductors are elements.' Which of the following best describes the validity of the conclusion 'Therefore, all metals are elements'?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A. The argument is valid because the premises are true."},{"label":"B","text":"B. The argument is invalid due to the fallacy of undistributed middle."},{"label":"C","text":"C. The argument is invalid because it commits the fallacy of illicit major."},{"label":"D","text":"D. The argument is valid regardless of whether the premises are actually true in reality."}],"answer":1,"solution":"The conclusion 'All metals are elements' does not logically follow from the premises provided, even if both premises were true. The term 'conductors' acts as the middle term but is not distributed (fully referenced) in either premise, making it impossible to establish a valid relationship between 'metals' and 'elements'. This specific structural error is known as the fallacy of the undistributed middle. Options A, C, and D are incorrect because validity depends on logical structure, not truth values or other fallacies."}]},{"name":"Planning-Based Questions","count":25,"questions":[{"id":1413,"text":"Under the Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act, 2006, which of the following categories of rights is NOT recognized by the Act?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Individual forest rights for those who have been residing in and depending on forests since before December 13, 2005"},{"label":"B","text":"Community forest resource rights for traditional forest dwellers"},{"label":"C","text":"Right to generate electricity from small hydro projects within their village limits"},{"label":"D","text":"Right to transfer of land title for non-forest land acquired by the State prior to 2006"}],"answer":3,"solution":"The Act recognizes individual rights (Section 3), community rights (Section 4), and rights for vulnerable sections, but it does not grant a right to generate electricity from small hydro projects as a specific category of forest right. Furthermore, the Act operates within existing land laws and does not create a new right to transfers tate-acquired non-forest land titles in the manner suggested."},{"id":1414,"text":"According to the Forest Rights Act, 2006, which authority is primarily responsible for preparing the 'Record of Rights' (RoR) at the village level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"District Collector"},{"label":"B","text":"Village Council consisting of Gram Sabha members"},{"label":"C","text":"Sub-Divisional Magistrate"},{"label":"D","text":"State Chief Secretary"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The RoR is prepared by the Village Council, which includes representatives from the Gram Sabha, as per the Act's decentralization principles. The District Collector and SDM oversee the process but do not prepare the initial village-level records themselves."},{"id":1415,"text":"In the context of India's National Forest Policy 1988, which of the following was NOT a primary objective?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To ensure optimum utilization of forest resources for economic development"},{"label":"B","text":"To conserve and develop forests as a self-regulating ecosystem"},{"label":"C","text":"To maintain ecological balance and biodiversity conservation"},{"label":"D","text":"To promote the concept of joint forest management with local communities"}],"answer":0,"solution":"While the 1988 policy emphasized conservation, biodiversity, and community participation (JFM), its primary focus was not 'optimum utilization for economic development' in the exploitative sense; rather, it shifted towards conservation and sustainable use. The policy aimed to balance needs but prioritized ecological stability over aggressive economic exploitation."},{"id":1416,"text":"The National Mission for a Green India (NM-GI) is a component of which larger national framework?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC)"},{"label":"B","text":"Swachh Bharat Abhiyan"},{"label":"C","text":"Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana"},{"label":"D","text":"Atal Bhima Yojana"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The National Mission for a Green India (NM-GI) is one of the eight missions under the National Action Plan on Climate Change (NAPCC). The other options are unrelated climate or social welfare schemes."},{"id":1417,"text":"According to the Forest Rights Act, 2006, what is the time limit prescribed for the Gram Sabha to deliberate on the recommendations of the Village Council regarding forest rights?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"30 days"},{"label":"B","text":"90 days"},{"label":"C","text":"180 days"},{"label":"D","text":"One year"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The Act mandates that the Gram Sabha must deliberate on the Village Council's recommendations within 180 days. The 30-day and 90-day periods do not match the statutory requirement for this specific deliberation phase."},{"id":1418,"text":"Which of the following forest types has the highest carbon sequestration potential per hectare annually among the options provided?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Open Forests (Jhum lands)"},{"label":"B","text":"Dense Forests (Tropical Rainforests)"},{"label":"C","text":"Monsoon Deciduous Forests"},{"label":"D","text":"Dry Mixed Forests"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Dense forests, particularly tropical rainforests, possess a higher biomass density and canopy cover compared to open or deciduous forests, leading to superior carbon sequestration rates. Open and dry forests have lower biomass and thus lower carbon capture potential."},{"id":1419,"text":"Under the Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act, 2006, who constitutes the 'Expert Committee' that assists the District Level Committee?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The Gram Sabha members only"},{"label":"B","text":"A team comprising experts from forest department, agriculture, and local administration"},{"label":"C","text":"The State Chief Minister's office staff"},{"label":"D","text":"District Court judges"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The Expert Committee is constituted to provide technical advice and includes experts from relevant departments like forests, agriculture, etc., to assist the District Level Committee. The Gram Sabha deliberates but does not form the expert committee itself."},{"id":1420,"text":"Which of the following is a key indicator used in the National Forest Policy 1988 to assess forest cover?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Area under dense forests as a percentage of total geographical area"},{"label":"B","text":"Total number of trees planted in a year"},{"label":"C","text":"Value of timber produced annually"},{"label":"D","text":"Number of people dependent on forests"}],"answer":0,"solution":"The National Forest Policy 1988 specifically aims to increase the area under 'dense forests' as a percentage of the total geographical area (targeting 33%) as a primary indicator of forest cover health. The other metrics are economic or social indicators, not direct measures of forest cover status."},{"id":1421,"text":"The concept of 'Ecological Security' in India's forest policy is most closely associated with which of the following outcomes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevention of land degradation and soil erosion"},{"label":"B","text":"Increase in urban real estate value"},{"label":"C","text":"Reduction in agricultural output due to deforestation"},{"label":"D","text":"Expansion of industrial zones within forest limits"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Ecological security in Indian forest policy directly relates to maintaining environmental stability, preventing soil erosion, and protecting watersheds. Increasing real estate or reducing agriculture are not goals; expanding industries within forests contradicts the principle of ecological security."},{"id":1422,"text":"In the context of the Forest Rights Act, 2006, what is the role of the State Level Committee?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"To prepare the initial Record of Rights at the village level"},{"label":"B","text":"To adjudicate appeals against orders passed by the District Level Committee"},{"label":"C","text":"To plant trees in urban areas"},{"label":"D","text":"To issue identity cards to forest workers"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The State Level Committee's primary function is to adjudicate appeals against the decisions made by the District Level Committee. The preparation of RoR is done at the village level, and tree planting/ID issuance are operational tasks not specific to this committee's appellate role."},{"id":1423,"text":"Which of the following forest products is typically classified as a 'Non-Timber Forest Product' (NTFP)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Teak logs"},{"label":"B","text":"Sal wood"},{"label":"C","text":"Medicinal herbs like Tulsi or Ashwagandha collected from forests"},{"label":"D","text":"Bamboo poles used for construction"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Medicinal herbs are classic examples of Non-Timber Forest Products (NTFPs). Teak and Sal are timber species, and while bamboo is a grass, in many contexts it is treated as a timber substitute or structural material; however, medicinal herbs are definitively NTFPs without being wood."},{"id":1424,"text":"Under the Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act, 2006, which of the following authorities is primarily responsible for preparing the 'Record of Rights' (RoR) at the village level?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"District Collector"},{"label":"B","text":"Village Council (Gram Sabha)"},{"label":"C","text":"Sub-Divisional Officer"},{"label":"D","text":"Chief Wildlife Warden"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The Village Council (Gram Sabha) is the primary authority mandated to deliberate and prepare the Record of Rights based on recommendations from the Gram Sabha itself, ensuring community participation. The District Collector oversees the process at the district level, the Sub-Divisional Officer acts as a nodal officer for verification, and the Chief Wildlife Warden manages wildlife conservation matters, none of which involve preparing the initial RoR at the village level."},{"id":1425,"text":"Which of the following forest policies explicitly shifted the focus from timber production to conservation and community rights?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Indian Forest Act, 1865"},{"label":"B","text":"National Forest Policy, 1988"},{"label":"C","text":"Forest Conservation Act, 1980"},{"label":"D","text":"Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act, 2006"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The National Forest Policy, 1988 marked a paradigm shift by prioritizing conservation, environmental balance, and community participation over mere timber production. The Indian Forest Act, 1865 was primarily for regulation and revenue generation. The Forest Conservation Act, 1980 deals specifically with diversion of forest land for non-forest purposes. While the FRA, 2006 is crucial for rights, the specific policy shift in focus described is best attributed to the 1988 policy framework which laid the groundwork for modern conservation strategies."},{"id":1426,"text":"According to the National Forest Policy, 1988, what was the target percentage of forest cover recommended for India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"20%"},{"label":"B","text":"33%"},{"label":"C","text":"67%"},{"label":"D","text":"50%"}],"answer":2,"solution":"The National Forest Policy, 1988 recommended that India should maintain a forest cover of 33% (or one-third) of its geographical area to ensure ecological stability. This was a significant increase from previous targets. The 20% figure was often associated with earlier colonial or early post-independence goals before the policy shift. The 50% target is not part of the 1988 policy recommendations, though it is sometimes discussed as an ideal goal for specific regions like the Western Ghats."},{"id":1428,"text":"Which of the following actions by a State Government requires prior approval from the Central Government under the Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Afforestation in barren lands"},{"label":"B","text":"Diversion of forest land for non-forest purposes like mining or industry"},{"label":"C","text":"Plantation of native species in village commons"},{"label":"D","text":"Construction of rural roads within existing forest boundaries"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980 mandates prior approval from the Central Government for any diversion of forest land for non-forest purposes such as mining, industry, or infrastructure projects. Afforestation and plantation activities generally promote conservation and do not require this specific approval if they align with state forestry plans. Construction of rural roads is a sensitive issue but the broad category of 'diversion' covers the major industrial shifts requiring central clearance."},{"id":1429,"text":"In the context of the National Mission for a Green India (NM-GI), which of the following is NOT a primary objective?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Carbon Sequestration and Storage"},{"label":"B","text":"Conservation and Restoration of Forest/Landscapes"},{"label":"C","text":"Enhancing Livelihood Security"},{"label":"D","text":"Conversion of all wastelands into commercial pine plantations"}],"answer":3,"solution":"The National Mission for a Green India (NM-GI), part of the National Action Plan on Climate Change, aims at carbon sequestration, conservation/restoration, and livelihood security. It does not mandate the conversion of all wastelands into commercial pine plantations; in fact, promoting monoculture like extensive pine plantations is often discouraged to maintain biodiversity. The other three options are core pillars of the mission's strategy."},{"id":1430,"text":"Which of the following forest types is most critical for preventing soil erosion in hilly regions of India?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mangroves along coastal areas"},{"label":"B","text":"Alpine meadows at high altitudes"},{"label":"C","text":"Tropical deciduous forests on steep slopes"},{"label":"D","text":"Temperate coniferous forests in the Himalayas"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Tropical deciduous forests on steep slopes play a vital role in preventing soil erosion in hilly regions due to their extensive root systems that hold the soil together. Mangroves prevent coastal erosion, not hillside erosion. Alpine meadows are fragile and specific to high altitudes, while temperate forests are geographically limited to the Himalayas. The question asks specifically about general hilly regions where tropical deciduous forests are widespread and effective."},{"id":1431,"text":"The concept of 'Community Forestry' in India is most closely associated with which of the following outcomes?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Centralized management by the Forest Department"},{"label":"B","text":"Exclusive ownership of forest produce by local industries"},{"label":"C","text":"Participatory management and benefit sharing by local communities"},{"label":"D","text":"Complete prohibition of any human entry into forest areas"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Community Forestry emphasizes participatory management where local communities have rights and responsibilities in managing forest resources, leading to benefit sharing. Centralized management is the traditional model, not community forestry. Exclusive ownership by industries contradicts the principle of community involvement. Complete prohibition of entry is contrary to the FRA which grants rights for access and use."},{"id":1432,"text":"Under the Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act, 2006, which category of rights includes the right to collect minor forest produce?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Individual Forest Right"},{"label":"B","text":"Community Forest Right"},{"label":"C","text":"Restriction on Entry Right"},{"label":"D","text":"Residential Land Right"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The Community Forest Right under the FRA, 2006 specifically grants rights to manage and protect community lands and includes the right to collect minor forest produce (MFP) from those areas. Individual Forest Rights are granted for residential land or specific cultivation/collecting rights on individual plots but the broad MFP rights often fall under community management in traditional contexts. Restriction on Entry is a prohibition, not a right. Residential Land Right is for habitation."},{"id":1433,"text":"Which of the following bodies is responsible for adjudicating disputes related to 'Community Forest Rights' under the FRA, 2006?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gram Sabha"},{"label":"B","text":"Village Council"},{"label":"C","text":"District Level Committee"},{"label":"D","text":"State Level Committee"}],"answer":3,"solution":"The State Level Committee is the authority designated to adjudicate disputes regarding Community Forest Rights under the FRA, 2006. The District Level Committee handles individual rights and initial community recommendations but refers complex community disputes upwards. The Gram Sabha and Village Council are lower-level bodies responsible for identification and recommendation, not final adjudication of disputes."},{"id":1434,"text":"According to the National Forest Policy of India, which of the following forest types is prioritized for afforestation in degraded areas to ensure ecological security?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Monoculture plantations of fast-growing species like Eucalyptus"},{"label":"B","text":"Mixed-species natural regeneration communities"},{"label":"C","text":"Clear-cutting followed by immediate monoculture planting"},{"label":"D","text":"Removal of all undergrowth to maximize sunlight penetration"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The National Forest Policy emphasizes mixed-species natural regeneration to maintain biodiversity and ecological balance, making 'Mixed-species natural regeneration communities' the correct choice. Monocultures (Option A) reduce biodiversity; clear-cutting (Option C) causes degradation; and removing all undergrowth (Option D) disrupts soil health and micro-habitats."},{"id":1435,"text":"Under the Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act, 2006, which body is constitutionally mandated to constitute the District Level Committee (DLC)?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The State Government alone"},{"label":"B","text":"The District Collector in consultation with the Chief Minister"},{"label":"C","text":"The Village Council exclusively"},{"label":"D","text":"The Central Ministry of Environment and Forests"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Section 5(3) of the Act mandates that the District Level Committee be constituted by the State Government, typically involving the District Collector, making Option B correct. The DLC is as tate-level body, not formed solely by the Village Council (Option C) or the Central Ministry (Option D), and while the State Government initiates it, the specific composition involves consultation with the CM, distinguishing it from an action taken 'alone' in isolation without defined structure (Option A)."},{"id":1436,"text":"In the context of India's National Forest Policy 1988, which of the following forest cover percentage was identified as the critical threshold to ensure ecological security? A) 25% B) 33% C) 40% D) 50%","options":[{"label":"A","text":"25%"},{"label":"B","text":"33%"},{"label":"C","text":"40%"},{"label":"D","text":"50%"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The National Forest Policy 1988 explicitly set a target of 33% forest cover for the country to maintain ecological security, noting that this figure was already achieved in hilly and mountainous areas but fell short in plains. Option A (25%) represents the lower limit often associated with degraded lands or earlier policies before 1988. Option C (40%) and D (50%) exceed the specific target set by the 1988 policy, which acknowledged that achieving such higher percentages was not immediately feasible for all regions at that time."},{"id":1437,"text":"Which of the following forest types is recognized in India's National Forest Policy 2009 as having the highest carbon sequestration potential per hectare annually?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A. Teak plantations"},{"label":"B","text":"B. Sal forests"},{"label":"C","text":"C. Tropical Moist Deciduous forests"},{"label":"D","text":"D. Mangroves"}],"answer":3,"solution":"Mangroves are widely recognized globally and in India's National Forest Policy 2009 for having the highest carbon sequestration potential per hectare due to their dense biomass accumulation and soil carbon storage capabilities. Teak plantations, Sal forests, and Tropical Moist Deciduous forests, while ecologically significant, generally store less carbon per unit area compared to mangrove ecosystems."},{"id":1427,"text":"Under the Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act, 2006, which body has the final authority to adjudicate disputes regarding individual forest rights?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gram Sabha"},{"label":"B","text":"Village Council"},{"label":"C","text":"District Level Committee"},{"label":"D","text":"Sub-Divisional Officer"}],"answer":1,"solution":"The Village Council (Sarpanch) is the primary authority that examines and recommends claims for individual forest rights under the FRA, 2006. The Gram Sabha deliberates on these recommendations but does not have final adjudication power over disputes. The District Level Committee reviews village-level decisions but typically refers disputes to higher authorities if they are contested. The Sub-Divisional Officer is an administrative functionary, not a statutory adjudicatory body under the Act for this specific role."}]},{"name":"Quantitative Aptitude","count":8,"questions":[{"id":1175,"text":"Hooke's law relates stress and:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Force"},{"label":"B","text":"Strain"},{"label":"C","text":"Area"},{"label":"D","text":"Volume"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Within elastic limit, stress ∝ strain (Hooke's law)."},{"id":1240,"text":"Hooke's law relates stress and:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Force"},{"label":"B","text":"Strain"},{"label":"C","text":"Area"},{"label":"D","text":"Volume"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Within elastic limit, stress  strain (Hooke's law)."},{"id":1345,"text":"Hooke's law relates stress and:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Force"},{"label":"B","text":"Strain"},{"label":"C","text":"Area"},{"label":"D","text":"Volume"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Within elastic limit, stress  strain (Hooke's law)."},{"id":1511,"text":"One mole of an ideal monoatomic gas undergoes a reversible adiabatic expansion such that its volume becomes 8 times the initial volume. The ratio of final temperature to initial temperature (T₂/T₁) is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1/2"},{"label":"B","text":"1/4"},{"label":"C","text":"1/8"},{"label":"D","text":"1/16"}],"answer":1,"solution":"Shortcut: For a reversible adiabatic process, TV^(γ-1) = constant. For a monoatomic gas, γ = 5/3, so γ-1 = 2/3. Since V₂ = 8V₁, (V₂/V₁)^(2/3) = 8^(2/3) = (2³)^(2/3) = 2² = 4. Thus T₁/T₂ = 4, giving T₂/T₁ = 1/4. Full Solution: Using the adiabatic relation T₁V₁^(γ-1) = T₂V₂^(γ-1). Substitute γ = Cp/Cv = 5/3 for monoatomic gas. T₂/T₁ = (V₁/V₂)^(γ-1) = (1/8)^(2/3) = 1/4."},{"id":1534,"text":"The molar mass of gas A is 4 g/mol and that of gas B is 64 g/mol. Both gases are maintained at the same temperature. The ratio of the root mean square speed of gas A to that of gas B is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1:4"},{"label":"B","text":"2:1"},{"label":"C","text":"4:1"},{"label":"D","text":"1:2"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: RMS speed is inversely proportional to the square root of molar mass (v_rms ∝ 1/√M). Ratio v_A:v_B = √(M_B/M_A) = √(64/4) = √16 = 4. Hence, ratio is 4:1. Full Solution: The formula for RMS speed is v_rms = √(3RT/M). Since Rand T are constant for both gases, v_A/v_B = √(M_B/M_A). Substituting M_A = 4 g/mol and M_B = 64 g/mol gives v_A/v_B = √(64/4) = √16 = 4. Therefore, the required ratio is 4:1."},{"id":1547,"text":"According to the ideal gas law, at constant volume, pressure is directly proportional to temperature (in Kelvin). If a sealed container initially at 300 Khas pressure 1.0 atm, what is the pressure when the temperature is raised to 450 K? Use the shortcut ratio P2/P1 = T2/T1.","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1.5 atm"},{"label":"B","text":"1.33 atm"},{"label":"C","text":"2.0 atm"},{"label":"D","text":"0.75 atm"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: P2 = P1·(T2/T1) = 1.0·(450/300) = 1·1.5 = 1.5 atm. Full steps: Convert temperatures to Kelvin (already given). Ratio = 450/300 = 1.5. Multiply by initial pressure 1.0 atm gives 1.5 atm. Option A is correct."},{"id":1561,"text":"A projectile is fired with an initial speed u at an angle θ with the horizontal. Its range is R. For the same initial speed u, there is another angle of projection that yields the same range R. If the ratio of the maximum heights attained in the two cases is 1 : 4, the value of tan θ is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"1/2"},{"label":"B","text":"1/√2"},{"label":"C","text":"1/3"},{"label":"D","text":"1/√3"}],"answer":0,"solution":"Shortcut: For complementary angles θ and (90°−θ) giving the same range, the ratio of maximum heights H₁/H₂ = tan²θ. Given H₁/H₂ = 1/4, we get tan²θ = 1/4 ⇒ tan θ = 1/2. Full Solution: Range R = u²sin2θ/g. Same range occurs for θ and 90°−θ. H₁ = u²sin²θ/2g, H₂ = u²cos²θ/2g. Ratio H₁/H₂ = tan²θ = 1/4 ⇒ tan θ = 1/2."},{"id":1579,"text":"In a photoelectric effect experiment, the stopping potential V_s is plotted against the frequency f of incident light. The slope of the straight line obtained gives:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Work function (φ)"},{"label":"B","text":"Planck's constant (h)"},{"label":"C","text":"Ratio h/e"},{"label":"D","text":"Ratio e/h"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Einstein's equation eV_s = hf - φ ⇒ V_s = (h/e)f - φ/e. This is a straight line y = mx + c where slope m = h/e. Full Solution: The graph of stopping potential vs frequency is linear. Comparing V_s = (h/e)f - φ/e with y = mx + c, the slope is h/e. The intercept on the frequency axis gives threshold frequency f0 = φ/h, and the intercept on the V_s axis gives -φ/e."}]},{"name":"General Intelligence & Reasoning","count":1,"questions":[{"id":1472,"text":"If A is a square matrix of order 3 such that |A| = 2, then the value of |adj(adj A)| is:","options":[{"label":"A","text":"4"},{"label":"B","text":"8"},{"label":"C","text":"16"},{"label":"D","text":"32"}],"answer":2,"solution":"Shortcut: Use direct formula |adj(adj A)| = |A|⁽ⁿ⁻¹⁾² for order n. Here n=3, |A|=2 ⇒ 2⁽²⁾² = 2⁴ = 16. Step-by-step: |adj A| = |A|ⁿ⁻¹ = 2² = 4. Then |adj(adj A)| = |adj A|ⁿ⁻¹ = 4² = 16."}]}]};